CC.provideCourse.bind(null,"net")({"lessons":{"net-01":{"html":"<p>A <strong>computer network</strong> is two or more devices connected together so they can share data and resources (such as files, printers, or an internet connection). Every time you send a message or load a web page, your device is talking across a network.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-what-makes-a-network-work\"><h2 id=\"what-makes-a-network-work\">What makes a network work?</h2>\n<p>Every network needs four basic pieces:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>End devices (hosts)</strong>: computers, phones, or servers that send and receive data.</li><li><strong>Intermediary devices</strong>: switches and routers that forward data along the right path.</li><li><strong>Transmission media</strong>: copper cables, fiber-optic cables, or radio waves (Wi-Fi) that carry signals.</li><li><strong>Protocols</strong>: agreed-upon rules (such as <strong>IP</strong>, <strong>TCP</strong>, and <strong>HTTP</strong>) so both sides speak the same language.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-client-server-vs-peer-to-peer\"><h2 id=\"client-server-vs-peer-to-peer\">Client-server vs. peer-to-peer</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>How it works</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Client-server</strong></td><td>Dedicated <strong>servers</strong> store data or run services; <strong>clients</strong> request them</td><td>Web browser (client) loading a page from a web server</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Peer-to-peer (P2P)</strong></td><td>Every device acts as both client and server; no central server is required</td><td>Two laptops sharing files directly over Wi-Fi</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Client-server networks are easier to secure and back up centrally. Peer-to-peer networks are cheap and quick to set up for a few devices, but harder to manage as they grow.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-a-brief-history-of-the-internet\"><h2 id=\"a-brief-history-of-the-internet\">A brief history of the internet</h2>\n<p>The <strong>internet</strong> is a global \"network of networks\" connecting millions of private, academic, business, and government networks.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"timeline\"><pre hidden class=\"src\">1969 | ARPANET | The US Department of Defense connects four university computers. It introduces packet switching, splitting messages into small chunks instead of holding one phone line open. | 1\n1983 | TCP/IP becomes standard | ARPANET adopts TCP/IP as its core protocol suite, letting different kinds of networks talk to each other. This is often called the birth of the modern internet. | 1\n1989 | World Wide Web invented | Tim Berners-Lee at CERN proposes hypertext documents (HTML), URLs, and HTTP—making the internet easy for non-specialists to browse. | 1\n1990s–present | Commercial &amp; mobile era | ISPs open the internet to the public; broadband, Wi-Fi, cloud computing, and smartphones connect billions of devices worldwide. | 1</pre></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Look at three actions you did online today (for example: watching a video, checking email, or messaging a friend). For each one, identify:</p>\n<ol><li>What is the <strong>client</strong>?</li><li>What is the <strong>server</strong>?</li><li>What <strong>medium</strong> carried the first hop from your device (Wi-Fi, cellular, or Ethernet cable)?</li></ol>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show sample answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><ul><li><strong>Watching a video</strong>: Client = your phone's video app; Server = the video platform's streaming server; First hop = Wi-Fi or 4G/5G cellular.</li><li><strong>Checking email</strong>: Client = your browser or mail app; Server = the university/provider mail server; First hop = Ethernet cable or Wi-Fi.</li></ul></div></details>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Confusing the <strong>internet</strong> (the global network infrastructure of cables, routers, and IP) with the <strong>World Wide Web</strong> (websites and HTTP running on top of the internet).</li><li>Thinking a <strong>protocol</strong> is hardware. A protocol is a set of rules (software/standard), not a cable or box.</li><li>Assuming peer-to-peer means \"no internet.\" P2P devices can be across the internet or on the same local Wi-Fi.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>computer network</strong></td><td>two or more devices connected to share data and resources</td><td lang=\"tr\">bilgisayar ağı</td></tr><tr><td><strong>protocol</strong></td><td>a standard set of rules that governs how data is formatted and sent</td><td lang=\"tr\">protokol</td></tr><tr><td><strong>client</strong></td><td>a device or program that requests a service or data</td><td lang=\"tr\">istemci</td></tr><tr><td><strong>server</strong></td><td>a device or program that provides data or services to clients</td><td lang=\"tr\">sunucu</td></tr><tr><td><strong>packet</strong></td><td>a small formatted chunk of data sent across a network</td><td lang=\"tr\">paket</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"What is the main difference between the internet and the World Wide Web?","opts":[{"h":"They are two names for the exact same system","why":"the Web is only one service running on top of the internet"},{"h":"The internet is the global network of connected networks, while the Web is a system of websites and pages running on it","ok":1,"why":"correct: email, SSH, and file transfers use the internet without being the Web"},{"h":"The Web is hardware cables and the internet is only browsers","why":"reversed: cables and routers belong to the internet infrastructure"},{"h":"The Web came first in 1969 and the internet was added in 1989","why":"reversed: ARPANET (1969) came 20 years before the Web (1989)"}],"why":"The internet is the underlying network infrastructure; the World Wide Web (HTTP/HTML) is one application running over it."},{"q":"Which statement best describes a <strong>client-server</strong> network?","opts":[{"h":"Every computer has equal responsibility and there is no central server","why":"that describes peer-to-peer (P2P)"},{"h":"Clients request resources and dedicated servers respond with those resources","ok":1,"why":"centralized servers make security and backups easier"},{"h":"Devices can only connect using copper cables","why":"client-server works over any medium, including Wi-Fi and fiber"},{"h":"Only two devices can communicate at a time","why":"servers handle requests from many clients"}],"why":"In a client-server model, specialized servers host data or services for many clients."},{"q":"Why was <strong>packet switching</strong> (used in ARPANET) important?","opts":[{"h":"It required a dedicated physical wire between every pair of users","why":"that was circuit switching, not packet switching"},{"h":"It encrypted all web pages with HTTPS in 1969","why":"HTTPS was invented much later after the Web"},{"h":"It broke messages into small packets that could share links and route around failures","ok":1,"why":"multiple conversations could share the same cables efficiently"},{"h":"It allowed only one computer to send data per hour","why":"packet switching improved sharing and throughput"}],"why":"Packet switching splits data into small packets so links are shared efficiently and traffic can reroute if a link fails."}]],"id":"net-01","title":"Network basics and internet history","goal":"explain what a computer network is, compare client-server and peer-to-peer models, and trace the key milestones of the internet","week":"1","minutes":15,"terms":[{"t":"computer network","m":"two or more devices connected to share data and resources","tr":"bilgisayar ağı"},{"t":"protocol","m":"a standard set of rules that governs how data is formatted and sent","tr":"protokol"},{"t":"client","m":"a device or program that requests a service or data","tr":"istemci"},{"t":"server","m":"a device or program that provides data or services to clients","tr":"sunucu"},{"t":"packet","m":"a small formatted chunk of data sent across a network","tr":"paket"}]},"net-02":{"html":"<p>Networks are described in two main ways: <strong>how big an area they cover</strong> (network type) and <strong>how devices are arranged and connected</strong> (topology).</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-network-types-by-size\"><h2 id=\"network-types-by-size\">Network types by size</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Full name</th><th>Typical range</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>PAN</strong></td><td>Personal Area Network</td><td>A few meters (1–10 m)</td><td>Bluetooth headphones paired to a phone</td></tr><tr><td><strong>LAN</strong></td><td>Local Area Network</td><td>One room, building, or campus</td><td>University computer lab or home Wi-Fi</td></tr><tr><td><strong>MAN</strong></td><td>Metropolitan Area Network</td><td>A city</td><td>Fiber ring linking municipal offices across a city</td></tr><tr><td><strong>WAN</strong></td><td>Wide Area Network</td><td>Across countries or globally</td><td>The internet, or bank branches across cities</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-physical-vs-logical-topology\"><h2 id=\"physical-vs-logical-topology\">Physical vs. logical topology</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Physical topology</strong>: how the cables and devices are actually laid out on desks, walls, and racks.</li><li><strong>Logical topology</strong>: the path data actually takes as it travels from device to device.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-network-topologies\"><h2 id=\"common-network-topologies\">Common network topologies</h2>\n<h3>Bus topology</h3>\n<p>All devices share a single backbone cable with terminators at both ends. Cheap to wire, but if the main cable breaks, the whole network goes down.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"topology\" data-type=\"bus\"></div>\n<h3>Star topology</h3>\n<p>Every device connects to a central switch (or hub). If one cable fails, only that one device loses connection. This is the standard layout for modern Ethernet LANs.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"topology\" data-type=\"star\"></div>\n<h3>Ring topology</h3>\n<p>Each device connects to two neighbors, forming a closed loop. Data travels in one direction (or both in a dual ring). A single break can disrupt a simple ring.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"topology\" data-type=\"ring\"></div>\n<h3>Mesh topology</h3>\n<p>Devices have multiple redundant links to each other. A <strong>full mesh</strong> connects every node to every other node, giving high fault tolerance at high cable cost—common in core WAN routers.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"topology\" data-type=\"mesh\"></div>\n<h3>Tree and hybrid topologies</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Tree (hierarchical)</strong>: a root switch connects to branch switches, forming levels—common in multi-floor campus buildings.</li><li><strong>Hybrid</strong>: combines two or more topologies (for example, star-wired floors linked by a mesh core).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"topology\" data-type=\"tree\"></div>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"topology\" data-type=\"hybrid\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>A university has 4 floors in its Engineering building. Each floor has 30 PCs wired to a floor switch, and the 4 floor switches connect to one core switch in the server room.</p>\n<ol><li>Is this network a PAN, LAN, MAN, or WAN?</li><li>Which topology does a single floor use?</li><li>Which topology describes the whole building?</li></ol>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><ol><li><strong>LAN</strong> (one building on a campus).</li><li><strong>Star</strong> topology (30 PCs connected to one central floor switch).</li><li><strong>Tree</strong> (hierarchical) topology—multiple stars connected to a core switch.</li></ol></div></details>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Calling a home network a <strong>WAN</strong> just because it connects to the internet. Inside your home, it is a <strong>LAN</strong>; your ISP connects your LAN to the <strong>WAN</strong>.</li><li>Confusing <strong>bus</strong> and <strong>star</strong>: in a star topology, a broken PC cable affects only that PC, whereas in a bus topology a broken backbone stops everyone.</li><li>Thinking <strong>mesh</strong> is used for desktop PCs in a lab. Full mesh is too expensive for PCs; it is used between core routers or wireless mesh nodes.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>LAN (Local Area Network)</strong></td><td>a network covering a small area such as a home, office, or campus building</td><td lang=\"tr\">yerel alan ağı</td></tr><tr><td><strong>WAN (Wide Area Network)</strong></td><td>a network spanning cities, countries, or continents</td><td lang=\"tr\">geniş alan ağı</td></tr><tr><td><strong>topology</strong></td><td>the physical or logical layout of devices and links in a network</td><td lang=\"tr\">topoloji</td></tr><tr><td><strong>star topology</strong></td><td>a layout where every host connects to a central switch or hub</td><td lang=\"tr\">yıldız topoloji</td></tr><tr><td><strong>mesh topology</strong></td><td>a layout with redundant links between devices for fault tolerance</td><td lang=\"tr\">örgü topoloji</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"Which network type covers a single university computer lab?","opts":[{"h":"PAN","why":"PAN covers only a few meters around one person (like Bluetooth)"},{"h":"LAN","ok":1,"why":"a Local Area Network covers a room, building, or campus"},{"h":"MAN","why":"a Metropolitan Area Network spans a whole city"},{"h":"WAN","why":"a Wide Area Network spans large geographic distances across cities or countries"}],"why":"A single room or building network is a LAN."},{"q":"In which topology does a single broken backbone cable bring down the entire network?","opts":[{"h":"Star","why":"a broken drop cable in a star only disconnects that one device"},{"h":"Bus","ok":1,"why":"every device shares the single backbone cable"},{"h":"Full mesh","why":"mesh has redundant alternate paths"},{"h":"Hybrid","why":"hybrid combines topologies and usually isolates failures"}],"why":"In a bus topology, all hosts share one main cable, so a break or missing terminator stops communication."},{"q":"Why do modern office and campus LANs use <strong>star</strong> (and tree) topologies instead of bus?","opts":[{"h":"Star uses less cable than any other topology","why":"star actually uses more cable than bus because each host has its own run"},{"h":"Star requires no central device","why":"star requires a central switch or hub"},{"h":"A single cable fault only affects one host, making troubleshooting easy","ok":1,"why":"isolating faults to one port/cable is a major advantage"},{"h":"Star only works with wireless devices","why":"Ethernet star networks use copper or fiber cables"}],"why":"Star topologies isolate single cable failures and make adding or removing hosts simple."}]],"id":"net-02","title":"Network topologies and types","goal":"classify networks by geographic size (PAN, LAN, MAN, WAN) and compare bus, star, ring, mesh, tree, and hybrid topologies","week":"2","minutes":18,"terms":[{"t":"LAN (Local Area Network)","m":"a network covering a small area such as a home, office, or campus building","tr":"yerel alan ağı"},{"t":"WAN (Wide Area Network)","m":"a network spanning cities, countries, or continents","tr":"geniş alan ağı"},{"t":"topology","m":"the physical or logical layout of devices and links in a network","tr":"topoloji"},{"t":"star topology","m":"a layout where every host connects to a central switch or hub","tr":"yıldız topoloji"},{"t":"mesh topology","m":"a layout with redundant links between devices for fault tolerance","tr":"örgü topoloji"}]},"net-03":{"html":"<p>Different network devices operate at different levels of intelligence. Understanding what each box reads—signals, MAC addresses, or IP addresses—tells you where to place it in a network.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-core-network-devices\"><h2 id=\"core-network-devices\">Core network devices</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Main job</th><th>Address used</th><th>Layer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>NIC</strong></td><td>Connects a host (PC/server) to the network medium</td><td>MAC address</td><td>Layer 1–2</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Repeater / Hub</strong></td><td>Boosts and repeats electrical signals to <strong>all</strong> other ports</td><td>None (raw bits)</td><td>Layer 1</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Switch</strong></td><td>Learns MAC addresses and forwards frames <strong>only</strong> to the target port</td><td>MAC address</td><td>Layer 2</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Access Point (AP)</strong></td><td>Bridges wireless Wi-Fi devices onto a wired Ethernet LAN</td><td>MAC address</td><td>Layer 2</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Router</strong></td><td>Connects different networks (LANs) and chooses best paths</td><td>IP address</td><td>Layer 3</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Modem</strong></td><td>Converts ISP line signals (DSL/cable/fiber) to digital data</td><td>—</td><td>Layer 1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-hub-vs-switch-why-switches-won\"><h2 id=\"hub-vs-switch-why-switches-won\">Hub vs. switch: why switches won</h2>\n<p>Suppose PC-A wants to send data to PC-C, and 4 PCs are plugged into the same box:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Hub (Layer 1)</strong>: copies the signal out of <strong>every</strong> other port. PC-B and PC-D also hear it, wasting bandwidth and risking collisions (one shared <strong>collision domain</strong>).</li><li><strong>Switch (Layer 2)</strong>: reads the destination <strong>MAC address</strong>, checks its <strong>MAC address table</strong>, and sends the frame <strong>only</strong> to PC-C's port. Each port is its own collision domain.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-switch-vs-router\"><h2 id=\"switch-vs-router\">Switch vs. router</h2>\n<ul><li>A <strong>switch</strong> connects devices <strong>inside the same network (LAN)</strong> using MAC addresses. It forwards broadcasts to all ports in that LAN.</li><li>A <strong>router</strong> connects <strong>different networks</strong> (for example, your home LAN to your ISP's WAN, or Lab 1 to Lab 2) using <strong>IP addresses</strong>. Routers stop LAN broadcasts from flooding the next network—each router interface marks a separate <strong>broadcast domain</strong>.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout callout-tip\"><div class=\"callout-h\">Home \"Wi-Fi router\"</div><p>The small box at home is actually 3–4 devices in one plastic case: a <strong>modem</strong> (or ONT), a <strong>router</strong>, a small 4-port <strong>switch</strong>, and a wireless <strong>access point</strong>.</p></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Match each network device to the layer and address type it uses:</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"match\" data-set=\"devlayer\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Saying a <strong>switch</strong> routes IP packets between different networks. A standard Layer 2 switch only forwards frames inside one LAN using MAC addresses; a <strong>router</strong> connects different networks.</li><li>Thinking a <strong>hub</strong> and a <strong>switch</strong> do the same thing because they look similar. A hub broadcasts every bit to all ports; a switch forwards selectively.</li><li>Confusing a <strong>modem</strong> with a <strong>router</strong>. A modem translates line signals from the ISP; a router directs IP packets between networks.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>NIC (Network Interface Card)</strong></td><td>hardware component that connects a computer to a network</td><td lang=\"tr\">ağ bağdaştırıcısı (NIC)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>hub</strong></td><td>a simple Layer 1 device that repeats incoming signals to all ports</td><td lang=\"tr\">göbek (hub)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>switch</strong></td><td>a Layer 2 device that forwards frames based on MAC addresses</td><td lang=\"tr\">anahtar (switch)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>router</strong></td><td>a Layer 3 device that forwards packets between different networks using IP addresses</td><td lang=\"tr\">yönlendirici (router)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>MAC address</strong></td><td>a 48-bit physical hardware address burned into a NIC</td><td lang=\"tr\">MAC adresi</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"Which device reads destination <strong>MAC addresses</strong> to send a frame only to the intended port?","opts":[{"h":"Hub","why":"a hub has no knowledge of addresses and repeats bits to every port"},{"h":"Switch","ok":1,"why":"a switch builds a MAC address table and forwards frames to the matching port"},{"h":"Repeater","why":"a repeater only regenerates signals at Layer 1"},{"h":"Modem","why":"a modem converts between ISP line signals and digital signals"}],"why":"Switches operate at Layer 2 and forward frames using MAC addresses."},{"q":"You need to connect two separate LANs (<code>192.168.1.0/24</code> and <code>192.168.2.0/24</code>). Which device do you need?","opts":[{"h":"Hub","why":"a hub only repeats Layer 1 signals inside one segment"},{"h":"Standard Layer 2 switch","why":"a Layer 2 switch keeps all ports in the same broadcast domain and does not route between IP subnets"},{"h":"Router","ok":1,"why":"a router operates at Layer 3 and forwards IP packets between different networks"},{"h":"Repeater","why":"a repeater only extends cable distance"}],"why":"Routers connect distinct IP networks and route packets between them."},{"q":"What is the main job of a wireless <strong>Access Point (AP)</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"To assign public IP addresses to websites","why":"DNS/ISPs handle public naming and routing"},{"h":"To connect Wi-Fi clients to a wired Ethernet LAN","ok":1,"why":"an AP bridges wireless frames onto the wired switch network"},{"h":"To amplify DSL telephone signals","why":"that is a DSL modem's job"},{"h":"To replace all routers on the internet","why":"an AP operates at Layer 2 inside a LAN"}],"why":"An access point lets wireless devices join a wired local network."}]],"id":"net-03","title":"Network devices","goal":"distinguish the roles of NICs, hubs, switches, routers, access points, and modems, and match each device to its OSI layer","week":"3","minutes":18,"terms":[{"t":"NIC (Network Interface Card)","m":"hardware component that connects a computer to a network","tr":"ağ bağdaştırıcısı (NIC)"},{"t":"hub","m":"a simple Layer 1 device that repeats incoming signals to all ports","tr":"göbek (hub)"},{"t":"switch","m":"a Layer 2 device that forwards frames based on MAC addresses","tr":"anahtar (switch)"},{"t":"router","m":"a Layer 3 device that forwards packets between different networks using IP addresses","tr":"yönlendirici (router)"},{"t":"MAC address","m":"a 48-bit physical hardware address burned into a NIC","tr":"MAC adresi"}]},"net-04":{"html":"<p>Data travels across a network as electrical pulses (copper), light pulses (fiber), or radio waves (wireless). Choosing the right medium depends on distance, speed, cost, and electrical noise (<strong>EMI</strong>).</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-guided-wired-media\"><h2 id=\"guided-wired-media\">Guided (wired) media</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Medium</th><th>Signal</th><th>Typical max run</th><th>Pros &amp; cons</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>UTP (Twisted Pair)</strong></td><td>Electrical</td><td>100 m</td><td>Cheap, flexible, standard for LANs (<code>Cat5e</code>, <code>Cat6</code>); vulnerable to EMI</td></tr><tr><td><strong>STP (Shielded TP)</strong></td><td>Electrical</td><td>100 m</td><td>Extra foil shield reduces EMI; stiffer and pricier than UTP</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Coaxial</strong></td><td>Electrical</td><td>~500 m</td><td>Good shielding; used by cable TV/internet ISPs</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fiber-optic</strong></td><td>Light</td><td>2 km (MMF) to 40+ km (SMF)</td><td>Immune to EMI, very high speed, long distance; costlier connectors</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Why are UTP wires twisted?</h3>\n<p>Twisting pairs of copper wires cancels out <strong>crosstalk</strong> (interference between neighboring wires) and external electromagnetic interference (<strong>EMI</strong>). <strong>Cat5e</strong> and <strong>Cat6</strong> are common Ethernet categories supporting 1 Gbps (and 10 Gbps over short Cat6 runs).</p>\n<h3>Single-mode vs. multi-mode fiber</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Single-mode fiber (SMF)</strong>: narrow core, laser light, travels tens of kilometers (used between cities and campus buildings).</li><li><strong>Multi-mode fiber (MMF)</strong>: wider core, LED light, shorter runs up to ~550 m–2 km (used inside buildings and data centers).</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-rj-45-t568a-t568b-and-cable-types\"><h2 id=\"rj-45-t568a-t568b-and-cable-types\">RJ-45, T568A/T568B, and cable types</h2>\n<p>Ethernet UTP cables use <strong>8-pin RJ-45</strong> connectors. Two wiring standards exist: <strong>T568A</strong> and <strong>T568B</strong> (T568B is most common).</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Straight-through cable</strong>: both ends use the <strong>same</strong> standard (e.g., T568B on both ends). Used to connect <strong>unlike devices</strong>: - PC ↔ Switch - Switch ↔ Router</li><li><strong>Crossover cable</strong>: one end is <strong>T568A</strong> and the other is <strong>T568B</strong> (transmit pins connect to receive pins). Used to connect <strong>like devices</strong>: - PC ↔ PC - Switch ↔ Switch - Router ↔ Router</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout callout-tip\"><div class=\"callout-h\">Auto-MDIX on modern hardware</div><p>Most modern switches and NICs support <strong>Auto-MDIX</strong>, which automatically detects and flips pins so a straight-through cable works everywhere. In <strong>Cisco Packet Tracer</strong> and exams, still know the straight-through vs. crossover rule!</p></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-unguided-wireless-media\"><h2 id=\"unguided-wireless-media\">Unguided (wireless) media</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Wi-Fi (802.11)</strong>: radio waves in 2.4 GHz / 5 GHz bands for LANs.</li><li><strong>Bluetooth</strong>: short-range PANs (~10 m).</li><li><strong>Cellular (4G/5G)</strong> and <strong>Satellite</strong>: wide-area wireless links.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Decide whether you should choose <strong>straight-through</strong>, <strong>crossover</strong>, or <strong>fiber-optic</strong> for each link (assuming no Auto-MDIX):</p>\n<ol><li>A lab PC to a classroom switch (15 m).</li><li>Two switches in the same rack.</li><li>Building A switch to Building B switch (400 m apart, outdoors).</li></ol>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><ol><li><strong>Straight-through</strong> UTP (unlike devices: PC ↔ Switch, under 100 m).</li><li><strong>Crossover</strong> UTP (like devices: Switch ↔ Switch).</li><li><strong>Fiber-optic</strong> (400 m exceeds the 100 m copper UTP limit, and fiber is immune to lightning/EMI outdoors).</li></ol></div></details>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Running UTP Ethernet beyond <strong>100 meters</strong>—signal attenuation causes packet loss; use fiber or add a switch/repeater.</li><li>Using a <strong>straight-through</strong> cable between two PCs in Packet Tracer when Auto-MDIX is off (the link stays red!).</li><li>Thinking <strong>fiber-optic</strong> carries electricity. It carries pulses of <strong>light</strong>, so it is immune to electromagnetic interference.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>UTP (Unshielded Twisted Pair)</strong></td><td>standard copper Ethernet cable with 4 twisted wire pairs</td><td lang=\"tr\">korumalı olmayan bükümlü çift kablo (UTP)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>fiber-optic cable</strong></td><td>glass or plastic strand that carries data as pulses of light</td><td lang=\"tr\">fiber optik kablo</td></tr><tr><td><strong>straight-through cable</strong></td><td>Ethernet cable wired identically at both ends (used for unlike devices)</td><td lang=\"tr\">düz kablo</td></tr><tr><td><strong>crossover cable</strong></td><td>Ethernet cable with transmit and receive pairs swapped (used for like devices)</td><td lang=\"tr\">çapraz kablo</td></tr><tr><td><strong>EMI (Electromagnetic Interference)</strong></td><td>electrical noise that can corrupt signals on copper cables</td><td lang=\"tr\">elektromanyetik girişim</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"Which cable type is immune to electromagnetic interference (EMI) and supports runs well over 100 meters?","opts":[{"h":"Cat5e UTP","why":"UTP is copper (affected by EMI) and limited to 100 m"},{"h":"Cat6 UTP","why":"Cat6 is still copper and limited to 100 m"},{"h":"Fiber-optic","ok":1,"why":"fiber uses light pulses inside glass, making it immune to EMI and suitable for long runs"},{"h":"Coaxial","why":"coaxial uses copper and is still susceptible to strong EMI"}],"why":"Fiber-optic cables transmit light rather than electricity, so EMI does not affect them."},{"q":"Without Auto-MDIX, which Ethernet cable connects a <strong>PC directly to another PC</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"Straight-through","why":"straight-through connects unlike devices (PC to switch, switch to router)"},{"h":"Crossover","ok":1,"why":"crossover swaps transmit and receive pins so two like devices can talk"},{"h":"Rollover (console)","why":"console cables connect a PC serial port to a router/switch console port for CLI setup"},{"h":"Coaxial","why":"PCs use RJ-45 twisted pair, not coax"}],"why":"Like devices (PC–PC, Switch–Switch, Router–Router) require a crossover cable when Auto-MDIX is absent."},{"q":"What is the maximum recommended length for a standard Cat5e/Cat6 Ethernet UTP cable run?","opts":[{"h":"10 meters","why":"10 m is typical for Bluetooth, not Ethernet"},{"h":"50 meters","why":"Ethernet UTP goes farther than 50 m"},{"h":"100 meters","ok":1,"why":"100 m is the standard Ethernet twisted-pair distance limit"},{"h":"2,000 meters","why":"2 km applies to multi-mode fiber, not copper UTP"}],"why":"Standard Ethernet over twisted-pair copper is rated up to 100 meters."}]],"id":"net-04","title":"Cabling and transmission media","goal":"compare twisted-pair, coaxial, fiber-optic, and wireless media and choose between straight-through and crossover Ethernet cables","week":"3","minutes":16,"terms":[{"t":"UTP (Unshielded Twisted Pair)","m":"standard copper Ethernet cable with 4 twisted wire pairs","tr":"korumalı olmayan bükümlü çift kablo (UTP)"},{"t":"fiber-optic cable","m":"glass or plastic strand that carries data as pulses of light","tr":"fiber optik kablo"},{"t":"straight-through cable","m":"Ethernet cable wired identically at both ends (used for unlike devices)","tr":"düz kablo"},{"t":"crossover cable","m":"Ethernet cable with transmit and receive pairs swapped (used for like devices)","tr":"çapraz kablo"},{"t":"EMI (Electromagnetic Interference)","m":"electrical noise that can corrupt signals on copper cables","tr":"elektromanyetik girişim"}]},"net-05":{"html":"<p><strong>Cisco Packet Tracer</strong> is a free network simulator used in İGÜ's Computer Networks labs. It lets you build virtual LANs and WANs, assign IP addresses, run <code>ping</code>, and watch packets travel step by step—without needing physical switches or routers at home.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-getting-packet-tracer\"><h2 id=\"getting-packet-tracer\">Getting Packet Tracer</h2>\n<ol><li>Create a free <strong>Cisco Networking Academy</strong> account at <code>netacad.com</code> (or use your university-provided installer).</li><li>Download <strong>Cisco Packet Tracer</strong> for Windows (64-bit) or macOS and install it.</li><li>Sign in once when prompted, then you can save and open <code>.pkt</code> lab files offline.</li></ol>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-parts-of-the-workspace\"><h2 id=\"key-parts-of-the-workspace\">Key parts of the workspace</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Where it is</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Logical / Physical</strong></td><td>Top-left bar</td><td><strong>Logical</strong> is where you draw and cable networks; <strong>Physical</strong> shows racks/rooms</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Device-Type Selection</strong></td><td>Bottom-left</td><td>Categories: Network Devices (Routers, Switches), End Devices (PCs, Servers), Connections (Cables)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Specific Devices</strong></td><td>Bottom-center</td><td>Exact models inside the chosen category (e.g., <code>2960-24TT</code> switch, <code>PC-PT</code>, <code>1941</code> router)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Realtime vs. Simulation</strong></td><td>Bottom-right</td><td><strong>Realtime</strong> runs immediately; <strong>Simulation</strong> pauses time so you can step through each packet</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Simple PDU (envelope)</strong></td><td>Right toolbar</td><td>Quick icon to test <code>ping</code> between two clicked devices</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-reading-link-lights\"><h2 id=\"reading-link-lights\">Reading link lights</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Green triangle</strong>: the link is up and ready.</li><li><strong>Amber (orange) circle</strong> on a switch port: Spanning Tree Protocol is checking for loops—wait ~30 seconds (or click <strong>Fast Forward Time</strong> <code>Alt+D</code> in the bottom bar) until it turns green.</li><li><strong>Red triangle</strong>: port is shut down, unplugged, or using the wrong cable type.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Open Cisco Packet Tracer on your computer and complete this setup checklist:</p>\n<div class=\"widget checklist\" data-widget=\"checklist\" data-id=\"net/05-packet-tracer-setup.md#1\"><div class=\"checklist-h\">Packet Tracer readiness checklist</div><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\" data-i=\"0\"><span>Installed and signed into Cisco Packet Tracer</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\" data-i=\"1\"><span>Located End Devices → PC (<code>PC-PT</code>) and Network Devices → Switches (<code>2960-24TT</code>)</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\" data-i=\"2\"><span>Found Connections → Copper Straight-Through (solid black line) and Copper Cross-Over (dashed black line)</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\" data-i=\"3\"><span>Switched between <strong>Realtime</strong> and <strong>Simulation</strong> modes in the bottom-right corner</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\" data-i=\"4\"><span>Tested <strong>Fast Forward Time</strong> (<code>Alt+D</code>) to skip the 30-second switch port startup delay</span></label></li></ul></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Using the <strong>Lightning Bolt (Automatically Choose Connection Type)</strong> for everything. It hides whether a link needs straight-through or crossover—practice picking the exact cable!</li><li>Thinking a PC cannot talk because the switch port is <strong>orange/amber</strong>. Switch ports take ~30 seconds to turn green unless you click <strong>Fast Forward Time</strong>.</li><li>Trying to delete a device with <code>BackSpace</code> when the <strong>Select</strong> tool isn't active—press <code>Esc</code> or click the Select arrow first, or use the <strong>Delete (X)</strong> tool carefully.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Packet Tracer</strong></td><td>Cisco's network simulation software for building and testing virtual topologies</td><td lang=\"tr\">Packet Tracer</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Realtime mode</strong></td><td>simulation mode where network traffic runs at normal speed</td><td lang=\"tr\">gerçek zamanlı mod</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Simulation mode</strong></td><td>step-by-step mode for inspecting individual packets (PDUs)</td><td lang=\"tr\">simülasyon modu</td></tr><tr><td><strong>PDU (Protocol Data Unit)</strong></td><td>a packet or frame envelope tracked in Packet Tracer</td><td lang=\"tr\">protokol veri birimi (PDU)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"What does an <strong>amber (orange) circle</strong> on a newly connected switch port mean in Packet Tracer?","opts":[{"h":"The cable is broken","why":"a broken or wrong cable shows red triangles"},{"h":"The switch port is still initializing (Spanning Tree listening/learning)","ok":1,"why":"wait ~30 seconds or click Fast Forward Time"},{"h":"The PC has a duplicate IP address","why":"Spanning Tree happens at Layer 2 before IP traffic"},{"h":"The switch has no power","why":"an unpowered device shows red or no lights"}],"why":"Newly cabled switch ports stay amber briefly while Spanning Tree checks for Layer 2 loops."},{"q":"Which mode should you use to watch an ICMP ping packet hop from PC0 to Switch0 to PC1 step by step?","opts":[{"h":"Physical mode","why":"Physical mode shows racks and rooms, not packet-by-packet steps"},{"h":"Realtime mode","why":"Realtime runs immediately without pausing at each hop"},{"h":"Simulation mode","ok":1,"why":"Simulation mode lets you click Capture/Forward to trace every hop"},{"h":"Config tab","why":"Config tab edits device settings"}],"why":"Simulation mode pauses time and lets you inspect each PDU at every device."},{"q":"In Packet Tracer's Connections category, what does a <strong>dashed black line</strong> represent?","opts":[{"h":"Copper Straight-Through","why":"straight-through is a solid black line"},{"h":"Copper Cross-Over","ok":1,"why":"cross-over is a dashed black line"},{"h":"Fiber","why":"fiber is an orange line"},{"h":"Console","why":"console is a light-blue curved line"}],"why":"Solid black = Copper Straight-Through; dashed black = Copper Cross-Over."}]],"id":"net-05","title":"Cisco Packet Tracer setup and workspace","goal":"install or open Cisco Packet Tracer, navigate its device panels and modes, and place and cable devices cleanly","week":"4","minutes":15,"terms":[{"t":"Packet Tracer","m":"Cisco's network simulation software for building and testing virtual topologies","tr":"Packet Tracer"},{"t":"Realtime mode","m":"simulation mode where network traffic runs at normal speed","tr":"gerçek zamanlı mod"},{"t":"Simulation mode","m":"step-by-step mode for inspecting individual packets (PDUs)","tr":"simülasyon modu"},{"t":"PDU (Protocol Data Unit)","m":"a packet or frame envelope tracked in Packet Tracer","tr":"protokol veri birimi (PDU)"}]},"net-06":{"html":"<p>In this first guided lab, you will build a working <strong>Local Area Network (LAN)</strong> from scratch, configure static IPv4 addresses, and verify communication using <code>ping</code> and Simulation mode.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-lab-topology\"><h2 id=\"lab-topology\">Lab topology</h2>\n<ul><li><code>Switch0</code>: Cisco <code>2960-24TT</code></li><li><code>PC0</code>: connected to <code>Switch0</code> port <code>FastEthernet0/1</code> (<code>Copper Straight-Through</code>)</li><li><code>PC1</code>: connected to <code>Switch0</code> port <code>FastEthernet0/2</code> (<code>Copper Straight-Through</code>)</li></ul>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>IPv4 Address</th><th>Subnet Mask</th><th>Default Gateway</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>PC0</code></td><td><code>192.168.1.10</code></td><td><code>255.255.255.0</code></td><td><em>(leave blank — single LAN)</em></td></tr><tr><td><code>PC1</code></td><td><code>192.168.1.11</code></td><td><code>255.255.255.0</code></td><td><em>(leave blank — single LAN)</em></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-step-by-step-instructions\"><h2 id=\"step-by-step-instructions\">Step-by-step instructions</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Place devices</strong>: Drag two <strong>PC-PT</strong> units (<code>PC0</code>, <code>PC1</code>) and one <strong>2960-24TT</strong> switch (<code>Switch0</code>) onto the Logical workspace.</li><li><strong>Cable them</strong>: Select <strong>Copper Straight-Through</strong> (solid black line). Click <code>PC0</code> → <code>FastEthernet0</code> → <code>Switch0</code> → <code>FastEthernet0/1</code>. Repeat for <code>PC1</code> → <code>FastEthernet0/2</code>.</li><li><strong>Wait for green</strong>: Click <strong>Fast Forward Time</strong> (<code>Alt+D</code>) twice so both switch lights turn green.</li><li><strong>Configure <code>PC0</code> IP</strong>: Click <code>PC0</code> → <strong>Desktop</strong> tab → <strong>IP Configuration</strong> → select <strong>Static</strong>. Enter IPv4 <code>192.168.1.10</code> and Subnet Mask <code>255.255.255.0</code>. Close the window.</li><li><strong>Configure <code>PC1</code> IP</strong>: Repeat on <code>PC1</code> with IPv4 <code>192.168.1.11</code> and Subnet Mask <code>255.255.255.0</code>.</li><li><strong>Test with <code>ping</code></strong>: Click <code>PC0</code> → <strong>Desktop</strong> → <strong>Command Prompt</strong>. Type: <code>`text ping 192.168.1.11</code><code> You should see 4 replies (</code>Reply from 192.168.1.11: bytes=32 time&lt;1ms TTL=128`).</li><li><strong>Watch it in Simulation mode</strong>: Switch to <strong>Simulation</strong> mode, click <strong>Add Simple PDU</strong> (closed envelope), click <code>PC0</code>, then click <code>PC1</code>. Click <strong>Capture / Forward</strong> repeatedly to watch <strong>ARP</strong> find <code>PC1</code>'s MAC address first, followed by <strong>ICMP</strong> echo request and reply!</li></ol>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Follow the 7 steps above in Cisco Packet Tracer and tick each milestone as you complete it:</p>\n<div class=\"widget checklist\" data-widget=\"checklist\" data-id=\"net/06-first-lan-lab.md#1\"><div class=\"checklist-h\">Guided Lab 1 completion checklist</div><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\" data-i=\"0\"><span>Placed <code>PC0</code>, <code>PC1</code>, and <code>Switch0</code> (<code>2960-24TT</code>)</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\" data-i=\"1\"><span>Connected both PCs with Copper Straight-Through cables and saw green link triangles</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\" data-i=\"2\"><span>Assigned <code>192.168.1.10/24</code> to <code>PC0</code> and <code>192.168.1.11/24</code> to <code>PC1</code></span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\" data-i=\"3\"><span>Ran <code>ping 192.168.1.11</code> from <code>PC0</code> Command Prompt and received 4 replies (<code>0% loss</code>)</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\" data-i=\"4\"><span>Stepped through one Simple PDU in Simulation mode and observed ARP + ICMP</span></label></li></ul></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Giving both PCs the <strong>exact same IP address</strong> (<code>192.168.1.10</code> on both). Every host on a LAN must have a unique IPv4 address.</li><li>Putting <code>PC0</code> on <code>192.168.1.10</code> and <code>PC1</code> on <code>192.168.2.11</code> with mask <code>255.255.255.0</code>. Without a router, two different subnets cannot <code>ping</code> each other.</li><li>Pinging immediately while switch ports are still <strong>amber</strong>. Wait or click <strong>Fast Forward Time</strong> first.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>static IP</strong></td><td>an IPv4 address typed manually on a host instead of received from a DHCP server</td><td lang=\"tr\">statik IP</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ping</strong></td><td>a command that sends ICMP Echo Requests to test if another host is reachable</td><td lang=\"tr\">ping komutu</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ARP (Address Resolution Protocol)</strong></td><td>protocol that discovers a host's MAC address from its IPv4 address on a LAN</td><td lang=\"tr\">Adres Çözümleme Protokolü (ARP)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ICMP</strong></td><td>protocol used by <code>ping</code> and error messages to test network reachability</td><td lang=\"tr\">İnternet Kontrol Mesaj Protokolü (ICMP)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"Why does <code>PC0</code> send an <strong>ARP</strong> broadcast before sending the first <code>ping</code> packet to <code>PC1</code>?","opts":[{"h":"To ask <code>PC1</code> for its IPv4 address","why":"<code>PC0</code> already knows <code>PC1</code>'s IP (<code>192.168.1.11</code>) because you typed it"},{"h":"To learn <code>PC1</code>'s MAC address so the Ethernet frame can be delivered on the LAN","ok":1,"why":"switches forward Ethernet frames using MAC addresses"},{"h":"To connect to the internet","why":"this lab has no internet connection"},{"h":"To assign a subnet mask to <code>Switch0</code>","why":"<code>PC0</code> already has its own subnet mask"}],"why":"On an Ethernet LAN, <code>PC0</code> knows the target IP but needs the target MAC address, which ARP discovers."},{"q":"What happens if you accidentally assign <code>192.168.1.10</code> (with mask <code>255.255.255.0</code>) to <strong>both</strong> <code>PC0</code> and <code>PC1</code>?","opts":[{"h":"Both PCs share the speed equally","why":"IPs must be unique per host"},{"h":"An IP address conflict occurs and communication fails","ok":1,"why":"duplicate IPs cause ARP/routing conflicts"},{"h":"The switch automatically changes <code>PC1</code> to <code>.11</code>","why":"switches do not assign IP addresses"},{"h":"<code>ping</code> works twice as fast","why":"duplicate IPs break connectivity"}],"why":"Every host on the same network must have a unique IP address."},{"q":"You configured <code>PC0</code> as <code>192.168.1.10 / 255.255.255.0</code> and <code>PC1</code> as <code>192.168.5.11 / 255.255.255.0</code> on one switch. Why does <code>ping</code> fail?","opts":[{"h":"Switches only work with 3 or more PCs","why":"a switch works fine with 2 PCs"},{"h":"<code>192.168.1.x</code> and <code>192.168.5.x</code> are in different subnets, and there is no router between them","ok":1,"why":"a Layer 2 switch cannot route between different IP subnets"},{"h":"You need a crossover cable between a PC and a switch","why":"PC to switch uses a straight-through cable"},{"h":"<code>ping</code> requires a Default Gateway even on the same subnet","why":"same-subnet traffic never uses a gateway"}],"why":"Hosts in different subnets need a router to communicate."}]],"id":"net-06","title":"Guided lab 1 — Build your first LAN","goal":"build a two-PC Ethernet LAN with a switch in Packet Tracer, assign IPv4 addresses, and verify connectivity with ping","week":"5","minutes":25,"terms":[{"t":"static IP","m":"an IPv4 address typed manually on a host instead of received from a DHCP server","tr":"statik IP"},{"t":"ping","m":"a command that sends ICMP Echo Requests to test if another host is reachable","tr":"ping komutu"},{"t":"ARP (Address Resolution Protocol)","m":"protocol that discovers a host's MAC address from its IPv4 address on a LAN","tr":"Adres Çözümleme Protokolü (ARP)"},{"t":"ICMP","m":"protocol used by `ping` and error messages to test network reachability","tr":"İnternet Kontrol Mesaj Protokolü (ICMP)"}]},"net-07":{"html":"<p>Sending a message across a network involves many jobs: formatting text, breaking data into chunks, adding IP addresses, adding MAC addresses, and putting electrical pulses on a wire. The <strong>OSI (Open Systems Interconnection) model</strong> splits these jobs into <strong>7 layers</strong> so engineers can design and troubleshoot each part independently.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-the-7-osi-layers-top-to-bottom\"><h2 id=\"the-7-osi-layers-top-to-bottom\">The 7 OSI layers (top to bottom)</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Layer</th><th>Main job</th><th>PDU name</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>7</strong></td><td><strong>Application</strong></td><td>Network services directly for apps</td><td>Data</td><td>HTTP, DNS, SMTP, FTP</td></tr><tr><td><strong>6</strong></td><td><strong>Presentation</strong></td><td>Data formatting, encryption, compression</td><td>Data</td><td>TLS/SSL, JPEG, UTF-8</td></tr><tr><td><strong>5</strong></td><td><strong>Session</strong></td><td>Starts, manages, and closes conversations</td><td>Data</td><td>Session tokens, NetBIOS</td></tr><tr><td><strong>4</strong></td><td><strong>Transport</strong></td><td>Reliable/fast delivery, port numbers, segmentation</td><td><strong>Segment</strong> (TCP) / Datagram (UDP)</td><td>TCP, UDP</td></tr><tr><td><strong>3</strong></td><td><strong>Network</strong></td><td>Logical addressing &amp; routing across networks</td><td><strong>Packet</strong></td><td>IPv4, IPv6, ICMP, Router</td></tr><tr><td><strong>2</strong></td><td><strong>Data Link</strong></td><td>Physical addressing (MAC), framing, error check</td><td><strong>Frame</strong></td><td>Ethernet, Wi-Fi, Switch</td></tr><tr><td><strong>1</strong></td><td><strong>Physical</strong></td><td>Raw bits on wires, fiber, or radio waves</td><td><strong>Bit</strong></td><td>Cat6, RJ-45, Hub</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-tip\"><div class=\"callout-h\">Memory trick (Layer 7 → Layer 1)</div><p>\"<strong>A</strong>ll <strong>P</strong>eople <strong>S</strong>eem <strong>T</strong>o <strong>N</strong>eed <strong>D</strong>ata <strong>P</strong>rocessing\" (Application, Presentation, Session, Transport, Network, Data Link, Physical).</p></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-encapsulation-and-decapsulation\"><h2 id=\"encapsulation-and-decapsulation\">Encapsulation and decapsulation</h2>\n<p>When you send data (moving <strong>down</strong> Layers 7 → 1):</p>\n<ol><li>Application creates <strong>Data</strong>.</li><li>Transport adds a TCP/UDP header (ports) → <strong>Segment</strong>.</li><li>Network adds an IP header (source &amp; destination IP) → <strong>Packet</strong>.</li><li>Data Link adds a frame header (MAC addresses) and trailer (FCS error check) → <strong>Frame</strong>.</li><li>Physical turns the frame into <strong>Bits</strong> on the wire.</li></ol>\n<p>When the receiver gets the bits (moving <strong>up</strong> Layers 1 → 7), it strips off each header in reverse order (<strong>decapsulation</strong>).</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Practice ordering the 7 OSI layers, the encapsulation steps, and matching each layer to its PDU:</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"order\" data-set=\"osi\"></div>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"order\" data-set=\"encap\"></div>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"match\" data-set=\"pdu\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Mixing up <strong>Layer 2 (Data Link)</strong> and <strong>Layer 3 (Network)</strong>. Remember: Layer 2 uses <strong>MAC addresses</strong> inside one LAN (<strong>Frames</strong>, Switches); Layer 3 uses <strong>IP addresses</strong> between networks (<strong>Packets</strong>, Routers).</li><li>Putting <strong>HTTP</strong> or <strong>web browsers</strong> at Layer 1 or forgetting that <strong>Application layer</strong> means network protocols used by apps (not the user's desktop monitor).</li><li>Reversing the encapsulation order: encapsulation goes <strong>top-down (7 → 1)</strong> on the sender; decapsulation goes <strong>bottom-up (1 → 7)</strong> on the receiver.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>OSI model</strong></td><td>a 7-layer conceptual framework describing how network communication works</td><td lang=\"tr\">OSI modeli</td></tr><tr><td><strong>encapsulation</strong></td><td>wrapping data with protocol headers as it moves down the layers</td><td lang=\"tr\">kapsülleme</td></tr><tr><td><strong>PDU (Protocol Data Unit)</strong></td><td>the name of a chunk of data at a specific layer (segment, packet, frame, bit)</td><td lang=\"tr\">protokol veri birimi</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Data Link layer</strong></td><td>Layer 2; handles MAC addressing, framing, and local error detection</td><td lang=\"tr\">Veri Bağlantısı katmanı</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Network layer</strong></td><td>Layer 3; handles IP addressing and routing between networks</td><td lang=\"tr\">Ağ katmanı</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"Which OSI layer is responsible for <strong>logical (IP) addressing</strong> and routing packets between networks?","opts":[{"h":"Physical (Layer 1)","why":"Layer 1 only moves raw bits"},{"h":"Data Link (Layer 2)","why":"Layer 2 uses physical MAC addresses on the local link"},{"h":"Network (Layer 3)","ok":1,"why":"Layer 3 routes packets using IP addresses"},{"h":"Transport (Layer 4)","why":"Layer 4 handles ports and segmentation"}],"why":"Layer 3 (Network) handles IP addresses and routing."},{"q":"What is the PDU called at <strong>Layer 2 (Data Link)</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"Bit","why":"Bit is Layer 1 (Physical)"},{"h":"Frame","ok":1,"why":"Frame is Layer 2 (Data Link)"},{"h":"Packet","why":"Packet is Layer 3 (Network)"},{"h":"Segment","why":"Segment is Layer 4 (Transport)"}],"why":"Data Link wraps packets into Frames (with source/destination MAC and FCS)."},{"q":"Which OSI layer handles <strong>encryption (TLS)</strong> and <strong>data format conversion (UTF-8, JPEG)</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"Session (Layer 5)","why":"Session manages dialog setup and teardown"},{"h":"Presentation (Layer 6)","ok":1,"why":"Presentation handles encoding, compression, and encryption"},{"h":"Transport (Layer 4)","why":"Transport handles ports and reliability"},{"h":"Application (Layer 7)","why":"Application provides app-level protocols like HTTP and DNS"}],"why":"Layer 6 (Presentation) translates, compresses, and encrypts data."}]],"id":"net-07","title":"The OSI 7-layer model and encapsulation","goal":"name all seven OSI layers in order, describe what each layer does, and trace data encapsulation through PDUs","week":"6","minutes":20,"terms":[{"t":"OSI model","m":"a 7-layer conceptual framework describing how network communication works","tr":"OSI modeli"},{"t":"encapsulation","m":"wrapping data with protocol headers as it moves down the layers","tr":"kapsülleme"},{"t":"PDU (Protocol Data Unit)","m":"the name of a chunk of data at a specific layer (segment, packet, frame, bit)","tr":"protokol veri birimi"},{"t":"Data Link layer","m":"Layer 2; handles MAC addressing, framing, and local error detection","tr":"Veri Bağlantısı katmanı"},{"t":"Network layer","m":"Layer 3; handles IP addressing and routing between networks","tr":"Ağ katmanı"}]},"net-08":{"html":"<p>Cables and radio waves are noisy—electrical spikes or interference can flip a <code>0</code> into a <code>1</code>. Layer 2 (Data Link) handles two key jobs: <strong>checking frames for bit errors</strong> and <strong>deciding whose turn it is to transmit</strong> on a shared medium.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-error-detection\"><h2 id=\"error-detection\">Error detection</h2>\n<h3>1. Parity bit (simple)</h3>\n<p>Add one extra bit so the total number of <code>1</code>s is even (<strong>even parity</strong>) or odd (<strong>odd parity</strong>).</p>\n<ul><li>Example (even parity): data <code>1011001</code> has four <code>1</code>s (already even) → parity bit = <code>0</code> → sent as <code>10110010</code>.</li><li>Data <code>1010001</code> has three <code>1</code>s (odd) → parity bit = <code>1</code> → sent as <code>10100011</code>.</li><li>Limitation: parity catches any <strong>single-bit</strong> error (1, 3, 5… flips), but <strong>misses 2-bit errors</strong> because two flips keep the parity even!</li></ul>\n<h3>2. Checksum</h3>\n<p>The sender adds up blocks of data and attaches the sum. The receiver repeats the sum; if they differ, the data was corrupted. (Used in IPv4/TCP/UDP headers.)</p>\n<h3>3. CRC (Cyclic Redundancy Check) / FCS</h3>\n<p>Ethernet frames end with a <strong>32-bit Frame Check Sequence (FCS)</strong> computed using <strong>CRC</strong>. The sender divides the frame bits by a fixed polynomial and sends the remainder. The receiver runs the same division; if the remainder is non-zero, the frame is <strong>discarded silently</strong> (higher layers like TCP will retransmit if needed).</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-error-correction-hamming-code-and-arq\"><h2 id=\"error-correction-hamming-code-and-arq\">Error correction: Hamming code and ARQ</h2>\n<p>Detecting an error is only half the job. There are two ways to <strong>fix</strong> it:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Retransmission (ARQ — Automatic Repeat reQuest).</strong> The receiver checks each frame. If it is correct, it sends an <strong>ACK</strong> (acknowledgement). If it is damaged, it sends a <strong>NAK</strong> or sends nothing; after a <strong>timeout</strong> the sender sends the frame again. The simplest form is <strong>Stop-and-Wait ARQ</strong>: send one frame, wait for its ACK, then send the next. TCP and Wi-Fi both use retransmission.</li><li><strong>Forward error correction (FEC).</strong> The sender adds enough extra bits that the receiver can <strong>find and fix</strong> an error itself, without asking again. Useful when resending is slow or impossible (satellites, deep-space probes, memory chips).</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Hamming code (the idea).</strong> Extra <strong>parity bits</strong> are placed at positions 1, 2, 4, 8… of the codeword. Each parity bit checks a different group of positions. When one bit is flipped, some parity checks fail; adding the positions of the failed parity bits gives the <strong>position of the wrong bit</strong>, which can then be flipped back.</p>\n<p><em>Mini example — Hamming(7,4):</em> 4 data bits + 3 parity bits = 7 bits. If the receiver finds that the checks for parity bits <strong>1</strong> and <strong>4</strong> fail but check <strong>2</strong> is fine, the error is at position 1 + 4 = <strong>5</strong>. Flip bit 5 and the data is correct again.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Detects</th><th>Corrects</th><th>Typical use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Parity bit</td><td>single-bit errors</td><td>no</td><td>simple serial links</td></tr><tr><td>Checksum</td><td>many errors</td><td>no</td><td>IP, TCP, UDP headers</td></tr><tr><td>CRC</td><td>almost all burst errors</td><td>no</td><td>Ethernet and Wi-Fi frames</td></tr><tr><td>Hamming code</td><td>1–2 bit errors</td><td><strong>1-bit errors</strong></td><td>ECC memory, storage</td></tr><tr><td>ARQ</td><td>(uses one of the above)</td><td>by <strong>resending</strong></td><td>TCP, Wi-Fi</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-media-access-control-csma-cd-vs-csma-ca\"><h2 id=\"media-access-control-csma-cd-vs-csma-ca\">Media access control: CSMA/CD vs. CSMA/CA</h2>\n<p>When multiple devices share a medium, two signals at once cause a <strong>collision</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Where used</th><th>How it handles collisions</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>CSMA/CD</strong> (Collision Detection)</td><td>Old half-duplex Ethernet (hubs)</td><td>Listen before sending → send → <strong>detect</strong> collision → send jam signal, wait random backoff, retry</td></tr><tr><td><strong>CSMA/CA</strong> (Collision Avoidance)</td><td><strong>Wi-Fi (802.11)</strong></td><td>Cannot easily detect collisions while transmitting radio → listen, wait random backoff, optionally use <strong>RTS/CTS</strong> to <strong>avoid</strong> collisions</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-tip\"><div class=\"callout-h\">Why modern wired Ethernet doesn't collide</div><p>Modern switched Ethernet uses <strong>full-duplex</strong> links (separate wires for send and receive, one PC per switch port), so collisions cannot happen and CSMA/CD is not needed.</p></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Check these 8-bit bytes using <strong>even parity</strong> (the rightmost bit is the parity bit). Which bytes arrived with an error?</p>\n<ol><li><code>11010100</code></li><li><code>01101010</code></li><li><code>11111111</code></li></ol>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><ol><li><code>11010100</code>: four <code>1</code>s (even) → <strong>OK</strong> (no single-bit error detected).</li><li><code>01101010</code>: four <code>1</code>s (even) → <strong>OK</strong>.</li><li><code>11111111</code>: eight <code>1</code>s (even) → <strong>OK</strong>. Wait—what if <code>10101011</code> arrived? Five <code>1</code>s (odd) → <strong>Error detected!</strong></li></ol></div></details>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Thinking a parity bit <strong>fixes</strong> errors. Parity only <strong>detects</strong> odd numbers of flipped bits; it cannot tell which bit flipped.</li><li>Confusing <strong>CSMA/CD</strong> (wired Ethernet hubs) with <strong>CSMA/CA</strong> (wireless Wi-Fi). Remember: <strong>D</strong> = Detect (wired), <strong>A</strong> = Avoid (air/wireless).</li><li>Thinking Ethernet switches <strong>repair</strong> corrupted frames. A switch checks the FCS and simply <strong>drops</strong> bad frames.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>parity bit</strong></td><td>an extra bit added to make the number of 1s even or odd for basic error detection</td><td lang=\"tr\">eşlik biti</td></tr><tr><td><strong>CRC (Cyclic Redundancy Check)</strong></td><td>a strong polynomial-based error check used in Ethernet frames (FCS)</td><td lang=\"tr\">döngüsel artıklık denetimi</td></tr><tr><td><strong>collision</strong></td><td>two devices transmitting on a shared medium at the same time, corrupting both signals</td><td lang=\"tr\">çarpışma</td></tr><tr><td><strong>CSMA/CD</strong></td><td>Carrier Sense Multiple Access with Collision Detection (legacy wired Ethernet)</td><td lang=\"tr\">çarpışma algılamalı erişim</td></tr><tr><td><strong>CSMA/CA</strong></td><td>Carrier Sense Multiple Access with Collision Avoidance (Wi-Fi)</td><td lang=\"tr\">çarpışma önlemeli erişim</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"Using <strong>even parity</strong>, what parity bit should be added to the 7-bit data <code>1100101</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>0</code>","ok":1,"why":"there are already four 1s (even), so parity bit is 0"},{"h":"<code>1</code>","why":"adding 1 would make five 1s (odd)"},{"h":"<code>10</code>","why":"a parity bit is always a single bit"},{"h":"<code>01</code>","why":"a parity bit is always a single bit"}],"why":"<code>1100101</code> has four <code>1</code>s, which is already even, so the even parity bit is <code>0</code>."},{"q":"Which media access method is used by <strong>Wi-Fi (802.11)</strong> networks?","opts":[{"h":"CSMA/CD","why":"CSMA/CD is for legacy shared-wire Ethernet, not radio"},{"h":"CSMA/CA","ok":1,"why":"Wi-Fi uses Collision Avoidance because wireless radios cannot easily listen while transmitting"},{"h":"Token Ring","why":"Token Ring is an older wired ring LAN technology"},{"h":"Full-duplex switching","why":"Wi-Fi shares open radio airwaves (half-duplex)"}],"why":"Wireless LANs use CSMA/CA to avoid collisions before transmitting."},{"q":"What does an Ethernet switch do when a received frame fails the <strong>FCS (CRC)</strong> check?","opts":[{"h":"Fixes the flipped bit automatically","why":"CRC detects errors; it does not correct them"},{"h":"Drops (discards) the corrupted frame","ok":1,"why":"Layer 2 drops bad frames so upper layers can retransmit if needed"},{"h":"Sends it to all ports","why":"flooding a corrupted frame wastes bandwidth"},{"h":"Converts it to a packet","why":"corrupted frames are discarded"}],"why":"Failed FCS frames are discarded at Layer 2."}]],"id":"net-08","title":"Error detection and media access control","goal":"detect single-bit errors with parity, explain how CRC/FCS protects frames, and compare CSMA/CD with CSMA/CA","week":"8","minutes":16,"terms":[{"t":"parity bit","m":"an extra bit added to make the number of 1s even or odd for basic error detection","tr":"eşlik biti"},{"t":"CRC (Cyclic Redundancy Check)","m":"a strong polynomial-based error check used in Ethernet frames (FCS)","tr":"döngüsel artıklık denetimi"},{"t":"collision","m":"two devices transmitting on a shared medium at the same time, corrupting both signals","tr":"çarpışma"},{"t":"CSMA/CD","m":"Carrier Sense Multiple Access with Collision Detection (legacy wired Ethernet)","tr":"çarpışma algılamalı erişim"},{"t":"CSMA/CA","m":"Carrier Sense Multiple Access with Collision Avoidance (Wi-Fi)","tr":"çarpışma önlemeli erişim"}]},"net-09":{"html":"<p>While the OSI model has 7 layers for teaching and troubleshooting, the real internet runs on the <strong>4-layer TCP/IP model</strong>.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-tcp-ip-vs-osi\"><h2 id=\"tcp-ip-vs-osi\">TCP/IP vs. OSI</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>TCP/IP Layer</th><th>Equivalent OSI Layers</th><th>Key protocols</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Application</strong></td><td>7 (Application), 6 (Presentation), 5 (Session)</td><td>HTTP, HTTPS, DNS, DHCP, FTP, SSH, SMTP</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Transport</strong></td><td>4 (Transport)</td><td><strong>TCP</strong>, <strong>UDP</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Internet</strong></td><td>3 (Network)</td><td><strong>IPv4</strong>, <strong>IPv6</strong>, ICMP, ARP</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Network Access (Link)</strong></td><td>2 (Data Link), 1 (Physical)</td><td>Ethernet, Wi-Fi (802.11)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-tcp-vs-udp-transport-layer\"><h2 id=\"tcp-vs-udp-transport-layer\">TCP vs. UDP (Transport layer)</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th><strong>TCP</strong> (Transmission Control Protocol)</th><th><strong>UDP</strong> (User Datagram Protocol)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Connection</strong></td><td>Connection-oriented (<strong>3-way handshake</strong>: <code>SYN</code> → <code>SYN-ACK</code> → <code>ACK</code>)</td><td>Connectionless (send immediately)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Reliability</strong></td><td>Reliable: ACKs, retransmits lost packets, preserves order</td><td>Best-effort: no ACKs, no retransmission</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Overhead</strong></td><td>Higher (20-byte header)</td><td>Low (8-byte header), very fast</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Use cases</strong></td><td>Web (HTTP/HTTPS), Email (SMTP), File transfer (FTP), SSH</td><td>Video calls, online gaming, DNS lookups, DHCP, streaming</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-well-known-port-numbers-memorize-for-exams\"><h2 id=\"well-known-port-numbers-memorize-for-exams\">Well-known port numbers (memorize for exams!)</h2>\n<p>A <strong>port number</strong> (0–65535) identifies which application on a host should receive the data. Well-known ports are <code>0–1023</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Port</th><th>Protocol</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Transport</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>20 / 21</strong></td><td>FTP</td><td>File Transfer Protocol (data / control)</td><td>TCP</td></tr><tr><td><strong>22</strong></td><td>SSH</td><td>Secure encrypted remote shell</td><td>TCP</td></tr><tr><td><strong>23</strong></td><td>Telnet</td><td>Unencrypted remote shell (insecure)</td><td>TCP</td></tr><tr><td><strong>25</strong></td><td>SMTP</td><td>Sending outgoing email</td><td>TCP</td></tr><tr><td><strong>53</strong></td><td>DNS</td><td>Translating domain names (e.g., <code>igu.edu.tr</code>) to IP addresses</td><td>UDP (mostly) / TCP</td></tr><tr><td><strong>67 / 68</strong></td><td>DHCP</td><td>Automatically assigning IP addresses to clients</td><td>UDP</td></tr><tr><td><strong>80</strong></td><td>HTTP</td><td>Unencrypted web pages</td><td>TCP</td></tr><tr><td><strong>443</strong></td><td>HTTPS</td><td>Encrypted web pages (HTTP + TLS)</td><td>TCP</td></tr><tr><td><strong>143</strong></td><td>IMAP</td><td>Read email that stays on the server (sync across devices)</td><td>TCP</td></tr><tr><td><strong>3389</strong></td><td>RDP</td><td>Remote Desktop (control a Windows PC over the network)</td><td>TCP</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Order the 4 TCP/IP layers (top to bottom) and match common protocols to their default port numbers:</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"order\" data-set=\"tcpip\"></div>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"match\" data-set=\"ports\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Thinking <strong>UDP</strong> is \"bad\" because it is unreliable. Real-time voice/video and gaming prefer UDP because waiting to retransmit a lost 20 ms audio frame would cause lag.</li><li>Confusing <strong>port 80 (HTTP)</strong> and <strong>port 443 (HTTPS)</strong>, or <strong>SSH (22)</strong> and <strong>Telnet (23)</strong>.</li><li>Forgetting that <strong>DNS</strong> uses port <strong>53</strong> and <strong>DHCP</strong> uses <strong>67/68</strong>.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>TCP/IP model</strong></td><td>the 4-layer protocol architecture used by the modern internet</td><td lang=\"tr\">TCP/IP modeli</td></tr><tr><td><strong>TCP</strong></td><td>reliable, connection-oriented transport protocol using a 3-way handshake</td><td lang=\"tr\">İletim Denetim Protokolü (TCP)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>UDP</strong></td><td>fast, connectionless transport protocol with low overhead</td><td lang=\"tr\">Kullanıcı Datagram Protokolü (UDP)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>port number</strong></td><td>a 16-bit number identifying a specific service or application on a host</td><td lang=\"tr\">port numarası</td></tr><tr><td><strong>three-way handshake</strong></td><td>TCP's <code>SYN</code>, <code>SYN-ACK</code>, <code>ACK</code> sequence to open a connection</td><td lang=\"tr\">üçlü el sıkışma</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"Which protocol uses a <strong>3-way handshake</strong> (<code>SYN</code>, <code>SYN-ACK</code>, <code>ACK</code>) to guarantee reliable, ordered delivery?","opts":[{"h":"UDP","why":"UDP is connectionless and has no handshake"},{"h":"TCP","ok":1,"why":"TCP establishes a connection with SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK and retransmits lost segments"},{"h":"IP","why":"IP is Layer 3 best-effort packet routing"},{"h":"ICMP","why":"ICMP sends diagnostic messages like ping"}],"why":"TCP guarantees reliable delivery via sequence numbers, acknowledgments, and the 3-way handshake."},{"q":"Which port number does <strong>HTTPS</strong> (encrypted web traffic) use by default?","opts":[{"h":"22","why":"port 22 is SSH"},{"h":"53","why":"port 53 is DNS"},{"h":"80","why":"port 80 is unencrypted HTTP"},{"h":"443","ok":1,"why":"port 443 is HTTPS"}],"why":"HTTP uses TCP port 80; HTTPS uses TCP port 443."},{"q":"Why do live voice and video calls usually use <strong>UDP</strong> instead of <strong>TCP</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"UDP encrypts audio automatically","why":"neither TCP nor UDP encrypts by itself (TLS/DTLS does)"},{"h":"UDP avoids retransmission delays and has smaller headers, keeping live audio smooth","ok":1,"why":"a late retransmitted packet is useless in a live call"},{"h":"UDP uses IP addresses while TCP does not","why":"both run on top of IP"},{"h":"TCP cannot carry audio or video files","why":"TCP can carry any bytes, just with higher latency on loss"}],"why":"Real-time media tolerates small drops better than retransmission stalls, so UDP is preferred."}]],"id":"net-09","title":"The TCP/IP model, TCP vs. UDP, and ports","goal":"map the 4-layer TCP/IP model to OSI, contrast TCP and UDP, and match common application protocols to their port numbers","week":"9","minutes":20,"terms":[{"t":"TCP/IP model","m":"the 4-layer protocol architecture used by the modern internet","tr":"TCP/IP modeli"},{"t":"TCP","m":"reliable, connection-oriented transport protocol using a 3-way handshake","tr":"İletim Denetim Protokolü (TCP)"},{"t":"UDP","m":"fast, connectionless transport protocol with low overhead","tr":"Kullanıcı Datagram Protokolü (UDP)"},{"t":"port number","m":"a 16-bit number identifying a specific service or application on a host","tr":"port numarası"},{"t":"three-way handshake","m":"TCP's `SYN`, `SYN-ACK`, `ACK` sequence to open a connection","tr":"üçlü el sıkışma"}]},"net-10":{"html":"<p>Computers and switches only understand <strong>binary</strong> (<code>0</code> and <code>1</code>). To make long 32-bit IPv4 addresses and 48-bit MAC addresses readable for humans, we write IPv4 in <strong>dotted decimal</strong> and MAC/IPv6 in <strong>hexadecimal</strong>.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-binary-powers-of-2-1-byte-8-bits\"><h2 id=\"binary-powers-of-2-1-byte-8-bits\">Binary powers of 2 (1 byte = 8 bits)</h2>\n<p>Each bit position in an <strong>octet</strong> (8 bits) has a place value:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Bit position</th><th><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^7\"></span></th><th><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^6\"></span></th><th><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^5\"></span></th><th><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^4\"></span></th><th><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^3\"></span></th><th><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^2\"></span></th><th><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^1\"></span></th><th><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^0\"></span></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Value</strong></td><td><strong>128</strong></td><td><strong>64</strong></td><td><strong>32</strong></td><td><strong>16</strong></td><td><strong>8</strong></td><td><strong>4</strong></td><td><strong>2</strong></td><td><strong>1</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li>Minimum 8-bit value: <code>00000000</code> = <strong>0</strong></li><li>Maximum 8-bit value: <code>11111111</code> = <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"128+64+32+16+8+4+2+1\"></span> = <strong>255</strong></li></ul>\n<h3>Example: Convert <code>192</code> to 8-bit binary</h3>\n<p>Subtract the largest powers of 2 that fit:</p>\n<ul><li><code>192 &gt;= 128</code> → bit 128 is <code>1</code>, remainder <code>192 - 128 = 64</code></li><li><code>64 &gt;= 64</code> → bit 64 is <code>1</code>, remainder <code>0</code></li><li>All remaining bits (<code>32, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1</code>) are <code>0</code></li><li>Result: <code>11000000</code></li></ul>\n<h3>Example: Convert <code>10101000</code> to decimal</h3>\n<p>Add the values where there is a <code>1</code>: $<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"128 + 32 + 8 = 168\"></span>$</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-ipv4-address-structure\"><h2 id=\"ipv4-address-structure\">IPv4 address structure</h2>\n<p>An <strong>IPv4 address</strong> is <strong>32 bits</strong> long, split into <strong>4 octets</strong> of 8 bits each, separated by dots:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><code>192      . 168      . 1        . 10\n11000000 . 10101000 . 00000001 . 00001010</code></pre>\n<p>Because each octet is 8 bits, every number in a valid IPv4 address must be between <strong>0 and 255</strong>. An address like <code>192.168.300.1</code> is invalid!</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-hexadecimal-base-16\"><h2 id=\"hexadecimal-base-16\">Hexadecimal (base 16)</h2>\n<p>Hex uses 16 digits: <code>0–9</code> and <code>A–F</code> (<code>A=10, B=11, C=12, D=13, E=14, F=15</code>). Every <strong>1 hex digit = 4 binary bits</strong> (a nibble), so <strong>2 hex digits = 1 byte</strong>:</p>\n<ul><li><code>1111 1111</code> = <code>FF</code> (255)</li><li><code>1100 0000</code> = <code>C0</code> (192)</li><li>MAC address example: <code>00:1A:2B:3C:4D:5E</code> (6 bytes = 48 bits).</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Use the interactive base converter below to test binary, decimal, and hex conversions (<code>192</code>, <code>168</code>, <code>255</code>, <code>10</code>, <code>0xFF</code>, <code>11110000</code>):</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"baseconv\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Writing <code>256</code> or higher in an IPv4 octet. Eight bits can only hold <code>0</code> through <code>255</code> (<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^8 = 256\"></span> values total, starting at <code>0</code>).</li><li>Dropping leading zeros when aligning 8-bit octets—<code>1</code> in an IPv4 octet is <code>00000001</code>, not just <code>1</code>, when doing subnetting math.</li><li>Confusing hex <code>A</code>–<code>F</code> values (<code>A=10</code>, <code>F=15</code>, not <code>A=11</code>).</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>octet</strong></td><td>an 8-bit group (0–255) forming one quarter of an IPv4 address</td><td lang=\"tr\">sekizli (oktet)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>binary</strong></td><td>base-2 number system using only digits 0 and 1</td><td lang=\"tr\">ikilik sayı sistemi</td></tr><tr><td><strong>hexadecimal</strong></td><td>base-16 number system using 0–9 and A–F (used in MAC and IPv6)</td><td lang=\"tr\">onaltılık sayı sistemi</td></tr><tr><td><strong>IPv4 address</strong></td><td>a 32-bit logical address written as four decimal octets</td><td lang=\"tr\">IPv4 adresi</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"What is the decimal value of the 8-bit binary number <code>11000000</code>?","opts":[{"h":"128","why":"128 is <code>10000000</code>"},{"h":"192","ok":1,"why":"<code>128 + 64 = 192</code>"},{"h":"196","why":"<code>128 + 64 + 4</code> would be <code>11000100</code>"},{"h":"224","why":"<code>128 + 64 + 32</code> would be <code>11100000</code>"}],"why":"Only the 128 and 64 bits are 1, so <code>128 + 64 = 192</code>."},{"q":"Why is <code>192.168.1.260</code> an <strong>invalid</strong> IPv4 address?","opts":[{"h":"It has too many dots","why":"it has 3 dots (4 octets), which is normal"},{"h":"One octet (<code>260</code>) is greater than 255","ok":1,"why":"8 bits can only represent 0 through 255"},{"h":"<code>192.168</code> cannot be used in private networks","why":"<code>192.168.0.0/16</code> is a valid private range"},{"h":"IPv4 addresses cannot end in 0","why":"260 is out of range, not ending in 0"}],"why":"Each octet is 8 bits, so its maximum possible value is 255."},{"q":"How many bits are in a standard <strong>IPv4</strong> address?","opts":[{"h":"8 bits","why":"8 bits is one octet"},{"h":"16 bits","why":"16 bits is two octets"},{"h":"32 bits","ok":1,"why":"4 octets × 8 bits = 32 bits"},{"h":"128 bits","why":"128 bits is IPv6"}],"why":"IPv4 addresses are 32 bits long (IPv6 addresses are 128 bits)."}]],"id":"net-10","title":"Binary, hexadecimal, and IPv4 addressing","goal":"convert between binary, decimal, and hexadecimal, and read 32-bit IPv4 addresses in dotted-decimal form","week":"10","minutes":20,"terms":[{"t":"octet","m":"an 8-bit group (0–255) forming one quarter of an IPv4 address","tr":"sekizli (oktet)"},{"t":"binary","m":"base-2 number system using only digits 0 and 1","tr":"ikilik sayı sistemi"},{"t":"hexadecimal","m":"base-16 number system using 0–9 and A–F (used in MAC and IPv6)","tr":"onaltılık sayı sistemi"},{"t":"IPv4 address","m":"a 32-bit logical address written as four decimal octets","tr":"IPv4 adresi"}]},"net-11":{"html":"<p>Before modern CIDR subnetting, IPv4 addresses were divided into fixed classes. Even today, you must know the <strong>class ranges</strong> and <strong>private IP ranges</strong> for exams and lab setups.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-traditional-ipv4-classes-by-first-octet\"><h2 id=\"traditional-ipv4-classes-by-first-octet\">Traditional IPv4 classes (by first octet)</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>First octet</th><th>Default mask</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>A</strong></td><td><code>1 – 126</code></td><td><code>255.0.0.0</code> (<code>/8</code>)</td><td>Very large networks</td></tr><tr><td><strong>B</strong></td><td><code>128 – 191</code></td><td><code>255.255.0.0</code> (<code>/16</code>)</td><td>Medium-to-large networks</td></tr><tr><td><strong>C</strong></td><td><code>192 – 223</code></td><td><code>255.255.255.0</code> (<code>/24</code>)</td><td>Small LANs (up to 254 hosts)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>D</strong></td><td><code>224 – 239</code></td><td>—</td><td>Multicast (group streaming)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>E</strong></td><td><code>240 – 255</code></td><td>—</td><td>Experimental / reserved</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Special reserved IPv4 addresses</h3>\n<ul><li><strong><code>127.0.0.0/8</code> (<code>127.0.0.1</code>)</strong>: <strong>Loopback</strong>—tests your own PC's TCP/IP stack (<code>ping 127.0.0.1</code>).</li><li><strong><code>169.254.0.0/16</code></strong>: <strong>APIPA</strong>—self-assigned by Windows/macOS when a DHCP server cannot be reached.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-private-vs-public-ipv4-nat\"><h2 id=\"private-vs-public-ipv4-nat\">Private vs. public IPv4 &amp; NAT</h2>\n<p>There are only ~4.3 billion IPv4 addresses—not enough for every phone and laptop on Earth. <strong>RFC 1918</strong> reserves three <strong>private</strong> ranges for internal LANs (not routable on the public internet):</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Private IPv4 range</th><th>CIDR</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>A</strong></td><td><code>10.0.0.0</code> – <code>10.255.255.255</code></td><td><code>10.0.0.0/8</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>B</strong></td><td><code>172.16.0.0</code> – <code>172.31.255.255</code></td><td><code>172.16.0.0/12</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>C</strong></td><td><code>192.168.0.0</code> – <code>192.168.255.255</code></td><td><code>192.168.0.0/16</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>NAT (Network Address Translation)</strong> runs on your home or campus router. It translates your LAN's private IPs (like <code>192.168.1.10</code>) into the router's single <strong>public IP</strong> when traffic goes out to the internet.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-ipv6-overview\"><h2 id=\"ipv6-overview\">IPv6 overview</h2>\n<p><strong>IPv6</strong> solves IPv4 exhaustion permanently with <strong>128-bit</strong> addresses (<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"3.4 \\times 10^{38}\"></span> addresses!), written as <strong>8 groups of 4 hex digits</strong> separated by colons:</p>\n<pre class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><code>2001:0db8:85a3:0000:0000:8a2e:0370:7334</code></pre>\n<p>Shortening rules:</p>\n<ol><li>Drop leading zeros in each group (<code>0db8</code> → <code>db8</code>).</li><li>Replace one consecutive block of all-zero groups with <code>::</code> (<code>2001:db8:85a3::8a2e:370:7334</code>). Loopback in IPv6 is <code>::1</code>.</li></ol>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Classify each IPv4 address as <strong>Public</strong>, <strong>Private</strong>, <strong>Loopback</strong>, or <strong>APIPA</strong>:</p>\n<ol><li><code>192.168.10.5</code></li><li><code>172.20.4.1</code></li><li><code>172.32.4.1</code></li><li><code>169.254.12.88</code></li><li><code>8.8.8.8</code></li></ol>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><ol><li><code>192.168.10.5</code> → <strong>Private</strong> (Class C private range <code>192.168.0.0/16</code>)</li><li><code>172.20.4.1</code> → <strong>Private</strong> (inside <code>172.16.0.0</code> – <code>172.31.255.255</code>)</li><li><code>172.32.4.1</code> → <strong>Public</strong> (just outside <code>172.31.255.255</code>!)</li><li><code>169.254.12.88</code> → <strong>APIPA</strong> (DHCP failure fallback)</li><li><code>8.8.8.8</code> → <strong>Public</strong> (Google Public DNS)</li></ol></div></details>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Thinking <code>172.32.0.1</code> is private. Only <code>172.16.0.0</code> through <code>172.31.255.255</code> is private!</li><li>Thinking <code>127.0.0.1</code> is a public or private LAN IP. <code>127.0.0.0/8</code> is strictly <strong>loopback</strong> (never leaves your own host).</li><li>Using <code>::</code> twice in a shortened IPv6 address. You may use <code>::</code> <strong>at most once</strong> per address, otherwise the length is ambiguous.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>private IP</strong></td><td>an RFC 1918 IPv4 address used inside LANs and not routed on the public internet</td><td lang=\"tr\">özel IP</td></tr><tr><td><strong>NAT (Network Address Translation)</strong></td><td>router feature that translates private LAN IPs to a public IP for internet access</td><td lang=\"tr\">Ağ Adresi Çevirisi (NAT)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>loopback (<code>127.0.0.1</code>)</strong></td><td>special address pointing back to the local host for testing TCP/IP</td><td lang=\"tr\">geri döngü adresi</td></tr><tr><td><strong>APIPA (<code>169.254.x.x</code>)</strong></td><td>automatic local IP assigned when a host cannot reach a DHCP server</td><td lang=\"tr\">APIPA</td></tr><tr><td><strong>IPv6</strong></td><td>128-bit next-generation IP addressing written in hexadecimal</td><td lang=\"tr\">IPv6</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"Which IPv4 address is a <strong>private</strong> address?","opts":[{"h":"<code>11.0.0.5</code>","why":"<code>10.x.x.x</code> is private; <code>11.x.x.x</code> is public"},{"h":"<code>172.35.1.1</code>","why":"Class B private stops at <code>172.31.255.255</code>"},{"h":"<code>172.18.5.10</code>","ok":1,"why":"falls inside <code>172.16.0.0</code> – <code>172.31.255.255</code>"},{"h":"<code>192.169.1.1</code>","why":"<code>192.168.x.x</code> is private; <code>192.169.x.x</code> is public"}],"why":"The three private IPv4 ranges are <code>10.0.0.0/8</code>, <code>172.16.0.0/12</code>, and <code>192.168.0.0/16</code>."},{"q":"You run <code>ipconfig</code> on a Windows PC and see <code>169.254.44.12</code>. What happened?","opts":[{"h":"The PC has a static public IP","why":"<code>169.254.x.x</code> is APIPA, not public"},{"h":"The PC failed to get an IP from a DHCP server and self-assigned an APIPA address","ok":1,"why":"check the cable or DHCP server"},{"h":"The PC is using loopback","why":"loopback is <code>127.0.0.1</code>"},{"h":"NAT is disabled on the router","why":"APIPA happens before reaching a router"}],"why":"<code>169.254.0.0/16</code> (APIPA) indicates DHCP discovery failed."},{"q":"What is the main purpose of <strong>NAT</strong> on a home or campus edge router?","opts":[{"h":"To convert IPv6 addresses into MAC addresses","why":"NAT translates IPv4 addresses"},{"h":"To let many devices with private LAN IPs share one (or a few) public IPv4 addresses","ok":1,"why":"NAT preserves scarce public IPv4 addresses"},{"h":"To replace DNS servers","why":"DNS resolves domain names; NAT translates IPs"},{"h":"To speed up Ethernet cables","why":"NAT is a Layer 3/4 address translation function"}],"why":"NAT maps private internal IPs to public internet IPs."}]],"id":"net-11","title":"IPv4 classes, private IPs, NAT, and IPv6","goal":"identify IPv4 address classes and special ranges, explain why NAT exists, and recognize IPv6 address notation","week":"10–11","minutes":18,"terms":[{"t":"private IP","m":"an RFC 1918 IPv4 address used inside LANs and not routed on the public internet","tr":"özel IP"},{"t":"NAT (Network Address Translation)","m":"router feature that translates private LAN IPs to a public IP for internet access","tr":"Ağ Adresi Çevirisi (NAT)"},{"t":"loopback (`127.0.0.1`)","m":"special address pointing back to the local host for testing TCP/IP","tr":"geri döngü adresi"},{"t":"APIPA (`169.254.x.x`)","m":"automatic local IP assigned when a host cannot reach a DHCP server","tr":"APIPA"},{"t":"IPv6","m":"128-bit next-generation IP addressing written in hexadecimal","tr":"IPv6"}]},"net-12":{"html":"<p><strong>Subnetting</strong> splits one large IPv4 network into smaller networks (<strong>subnets</strong>). This reduces broadcast traffic, improves security, and saves IP addresses.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-the-subnet-mask-and-cidr-n\"><h2 id=\"the-subnet-mask-and-cidr-n\">The subnet mask and CIDR (<code>/n</code>)</h2>\n<p>Every IPv4 address has two parts: the <strong>network portion</strong> (left) and the <strong>host portion</strong> (right). The <strong>subnet mask</strong> tells your device where the split happens:</p>\n<ul><li>Mask <code>1</code> bits = <strong>network</strong></li><li>Mask <code>0</code> bits = <strong>host</strong></li></ul>\n<p><strong>CIDR</strong> (Classless Inter-Domain Routing) writes the count of <code>1</code> bits after a slash:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>CIDR</th><th>Subnet Mask</th><th>Host bits (<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"h\"></span>)</th><th>Usable hosts (<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^h - 2\"></span>)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>/24</code></td><td><code>255.255.255.0</code></td><td>8</td><td><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^8 - 2 = \\mathbf{254}\"></span></td></tr><tr><td><code>/25</code></td><td><code>255.255.255.128</code></td><td>7</td><td><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^7 - 2 = \\mathbf{126}\"></span></td></tr><tr><td><code>/26</code></td><td><code>255.255.255.192</code></td><td>6</td><td><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^6 - 2 = \\mathbf{62}\"></span></td></tr><tr><td><code>/27</code></td><td><code>255.255.255.224</code></td><td>5</td><td><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^5 - 2 = \\mathbf{30}\"></span></td></tr><tr><td><code>/28</code></td><td><code>255.255.255.240</code></td><td>4</td><td><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^4 - 2 = \\mathbf{14}\"></span></td></tr><tr><td><code>/29</code></td><td><code>255.255.255.248</code></td><td>3</td><td><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^3 - 2 = \\mathbf{6}\"></span></td></tr><tr><td><code>/30</code></td><td><code>255.255.255.252</code></td><td>2</td><td><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^2 - 2 = \\mathbf{2}\"></span> (router links!)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Why <strong><span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"- 2\"></span></strong>? Every subnet reserves two addresses that cannot be assigned to hosts:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>First address</strong> (all host bits <code>0</code>): <strong>Network Address</strong> (identifies the subnet itself).</li><li><strong>Last address</strong> (all host bits <code>1</code>): <strong>Broadcast Address</strong> (reaches every host in that subnet).</li></ol>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-fast-block-size-method-the-exam-shortcut\"><h2 id=\"fast-block-size-method-the-exam-shortcut\">Fast \"block size\" method (the exam shortcut!)</h2>\n<p>Suppose you are given <code>192.168.10.75/26</code>.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Find the interesting octet</strong>: <code>/26</code> means 24 network bits + 2 bits in the 4th octet (<code>255.255.255.192</code>).</li><li><strong>Block size</strong> = <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"256 - 192 = \\mathbf{64}\"></span>. Subnets step by <code>64</code>: <code>0, 64, 128, 192</code>.</li><li><strong>Locate <code>75</code></strong>: <code>75</code> falls between <code>64</code> and <code>128</code>! - <strong>Network address</strong>: <code>192.168.10.64</code> - <strong>Next subnet</strong>: <code>192.168.10.128</code> → so <strong>Broadcast</strong> is one less: <code>192.168.10.127</code> - <strong>First usable host</strong>: <code>192.168.10.65</code> - <strong>Last usable host</strong>: <code>192.168.10.126</code> - <strong>Usable hosts</strong>: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"64 - 2 = 62\"></span>.</li></ol>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Use the interactive Subnet Calculator below to verify <code>192.168.10.75/26</code>, <code>10.0.5.130/25</code>, and <code>172.16.1.5/30</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"subnetcalc\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Assigning the <strong>network address</strong> (<code>.0</code>, <code>.64</code>, etc.) or <strong>broadcast address</strong> (<code>.255</code>, <code>.127</code>, etc.) to a PC. Packet Tracer and real OSes reject them!</li><li>Forgetting to subtract <strong>2</strong> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^h - 2\"></span> when asked for <strong>usable hosts</strong>.</li><li>Using <code>256 - mask</code> on the wrong octet when the prefix is <code>/16</code>–<code>/23</code> (block size applies to the <strong>3rd</strong> octet there, with <code>.0</code> in the 4th).</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>subnet mask</strong></td><td>a 32-bit mask separating the network bits (1s) from host bits (0s)</td><td lang=\"tr\">alt ağ maskesi</td></tr><tr><td><strong>CIDR</strong></td><td>Classless Inter-Domain Routing notation like <code>/26</code> counting network bits</td><td lang=\"tr\">CIDR gösterimi</td></tr><tr><td><strong>network address</strong></td><td>the first address in a subnet (host bits all 0), identifying the subnet</td><td lang=\"tr\">ağ adresi</td></tr><tr><td><strong>broadcast address</strong></td><td>the last address in a subnet (host bits all 1), reaching all hosts in the subnet</td><td lang=\"tr\">yayın adresi</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"How many <strong>usable host</strong> IP addresses are in a <code>/26</code> subnet?","opts":[{"h":"64","why":"64 is total addresses, before subtracting network and broadcast"},{"h":"62","ok":1,"why":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"32 - 26 = 6\"></span> host bits, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^6 - 2 = 62\"></span> usable hosts"},{"h":"30","why":"30 is for <code>/27</code>"},{"h":"126","why":"126 is for <code>/25</code>"}],"why":"A <code>/26</code> has 6 host bits: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^6 - 2 = 62\"></span> usable host addresses."},{"q":"What is the <strong>network address</strong> for host <code>192.168.1.140/25</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>192.168.1.0</code>","why":"<code>/25</code> splits the <code>/24</code> in half at <code>.128</code>"},{"h":"<code>192.168.1.128</code>","ok":1,"why":"block size is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"256 - 128 = 128\"></span>, and <code>140</code> is in the second half (<code>128..255</code>)"},{"h":"<code>192.168.1.129</code>","why":"<code>.129</code> is the first usable host, not the network address"},{"h":"<code>192.168.1.255</code>","why":"<code>.255</code> is the broadcast address"}],"why":"With <code>/25</code> (<code>255.255.255.128</code>), subnets are <code>.0–.127</code> and <code>.128–.255</code>; <code>140</code> belongs to <code>192.168.1.128</code>."},{"q":"Which CIDR prefix gives <strong>exactly 2 usable host</strong> addresses—ideal for a point-to-point link between two routers?","opts":[{"h":"<code>/28</code>","why":"<code>/28</code> gives 14 usable hosts"},{"h":"<code>/29</code>","why":"<code>/29</code> gives 6 usable hosts"},{"h":"<code>/30</code>","ok":1,"why":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"32 - 30 = 2\"></span> host bits, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^2 - 2 = 2\"></span> usable hosts"},{"h":"<code>/31</code>","why":"standard host subnetting formula gives 0 usable hosts for <code>/31</code>"}],"why":"<code>/30</code> (<code>255.255.255.252</code>) provides 2 usable host IPs."}]],"id":"net-12","title":"Subnetting basics and CIDR","goal":"calculate the network address, broadcast address, first/last usable host, and usable host count for an IPv4 CIDR subnet","week":"12","minutes":25,"terms":[{"t":"subnet mask","m":"a 32-bit mask separating the network bits (1s) from host bits (0s)","tr":"alt ağ maskesi"},{"t":"CIDR","m":"Classless Inter-Domain Routing notation like `/26` counting network bits","tr":"CIDR gösterimi"},{"t":"network address","m":"the first address in a subnet (host bits all 0), identifying the subnet","tr":"ağ adresi"},{"t":"broadcast address","m":"the last address in a subnet (host bits all 1), reaching all hosts in the subnet","tr":"yayın adresi"}]},"net-13":{"html":"<p>In exams and labs, you are often given a base network like <code>192.168.10.0/24</code> and asked to split it into subnets for different rooms—and then build it in Packet Tracer with a <strong>router</strong> connecting them.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-designing-subnets-from-host-requirements\"><h2 id=\"designing-subnets-from-host-requirements\">Designing subnets from host requirements</h2>\n<p>Suppose Lab A needs <strong>50 hosts</strong> and Lab B needs <strong>25 hosts</strong>, starting from <code>192.168.10.0/24</code>. Always allocate the <strong>largest</strong> requirement first (VLSM — Variable Length Subnet Masking):</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Lab A (needs 50 hosts)</strong>: - Smallest power of 2 with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^h - 2 \\ge 50\"></span>: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"h = 6\"></span> (<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^6 - 2 = 62\"></span>). - Prefix = <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"32 - 6 = \\mathbf{/26}\"></span> (<code>255.255.255.192</code>, block size 64). - Subnet: <code>192.168.10.0/26</code> (usable <code>.1</code> – <code>.62</code>, broadcast <code>.63</code>).</li><li><strong>Lab B (needs 25 hosts)</strong>: - Next free address is <code>.64</code>. - Smallest <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"h\"></span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^h - 2 \\ge 25\"></span>: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"h = 5\"></span> (<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^5 - 2 = 30\"></span>). - Prefix = <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"32 - 5 = \\mathbf{/27}\"></span> (<code>255.255.255.224</code>, block size 32). - Subnet: <code>192.168.10.64/27</code> (usable <code>.65</code> – <code>.94</code>, broadcast <code>.95</code>).</li></ol>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-guided-packet-tracer-lab-2-two-lans-router\"><h2 id=\"guided-packet-tracer-lab-2-two-lans-router\">Guided Packet Tracer Lab 2: Two LANs + Router</h2>\n<p>When two PCs are in <strong>different subnets</strong>, a switch alone cannot connect them. You need a <strong>router</strong>, and each PC must know its <strong>Default Gateway</strong> (the IP of the router interface on that PC's LAN).</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Interface</th><th>IPv4 Address</th><th>Subnet Mask</th><th>Default Gateway</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>Router0</code></td><td><code>GigabitEthernet0/0</code></td><td><code>192.168.10.1</code></td><td><code>255.255.255.192</code> (<code>/26</code>)</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>PC0</code> (Lab A)</td><td><code>FastEthernet0</code></td><td><code>192.168.10.10</code></td><td><code>255.255.255.192</code></td><td><code>192.168.10.1</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>Router0</code></td><td><code>GigabitEthernet0/1</code></td><td><code>192.168.10.65</code></td><td><code>255.255.255.224</code> (<code>/27</code>)</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>PC1</code> (Lab B)</td><td><code>FastEthernet0</code></td><td><code>192.168.10.70</code></td><td><code>255.255.255.224</code></td><td><code>192.168.10.65</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Lab steps</h3>\n<ol><li>Place <code>Router0</code> (<code>1941</code> or <code>2911</code>), <code>Switch0</code>, <code>Switch1</code>, <code>PC0</code>, and <code>PC1</code>.</li><li>Cable <code>PC0</code> ↔ <code>Switch0</code> ↔ <code>Router0 (Gig0/0)</code> using <strong>Copper Straight-Through</strong>. Cable <code>PC1</code> ↔ <code>Switch1</code> ↔ <code>Router0 (Gig0/1)</code> using <strong>Copper Straight-Through</strong>.</li><li>Configure <code>PC0</code> and <code>PC1</code> IP, Subnet Mask, and <strong>Default Gateway</strong> (Desktop → IP Configuration).</li><li>Click <code>Router0</code> → <strong>Config</strong> tab → <code>GigabitEthernet0/0</code>: enter IP <code>192.168.10.1</code>, Mask <code>255.255.255.192</code>, and tick <strong>Port Status: On</strong> (<code>no shutdown</code> in CLI).</li><li>Repeat for <code>GigabitEthernet0/1</code>: IP <code>192.168.10.65</code>, Mask <code>255.255.255.224</code>, tick <strong>Port Status: On</strong>.</li><li>Wait for green lights, then from <code>PC0</code> run <code>ping 192.168.10.70</code>. (The very first packet may say <code>Request timed out</code> while ARP runs—the next 3 should reply!)</li></ol>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Practice with the unlimited subnetting drill (aim for 10+ in a row without a calculator), and complete Lab 2:</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"subnetdrill\"></div>\n<div class=\"widget checklist\" data-widget=\"checklist\" data-id=\"net/13-subnetting-practice-lab.md#1\"><div class=\"checklist-h\">Guided Lab 2 completion checklist</div><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\" data-i=\"0\"><span>Built two LANs connected to <code>Router0</code> (<code>Gig0/0</code> and <code>Gig0/1</code>)</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\" data-i=\"1\"><span>Turned <strong>On</strong> (<code>no shutdown</code>) both router interfaces so all links are green</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\" data-i=\"2\"><span>Set each PC's <strong>Default Gateway</strong> to its local router interface IP</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\" data-i=\"3\"><span>Verified cross-subnet connectivity with <code>ping 192.168.10.70</code> from <code>PC0</code></span></label></li></ul></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Leaving router interfaces <strong>shut down</strong> (red triangles). Cisco router ports are <strong>administratively down by default</strong> until you turn Port Status On (<code>no shutdown</code>).</li><li>Forgetting the <strong>Default Gateway</strong> on <code>PC0</code> or <code>PC1</code>. Without it, <code>PC0</code> does not know where to send packets destined for another subnet.</li><li>Using <code>192.168.10.63</code> as a host IP in Lab A—<code>.63</code> is Lab A's broadcast address!</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>default gateway</strong></td><td>the router interface IP on a local LAN where hosts send packets bound for other networks</td><td lang=\"tr\">varsayılan ağ geçidi</td></tr><tr><td><strong>VLSM (Variable Length Subnet Mask)</strong></td><td>dividing a network into subnets of different sizes to match actual host counts</td><td lang=\"tr\">değişken uzunluklu alt ağ maskesi</td></tr><tr><td><strong><code>no shutdown</code></strong></td><td>Cisco IOS command that enables (turns on) a router interface</td><td lang=\"tr\">arayüzü etkinleştirme komutu</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"You need a subnet for <strong>12 hosts</strong>. Which CIDR prefix wastes the fewest IP addresses?","opts":[{"h":"<code>/29</code>","why":"<code>/29</code> only gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^3 - 2 = 6\"></span> usable hosts (too small!)"},{"h":"<code>/28</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>/28</code> gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^4 - 2 = 14\"></span> usable hosts (enough for 12)"},{"h":"<code>/27</code>","why":"<code>/27</code> gives 30 hosts, wasting 16 extra addresses"},{"h":"<code>/26</code>","why":"<code>/26</code> gives 62 hosts, wasting 50 addresses"}],"why":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^4 - 2 = 14 \\ge 12\"></span>, so <code>/28</code> (<code>255.255.255.240</code>) is the smallest fit."},{"q":"In Guided Lab 2, <code>PC0</code> (<code>192.168.10.10/26</code>) can ping its gateway <code>192.168.10.1</code>, and <code>Router0</code> links are all green, but <code>ping 192.168.10.70</code> fails. What is the most likely missing setting?","opts":[{"h":"<code>Switch0</code> needs an IP address","why":"Layer 2 switches forward frames without needing an IP to pass traffic"},{"h":"<code>PC1</code> is missing its Default Gateway (<code>192.168.10.65</code>) or has the wrong subnet mask","ok":1,"why":"the reply packet cannot return to <code>PC0</code>'s subnet without <code>PC1</code>'s gateway"},{"h":"A crossover cable is needed between <code>Switch0</code> and <code>Router0</code>","why":"switch to router uses straight-through"},{"h":"DNS is not configured","why":"<code>ping</code> to an IP address does not use DNS"}],"why":"Both sides need the correct Default Gateway so both the request and the reply can cross the router."},{"q":"Why do Cisco router ports start with <strong>red</strong> link triangles when first cabled in Packet Tracer?","opts":[{"h":"The cable is broken","why":"straight-through cables work fine between router and switch"},{"h":"Router interfaces are shut down by default and must be enabled (<code>Port Status: On</code> / <code>no shutdown</code>)","ok":1,"why":"switches start enabled, routers start disabled"},{"h":"Routers only support fiber cables","why":"routers have GigabitEthernet copper ports"},{"h":"You must restart Packet Tracer","why":"just turn Port Status On"}],"why":"Unlike switch ports, Cisco router interfaces are administratively disabled until turned on."}]],"id":"net-13","title":"Subnetting practice and Guided lab 2 (Two LANs + Router)","goal":"design subnets for a host requirement, run the unlimited subnetting drill, and route between two LANs in Packet Tracer","week":"12","minutes":25,"terms":[{"t":"default gateway","m":"the router interface IP on a local LAN where hosts send packets bound for other networks","tr":"varsayılan ağ geçidi"},{"t":"VLSM (Variable Length Subnet Mask)","m":"dividing a network into subnets of different sizes to match actual host counts","tr":"değişken uzunluklu alt ağ maskesi"},{"t":"`no shutdown`","m":"Cisco IOS command that enables (turns on) a router interface","tr":"arayüzü etkinleştirme komutu"}]},"net-14":{"html":"<p>When a network stops working, engineers test from bottom to top using built-in command-line tools.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-the-6-commands-you-will-use-most\"><h2 id=\"the-6-commands-you-will-use-most\">The 6 commands you will use most</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Command (Windows / Linux-macOS)</th><th>What it shows or tests</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>ipconfig</code> / <code>ip a</code> (<code>ifconfig</code>)</td><td>Shows your IP address, subnet mask, and default gateway (<code>ipconfig /all</code> adds MAC &amp; DNS)</td></tr><tr><td><code>ping &lt;host&gt;</code></td><td>Tests Layer 3 reachability and round-trip time (ms) using <strong>ICMP</strong></td></tr><tr><td><code>tracert &lt;host&gt;</code> / <code>traceroute &lt;host&gt;</code></td><td>Lists every <strong>router hop</strong> between you and the destination</td></tr><tr><td><code>arp -a</code></td><td>Displays the local <strong>IP-to-MAC</strong> address cache</td></tr><tr><td><code>nslookup &lt;domain&gt;</code></td><td>Queries your <strong>DNS server</strong> to translate a domain name (e.g., <code>igu.edu.tr</code>) into an IP</td></tr><tr><td><code>netstat -an</code></td><td>Lists active <strong>TCP/UDP connections</strong> and <strong>listening ports</strong> on your machine</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Two more small commands</h3>\n<ul><li><strong><code>hostname</code></strong> — prints the computer's name on the network (e.g. <code>LAB-PC-07</code>). Useful before you ask someone to ping you, or to check which machine you are logged in to remotely.</li><li><strong><code>ipconfig /release</code></strong> and <strong><code>ipconfig /renew</code></strong> — give back the current DHCP address and ask the DHCP server for a new one. <code>ipconfig /flushdns</code> clears the DNS cache. On Linux/macOS the equivalents are <code>ip addr</code> / <code>ifconfig</code>, <code>dhclient</code>.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-step-by-step-troubleshooting-flow\"><h2 id=\"step-by-step-troubleshooting-flow\">Step-by-step troubleshooting flow</h2>\n<p>If a user says <em>\"I can't open websites!\"</em>, test in this order:</p>\n<ol><li><code>ipconfig</code> → Do I have a valid IP (not <code>169.254.x.x</code> APIPA) and a Default Gateway?</li><li><code>ping 127.0.0.1</code> → Is my own TCP/IP software stack working?</li><li><code>ping &lt;Default Gateway&gt;</code> → Can I reach my local router? (If no, check cable/Wi-Fi!)</li><li><code>ping 8.8.8.8</code> → Can I reach the internet by IP?</li><li><code>ping google.com</code> or <code>nslookup google.com</code> → If <code>8.8.8.8</code> works by IP but <code>google.com</code> fails, the problem is <strong>DNS</strong>!</li></ol>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>A student runs two commands on a lab PC:</p>\n<ul><li><code>ping 8.8.8.8</code> → <strong>4 replies, 0% loss</strong></li><li><code>nslookup igu.edu.tr</code> → <strong>DNS request timed out</strong></li></ul>\n<p>What is working, and what single service is broken?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><ul><li><strong>Working</strong>: Physical cable/Wi-Fi, local IP/subnet mask, Default Gateway, and internet routing (proved by <code>8.8.8.8</code> replying).</li><li><strong>Broken</strong>: <strong>DNS</strong> (name resolution)—the PC can reach public IPs, so either the configured DNS server IP is wrong/down or UDP port 53 is blocked.</li></ul></div></details>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Thinking <code>ping</code> failing means the server is definitely off. Many servers and firewalls <strong>block ICMP (<code>ping</code>)</strong> for security even while HTTP (<code>443</code>) works.</li><li>Running <code>ipconfig</code> without <code>/all</code> when looking for the <strong>MAC (Physical) Address</strong> or <strong>DNS Server</strong>—you need <code>ipconfig /all</code> on Windows.</li><li>Confusing <code>netstat</code> (local open sockets/ports) with <code>tracert</code> (path of routers across the network).</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong><code>ipconfig</code></strong></td><td>Windows command displaying IPv4/IPv6, subnet mask, and gateway settings</td><td lang=\"tr\">ipconfig komutu</td></tr><tr><td><strong><code>tracert</code> / <code>traceroute</code></strong></td><td>command tracing each router hop along the path to a destination</td><td lang=\"tr\">yol izleme komutu</td></tr><tr><td><strong><code>nslookup</code></strong></td><td>command querying DNS servers to resolve domain names to IP addresses</td><td lang=\"tr\">DNS sorgulama komutu</td></tr><tr><td><strong><code>netstat</code></strong></td><td>command listing active network connections and listening ports</td><td lang=\"tr\">ağ istatistikleri komutu</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"You can <code>ping 1.1.1.1</code> successfully, but opening <code>https://gelisim.edu.tr</code> fails and <code>nslookup gelisim.edu.tr</code> times out. Where is the fault?","opts":[{"h":"Your Ethernet cable is unplugged","why":"<code>1.1.1.1</code> replied, so the link is up"},{"h":"Your Default Gateway is wrong","why":"traffic reached <code>1.1.1.1</code> through the gateway"},{"h":"DNS resolution is failing","ok":1,"why":"IP connectivity works, but domain-to-IP lookup is broken"},{"h":"Your NIC is damaged","why":"hardware is working fine"}],"why":"Reaching an IP directly while domain names fail points directly to DNS."},{"q":"Which command shows the <strong>path of routers (hops)</strong> packets take to reach <code>example.com</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>arp -a</code>","why":"<code>arp -a</code> only shows the local LAN IP-to-MAC table"},{"h":"<code>tracert example.com</code> (or <code>traceroute</code>)","ok":1,"why":"lists each router hop and latency along the path"},{"h":"<code>ipconfig /all</code>","why":"shows local interface configuration only"},{"h":"<code>netstat -an</code>","why":"shows local sockets and listening ports"}],"why":"<code>tracert</code> (Windows) / <code>traceroute</code> (Linux/macOS) reveals every router hop to the target."},{"q":"Which command displays your PC's <strong>MAC (Physical) address</strong> and <strong>DNS servers</strong> on Windows?","opts":[{"h":"<code>ping /all</code>","why":"<code>ping</code> has no <code>/all</code> option for NIC config"},{"h":"<code>ipconfig /all</code>","ok":1,"why":"displays MAC address, DHCP status, IP, mask, gateway, and DNS servers"},{"h":"<code>arp -a</code>","why":"shows other hosts' cached MACs, not your own DNS server"},{"h":"<code>tracert</code>","why":"traces remote router hops"}],"why":"<code>ipconfig /all</code> shows complete adapter details including Physical Address and DNS servers."}]],"id":"net-14","title":"Essential network diagnostic commands","goal":"use ipconfig/ifconfig, ping, tracert/traceroute, arp, nslookup, and netstat to diagnose connectivity problems","week":"13","minutes":16,"terms":[{"t":"`ipconfig`","m":"Windows command displaying IPv4/IPv6, subnet mask, and gateway settings","tr":"ipconfig komutu"},{"t":"`tracert` / `traceroute`","m":"command tracing each router hop along the path to a destination","tr":"yol izleme komutu"},{"t":"`nslookup`","m":"command querying DNS servers to resolve domain names to IP addresses","tr":"DNS sorgulama komutu"},{"t":"`netstat`","m":"command listing active network connections and listening ports","tr":"ağ istatistikleri komutu"}]},"net-15":{"html":"<p>Wireless LANs (<strong>WLANs</strong>) follow the <strong>IEEE 802.11</strong> family of standards—commonly called <strong>Wi-Fi</strong>. Instead of copper wires, devices share radio frequencies using <strong>CSMA/CA</strong>.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-wi-fi-standards-at-a-glance\"><h2 id=\"wi-fi-standards-at-a-glance\">Wi-Fi standards at a glance</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>IEEE Standard</th><th>Wi-Fi Name</th><th>Band(s)</th><th>Typical max speed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>802.11n</strong></td><td>Wi-Fi 4</td><td>2.4 GHz &amp; 5 GHz</td><td>~600 Mbps</td></tr><tr><td><strong>802.11ac</strong></td><td>Wi-Fi 5</td><td>5 GHz only</td><td>~1.3–3.5 Gbps</td></tr><tr><td><strong>802.11ax</strong></td><td>Wi-Fi 6 / 6E</td><td>2.4, 5 (&amp; 6 GHz)</td><td>Up to 9.6 Gbps, better in crowded rooms</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-2-4-ghz-vs-5-ghz\"><h2 id=\"2-4-ghz-vs-5-ghz\">2.4 GHz vs. 5 GHz</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>2.4 GHz</strong>: travels <strong>farther</strong> and passes through walls better, but is <strong>slower</strong> and crowded (shared with Bluetooth, microwaves, and neighbors). Only <strong>3 non-overlapping channels</strong> exist: <strong>1, 6, and 11</strong>!</li><li><strong>5 GHz</strong>: <strong>faster</strong> speeds and many more non-overlapping channels (less interference), but <strong>shorter range</strong> and weakens faster through concrete walls.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-wireless-security-ssid\"><h2 id=\"wireless-security-ssid\">Wireless security &amp; SSID</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>SSID (Service Set Identifier)</strong>: the human-readable Wi-Fi network name (e.g., <code>IGU-Student</code>). Hiding the SSID is <em>not</em> real security—sniffers still see it in frames.</li><li><strong>WEP</strong> (broken, obsolete) → <strong>WPA</strong> (deprecated) → <strong>WPA2 (AES)</strong> (standard) → <strong>WPA3</strong> (strongest modern standard, resists offline password guessing).</li><li><strong>MAC filtering</strong>: allows/blocks listed MAC addresses—helpful as a small extra layer, but easy to spoof, so never use it instead of WPA2/WPA3.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>You are placing Wi-Fi access points in a crowded 3-classroom hallway using <strong>2.4 GHz</strong> so students' laptops stay connected through thick walls.</p>\n<ol><li>Which three channels should you assign to the three neighboring APs so they do not interfere with each other?</li><li>Which security protocol should you enable: WEP, WPA2-AES, or open?</li></ol>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><ol><li>Channels <strong>1, 6, and 11</strong> (the three non-overlapping 2.4 GHz channels).</li><li><strong>WPA2-AES</strong> (or <strong>WPA3</strong> if all client devices support it). Never use WEP or open for private/campus access.</li></ol></div></details>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Setting neighboring 2.4 GHz access points to channels <code>1, 2, 3</code>. Adjacent channels overlap and cause severe interference—always use <strong>1, 6, 11</strong>.</li><li>Thinking <strong>5 GHz</strong> always has better range than <strong>2.4 GHz</strong>. Higher frequency = faster data, <strong>shorter</strong> wall-penetrating range.</li><li>Relying on <strong>hidden SSID</strong> or <strong>MAC filtering</strong> without WPA2/WPA3 encryption.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>IEEE 802.11</strong></td><td>the family of standards defining wireless LANs (Wi-Fi)</td><td lang=\"tr\">IEEE 802.11 standardı</td></tr><tr><td><strong>SSID</strong></td><td>the broadcast name of a wireless network</td><td lang=\"tr\">kablosuz ağ adı (SSID)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>non-overlapping channels</strong></td><td>channels 1, 6, and 11 in 2.4 GHz that do not interfere with each other</td><td lang=\"tr\">çakışmayan kanallar</td></tr><tr><td><strong>WPA2 / WPA3</strong></td><td>modern Wi-Fi security protocols using strong AES encryption</td><td lang=\"tr\">kablosuz güvenlik protokolleri</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"Which three channels are <strong>non-overlapping</strong> in the <strong>2.4 GHz</strong> Wi-Fi band?","opts":[{"h":"1, 2, 3","why":"adjacent channels overlap heavily and cause interference"},{"h":"1, 6, 11","ok":1,"why":"spaced 5 channels apart so their 20–22 MHz widths do not overlap"},{"h":"2, 5, 8","why":"spaced only 3 channels apart, so they overlap"},{"h":"36, 40, 44","why":"those are 5 GHz channels"}],"why":"In 2.4 GHz, only channels 1, 6, and 11 do not overlap."},{"q":"Which statement accurately compares <strong>2.4 GHz</strong> and <strong>5 GHz</strong> Wi-Fi?","opts":[{"h":"5 GHz travels farther through thick walls than 2.4 GHz","why":"2.4 GHz penetrates walls better"},{"h":"5 GHz offers higher speeds and less congestion, while 2.4 GHz has longer range","ok":1,"why":"trade-off between speed/channels and range"},{"h":"2.4 GHz is used only by 802.11ac","why":"802.11ac is 5 GHz only"},{"h":"5 GHz has only 3 channels","why":"5 GHz has over 20 non-overlapping channels"}],"why":"2.4 GHz reaches farther; 5 GHz is faster and cleaner at shorter distances."},{"q":"Which Wi-Fi security standard should you <strong>never</strong> use today because it can be cracked in minutes?","opts":[{"h":"WPA3","why":"WPA3 is the newest and strongest standard"},{"h":"WPA2 (AES)","why":"WPA2 with AES and a strong passphrase is still widely secure"},{"h":"WEP","ok":1,"why":"WEP has fatal cryptographic flaws and is completely broken"},{"h":"802.1X","why":"802.1X is enterprise authentication"}],"why":"WEP is obsolete and easily cracked; always use WPA2-AES or WPA3."}]],"id":"net-15","title":"Wireless networking (Wi-Fi)","goal":"compare Wi-Fi standards (802.11n/ac/ax), 2.4 GHz vs. 5 GHz bands, non-overlapping channels, and WPA2/WPA3 security","week":"14","minutes":15,"terms":[{"t":"IEEE 802.11","m":"the family of standards defining wireless LANs (Wi-Fi)","tr":"IEEE 802.11 standardı"},{"t":"SSID","m":"the broadcast name of a wireless network","tr":"kablosuz ağ adı (SSID)"},{"t":"non-overlapping channels","m":"channels 1, 6, and 11 in 2.4 GHz that do not interfere with each other","tr":"çakışmayan kanallar"},{"t":"WPA2 / WPA3","m":"modern Wi-Fi security protocols using strong AES encryption","tr":"kablosuz güvenlik protokolleri"}]},"net-16":{"html":"<p>Connecting a network to the internet exposes it to attackers. <strong>Network security</strong> aims to keep systems working and data safe using three core goals called the <strong>CIA triad</strong>.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-the-cia-triad\"><h2 id=\"the-cia-triad\">The CIA triad</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Confidentiality</strong>: only authorized people can read the data (protected by <strong>encryption</strong> like HTTPS/TLS and VPNs).</li><li><strong>Integrity</strong>: data is not altered in transit (protected by <strong>hashes</strong> and digital signatures).</li><li><strong>Availability</strong>: authorized users can access services when needed (protected by backups, redundancy, and <strong>DoS</strong> defense).</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-network-threats\"><h2 id=\"common-network-threats\">Common network threats</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Threat</th><th>What happens</th><th>Defense</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Phishing</strong></td><td>Fake email/website tricks a user into typing a password</td><td>User awareness, MFA, email filters</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Sniffing / Eavesdropping</strong></td><td>Attacker captures unencrypted packets on Wi-Fi or a shared LAN</td><td>Encrypt traffic (<strong>HTTPS</strong>, <strong>SSH</strong>, <strong>VPN</strong>)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Man-in-the-Middle (MITM)</strong></td><td>Attacker secretly intercepts and alters traffic between two hosts</td><td>TLS certificates, WPA2/WPA3, ARP inspection</td></tr><tr><td><strong>DoS / DDoS</strong></td><td>Floods a server with fake traffic so real users cannot connect</td><td>Rate limiting, DDoS scrubbing, firewalls</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-firewalls-acls-and-vpns\"><h2 id=\"firewalls-acls-and-vpns\">Firewalls, ACLs, and VPNs</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Firewall</strong>: inspects traffic entering or leaving a network and allows or blocks it based on rules (IP address, port number, protocol). - <strong>Packet-filtering (stateless)</strong>: checks each packet's header against rules one by one. - <strong>Stateful firewall</strong>: remembers active connections (so it automatically allows replies to traffic you started).</li><li><strong>ACL (Access Control List)</strong>: ordered list of <code>permit</code> / <code>deny</code> rules applied to a router interface. Rules are checked <strong>top to bottom</strong>—first match wins!</li><li><strong>VPN (Virtual Private Network)</strong>: creates an <strong>encrypted tunnel</strong> over the public internet so remote workers or students can safely reach an internal campus/company network.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>A router ACL has these rules in order:</p>\n<ol><li><code>deny tcp any any eq 23</code> (Telnet)</li><li><code>permit tcp 192.168.1.0 0.0.0.255 any eq 80</code> (HTTP)</li><li><code>deny ip any any</code> (implicit/explicit deny all)</li></ol>\n<p>What happens to:</p>\n<ul><li>A Telnet packet (port 23) from <code>192.168.1.10</code>?</li><li>An HTTPS packet (port 443) from <code>192.168.1.10</code>?</li></ul>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><ul><li><strong>Telnet (port 23)</strong>: matches Rule 1 → <strong>Denied</strong> (Telnet is unencrypted, so blocking it is good practice).</li><li><strong>HTTPS (port 443)</strong>: does not match Rule 1 or Rule 2 (port 80) → hits Rule 3 → <strong>Denied</strong>. (If you want HTTPS allowed, you must add a <code>permit ... eq 443</code> rule before <code>deny ip any any</code>!)</li></ul></div></details>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Putting <code>deny ip any any</code> <strong>at the top</strong> of an ACL—because ACLs stop at the <strong>first match</strong>, nothing below it will ever run!</li><li>Confusing <strong>encryption</strong> (protects <strong>Confidentiality</strong>) with <strong>backups/redundancy</strong> (protects <strong>Availability</strong>).</li><li>Using <strong>Telnet (port 23)</strong> or <strong>HTTP (port 80)</strong> for admin logins—anyone sniffing the LAN can read your password in plain text; use <strong>SSH (22)</strong> and <strong>HTTPS (443)</strong>.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>CIA triad</strong></td><td>Confidentiality, Integrity, Availability — the three core goals of security</td><td lang=\"tr\">CIA üçlüsü</td></tr><tr><td><strong>firewall</strong></td><td>a security device or software that filters network traffic using rules</td><td lang=\"tr\">güvenlik duvarı</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ACL (Access Control List)</strong></td><td>an ordered list of permit/deny rules on a router interface</td><td lang=\"tr\">erişim denetim listesi</td></tr><tr><td><strong>VPN (Virtual Private Network)</strong></td><td>an encrypted tunnel carrying private traffic safely across a public network</td><td lang=\"tr\">sanal özel ağ (VPN)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>DDoS</strong></td><td>Distributed Denial of Service — flooding a target from many machines to knock it offline</td><td lang=\"tr\">dağıtık hizmet reddi saldırısı</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"Which part of the <strong>CIA triad</strong> is protected when you switch from HTTP to <strong>HTTPS</strong> so attackers on public Wi-Fi cannot read your password?","opts":[{"h":"Confidentiality","ok":1,"why":"encryption keeps data secret from unauthorized eavesdroppers"},{"h":"Availability","why":"availability keeps services online"},{"h":"Non-repudiation","why":"non-repudiation is not one of the three CIA letters"},{"h":"Redundancy","why":"redundancy is for availability, not secrecy"}],"why":"Encryption protects Confidentiality."},{"q":"How does a router process rules in an <strong>ACL (Access Control List)</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"Bottom to top, applying the most specific rule","why":"ACLs are strictly top-down"},{"h":"Top to bottom, stopping at the first matching rule","ok":1,"why":"order matters critically in ACLs"},{"h":"Randomly, to balance CPU load","why":"ACL evaluation is deterministic"},{"h":"Only on UDP packets","why":"ACLs filter IP, TCP, UDP, and ICMP"}],"why":"ACLs evaluate rules sequentially from top to bottom and act on the first match."},{"q":"What is the main purpose of a <strong>VPN</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"To increase your ISP's physical cable speed","why":"encryption adds slight overhead rather than raw speed"},{"h":"To create an encrypted tunnel over an untrusted public network","ok":1,"why":"keeps remote traffic private and authenticated"},{"h":"To replace MAC addresses with domain names","why":"that is unrelated to VPNs"},{"h":"To disable firewalls on your laptop","why":"VPNs complement firewalls"}],"why":"A VPN builds an encrypted tunnel across public networks like the internet."}]],"id":"net-16","title":"Network security, firewalls, and VPNs","goal":"explain the CIA triad, recognize common network attacks, and describe how firewalls, ACLs, and VPNs protect a network","week":"15","minutes":18,"terms":[{"t":"CIA triad","m":"Confidentiality, Integrity, Availability — the three core goals of security","tr":"CIA üçlüsü"},{"t":"firewall","m":"a security device or software that filters network traffic using rules","tr":"güvenlik duvarı"},{"t":"ACL (Access Control List)","m":"an ordered list of permit/deny rules on a router interface","tr":"erişim denetim listesi"},{"t":"VPN (Virtual Private Network)","m":"an encrypted tunnel carrying private traffic safely across a public network","tr":"sanal özel ağ (VPN)"},{"t":"DDoS","m":"Distributed Denial of Service — flooding a target from many machines to knock it offline","tr":"dağıtık hizmet reddi saldırısı"}]}},"bank":[{"q":"Which four elements are required for every computer network?","opts":[{"h":"Only routers and web browsers","why":"networks also need hosts, media, and protocols"},{"h":"End devices, intermediary devices, transmission media, and protocols","ok":1,"why":"all four work together to move data"},{"h":"Only Wi-Fi and fiber cables","why":"copper and protocols are omitted"},{"h":"A web server and an email server","why":"P2P networks do not need dedicated servers"}],"why":"Every network combines hosts, intermediary devices, media, and shared protocols.","tag":"01"},{"q":"In a peer-to-peer (P2P) network, which statement is true?","opts":[{"h":"One central server controls all logins","why":"that describes client-server"},{"h":"Every device can act as both a client and a server","ok":1,"why":"peers share directly without a dedicated server"},{"h":"Devices must connect with fiber-optic cables","why":"P2P works over any medium"},{"h":"It only works across different countries","why":"P2P works on small local LANs as well"}],"why":"P2P networks have no dedicated central server; each peer can request and provide resources.","tag":"01"},{"q":"Which 1969 project introduced packet switching and became the ancestor of the modern internet?","opts":[{"h":"World Wide Web","why":"Tim Berners-Lee proposed the Web in 1989"},{"h":"ARPANET","ok":1,"why":"ARPANET connected four university computers using packet switching"},{"h":"Ethernet 802.11","why":"802.11 is Wi-Fi"},{"h":"Bluetooth SIG","why":"Bluetooth is a short-range PAN standard"}],"why":"ARPANET (1969) pioneered packet switching; TCP/IP became its standard in 1983.","tag":"01"},{"q":"What is a network <strong>protocol</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"A physical copper cable","why":"cables are transmission media"},{"h":"An agreed set of rules for formatting and exchanging data","ok":1,"why":"both sender and receiver follow the same rules"},{"h":"A brand of network switch","why":"vendors build hardware that implements protocols"},{"h":"A type of computer monitor","why":"monitors are output hardware"}],"why":"Protocols (like IP, TCP, HTTP) define the format, timing, and rules of communication.","tag":"01"},{"q":"Which statement accurately separates the <strong>internet</strong> from the <strong>World Wide Web</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"They are identical terms","why":"the Web is one service on top of the internet"},{"h":"The internet is the global network of networks; the Web is HTTP/HTML pages running on it","ok":1,"why":"email and SSH also use the internet without using the Web"},{"h":"The Web came first in 1969","why":"ARPANET came in 1969; the Web came in 1989"},{"h":"The internet is only software in your browser","why":"the internet includes physical routers, cables, and ISPs"}],"why":"The internet is the infrastructure; the Web is an application running on top of it.","tag":"01"},{"q":"What is the main advantage of a <strong>client-server</strong> network over a peer-to-peer network?","opts":[{"h":"No cables or Wi-Fi are needed","why":"physical or wireless media are still required"},{"h":"Centralized security, permissions, and backups are easier to manage","ok":1,"why":"servers store data in one controlled place"},{"h":"It never needs IP addresses","why":"IP is still required on TCP/IP networks"},{"h":"Every user's laptop must stay turned on 24/7","why":"only the server needs to stay online"}],"why":"Client-server centralizes administration, backups, and security policies.","tag":"01"},{"q":"In what year did ARPANET adopt <strong>TCP/IP</strong> as its standard protocol suite?","opts":[{"h":"1969","why":"1969 was the first ARPANET link"},{"h":"1983","ok":1,"why":"January 1, 1983 is known as the birth of the modern TCP/IP internet"},{"h":"1995","why":"commercial ISPs grew in the mid-1990s"},{"h":"2007","why":"2007 was the modern smartphone era"}],"why":"ARPANET switched to TCP/IP in 1983.","tag":"01"},{"q":"When your browser loads <code>https://gelisim.edu.tr</code>, what role does your laptop play?","opts":[{"h":"Intermediary router","why":"routers forward packets between networks"},{"h":"Client","ok":1,"why":"the browser requests the web page from the server"},{"h":"Server","why":"the university web host is the server"},{"h":"Transmission medium","why":"Wi-Fi or Ethernet is the medium"}],"why":"The requesting device/application is the client.","tag":"01"},{"q":"Which network type covers a small personal space of 1–10 meters (e.g., Bluetooth earbuds to phone)?","opts":[{"h":"PAN","ok":1,"why":"Personal Area Network"},{"h":"LAN","why":"Local Area Network covers a room or building"},{"h":"MAN","why":"Metropolitan Area Network covers a city"},{"h":"WAN","why":"Wide Area Network spans large regions"}],"why":"PAN covers personal devices within a few meters.","tag":"02"},{"q":"A company connects branch offices in Istanbul, Ankara, and Izmir using leased ISP lines. What type of network is this?","opts":[{"h":"PAN","why":"only a few meters"},{"h":"LAN","why":"limited to one building/campus"},{"h":"MAN","why":"limited to one city"},{"h":"WAN","ok":1,"why":"spans multiple cities across the country"}],"why":"Networks spanning cities or countries are Wide Area Networks (WANs).","tag":"02"},{"q":"In which topology does every device connect to a single central switch?","opts":[{"h":"Bus","why":"devices tap into one shared backbone cable"},{"h":"Star","ok":1,"why":"every host has its own cable to a central switch"},{"h":"Ring","why":"devices form a closed point-to-point loop"},{"h":"Full mesh","why":"every device connects directly to every other device"}],"why":"Star topology is the standard layout for modern Ethernet LANs.","tag":"02"},{"q":"Which topology provides the highest fault tolerance because every node connects directly to every other node?","opts":[{"h":"Bus","why":"one backbone break stops the bus"},{"h":"Star","why":"if the central switch fails, the star stops"},{"h":"Ring","why":"a break can disrupt a simple ring"},{"h":"Full mesh","ok":1,"why":"multiple redundant paths exist between every pair of nodes"}],"why":"Full mesh has <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"n(n-1)/2\"></span> links and survives link failures easily.","tag":"02"},{"q":"What is the difference between <strong>physical</strong> and <strong>logical</strong> topology?","opts":[{"h":"Physical means wireless; logical means wired","why":"both can be wired or wireless"},{"h":"Physical is how cables and devices are arranged; logical is the path data actually follows","ok":1,"why":"for example, physically star-wired Ethernet can behave differently logically"},{"h":"Logical topology cannot exist without a router","why":"LANs have logical topologies too"},{"h":"Physical topology only applies to WANs","why":"physical topology applies to every network"}],"why":"Physical = layout of hardware/cables; logical = data flow path.","tag":"02"},{"q":"A 5-floor campus building connects each floor's star-wired PCs to a floor switch, and all floor switches to a core switch. Which topology is this?","opts":[{"h":"Bus","why":"no shared coaxial backbone"},{"h":"Ring","why":"switches are not in a closed loop"},{"h":"Tree (hierarchical)","ok":1,"why":"multiple star switches connected to a root/core switch"},{"h":"PAN","why":"a 5-floor building is a LAN, not a PAN"}],"why":"Linking star networks to a central root switch forms a tree (hierarchical) topology.","tag":"02"},{"q":"Why are <strong>terminators</strong> required at both ends of a classic bus topology?","opts":[{"h":"To assign IP addresses","why":"DHCP assigns IP addresses"},{"h":"To absorb signals so they do not bounce back and cause interference","ok":1,"why":"un-terminated ends reflect signals"},{"h":"To convert copper to fiber","why":"terminators are simple resistors"},{"h":"To encrypt frames","why":"encryption is done in software/protocols"}],"why":"Bus terminators absorb electrical signals at the cable ends.","tag":"02"},{"q":"What happens in a <strong>star</strong> topology if the cable to <code>PC-4</code> is cut?","opts":[{"h":"The entire LAN stops working","why":"that happens in a bus, not a star"},{"h":"Only <code>PC-4</code> loses connectivity; all other PCs keep working","ok":1,"why":"each host has an independent link to the switch"},{"h":"All packets reverse direction","why":"that describes a dual ring"},{"h":"The switch turns into a router","why":"the switch keeps operating normally"}],"why":"Star topology isolates single-cable faults to one host.","tag":"02"},{"q":"Which device operates at Layer 2 and forwards frames only to the port matching the destination MAC address?","opts":[{"h":"Hub","why":"Layer 1 hub floods signals to all ports"},{"h":"Switch","ok":1,"why":"Layer 2 switch maintains a MAC address table"},{"h":"Repeater","why":"Layer 1 repeater only boosts signals"},{"h":"Modem","why":"converts ISP line signals"}],"why":"Switches learn source MACs and forward frames selectively to the destination port.","tag":"03"},{"q":"Why did <strong>switches</strong> replace <strong>hubs</strong> in modern Ethernet LANs?","opts":[{"h":"Hubs cannot connect more than 2 PCs","why":"hubs came in 4, 8, or 24 ports"},{"h":"Hubs flood every signal to all ports (shared collision domain), whereas switches give each port its own collision domain","ok":1,"why":"switches improve speed and security"},{"h":"Hubs only work with fiber","why":"hubs were mostly copper UTP"},{"h":"Switches automatically assign public IPv4 addresses","why":"DHCP servers assign IPs"}],"why":"Switches eliminate shared collision domains by isolating traffic per port.","tag":"03"},{"q":"Which device is required to route packets <strong>between</strong> <code>192.168.1.0/24</code> and <code>192.168.2.0/24</code>?","opts":[{"h":"Hub","why":"Layer 1 only"},{"h":"Unmanaged Layer 2 switch","why":"stays within one broadcast domain/subnet"},{"h":"Router","ok":1,"why":"Layer 3 device that forwards packets across different IP networks"},{"h":"Access Point","why":"Layer 2 wireless bridge"}],"why":"Routers connect distinct IP networks at Layer 3.","tag":"03"},{"q":"What is a <strong>collision domain</strong> on a 24-port Ethernet switch?","opts":[{"h":"All 24 ports together","why":"that is true for a hub, not a switch"},{"h":"Each individual switch port is its own collision domain","ok":1,"why":"full-duplex links on a switch do not collide"},{"h":"The entire internet","why":"routers and switches separate collision domains"},{"h":"Only the uplink port","why":"every port is separate"}],"why":"Each port on a Layer 2 switch is a separate collision domain, while all ports (in one VLAN) share one broadcast domain.","tag":"03"},{"q":"What is a <strong>broadcast domain</strong> bounded by?","opts":[{"h":"A hub","why":"hubs forward broadcasts to all ports"},{"h":"A repeater","why":"repeaters forward everything"},{"h":"A router","ok":1,"why":"routers do not forward Layer 2 broadcasts between interfaces"},{"h":"A patch panel","why":"patch panels are passive wiring"}],"why":"Routers separate broadcast domains; switches forward broadcasts within a LAN.","tag":"03"},{"q":"What is the role of a <strong>NIC (Network Interface Card)</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"To route packets across the internet","why":"routers do that"},{"h":"To physically connect a host to the network medium and provide a MAC address","ok":1,"why":"every wired/wireless host needs a NIC"},{"h":"To amplify ISP fiber signals across cities","why":"repeaters/amplifiers do that"},{"h":"To store DNS records for a domain","why":"DNS servers do that"}],"why":"A NIC connects a computer to wired or wireless media and holds a burned-in MAC address.","tag":"03"},{"q":"Which device converts analog/line signals from a phone or cable ISP line into digital data for your home router?","opts":[{"h":"Switch","why":"connects local LAN devices"},{"h":"Modem","ok":1,"why":"modulates and demodulates ISP line signals"},{"h":"Hub","why":"repeats local Ethernet signals"},{"h":"Access Point","why":"provides Wi-Fi inside the LAN"}],"why":"A modem translates between the ISP's physical line signaling and digital Ethernet frames.","tag":"03"},{"q":"A wireless <strong>Access Point (AP)</strong> primarily operates at which OSI layer?","opts":[{"h":"Layer 1 only","why":"it understands Wi-Fi and Ethernet MAC frames"},{"h":"Layer 2 (Data Link)","ok":1,"why":"bridges 802.11 wireless frames to 802.3 Ethernet frames"},{"h":"Layer 4 (Transport)","why":"does not manage TCP ports"},{"h":"Layer 7 (Application)","why":"does not process HTTP/SMTP"}],"why":"An AP bridges wireless clients onto a wired LAN at Layer 2.","tag":"03"},{"q":"What is the maximum cable length for a standard Cat5e/Cat6 Ethernet UTP run?","opts":[{"h":"25 meters","why":"too short"},{"h":"50 meters","why":"too short"},{"h":"100 meters","ok":1,"why":"100 m is the standard Ethernet UTP limit"},{"h":"500 meters","why":"500 m is coaxial/fiber territory"}],"why":"Copper twisted-pair Ethernet (Cat5e/Cat6) is limited to 100 meters.","tag":"04"},{"q":"Why are the copper wire pairs twisted inside a UTP cable?","opts":[{"h":"To make the cable harder to bend","why":"twisting is electrical, not mechanical"},{"h":"To cancel out crosstalk and electromagnetic interference (EMI)","ok":1,"why":"balanced twisted pairs cancel noise"},{"h":"To increase the cable length to 10 km","why":"UTP is still limited to 100 m"},{"h":"To carry light pulses","why":"fiber carries light, not copper"}],"why":"Twisting pairs reduces crosstalk between pairs and external EMI.","tag":"04"},{"q":"Which medium is <strong>completely immune</strong> to electromagnetic interference (EMI)?","opts":[{"h":"UTP Cat5e","why":"copper picks up EMI"},{"h":"STP Cat6","why":"shielded copper reduces EMI, not 100% immune"},{"h":"Coaxial","why":"copper core can still suffer strong EMI"},{"h":"Fiber-optic","ok":1,"why":"transmits pulses of light instead of electricity"}],"why":"Because fiber uses light in glass/plastic, electrical interference cannot corrupt the signal.","tag":"04"},{"q":"Assuming Auto-MDIX is disabled, which cable connects a <strong>PC to a Switch</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"Straight-through","ok":1,"why":"unlike devices use straight-through cables"},{"h":"Crossover","why":"crossover connects like devices"},{"h":"Coaxial","why":"Ethernet PCs use RJ-45 UTP"},{"h":"Console rollover","why":"console is only for CLI management"}],"why":"Unlike devices (PC–Switch, Switch–Router) use a straight-through cable.","tag":"04"},{"q":"Assuming Auto-MDIX is disabled, which cable connects <strong>two Switches</strong> directly together?","opts":[{"h":"Straight-through","why":"straight-through connects unlike devices"},{"h":"Crossover","ok":1,"why":"like devices (Switch–Switch, PC–PC, Router–Router) use crossover"},{"h":"Rollover","why":"rollover is for console ports"},{"h":"RJ-11 phone cable","why":"RJ-11 is 4/6-pin telephone cable, not Ethernet"}],"why":"Like devices require a crossover cable when Auto-MDIX is off.","tag":"04"},{"q":"Which connector is standard on Cat5e/Cat6 Ethernet cables?","opts":[{"h":"RJ-11","why":"RJ-11 is smaller (telephone)"},{"h":"RJ-45","ok":1,"why":"8-pin modular connector for Ethernet"},{"h":"BNC","why":"BNC is for coaxial cable"},{"h":"LC / SC","why":"LC and SC are fiber-optic connectors"}],"why":"Ethernet UTP cables terminate in 8-pin RJ-45 plugs.","tag":"04"},{"q":"Which fiber-optic type uses laser light and a narrow core for distances of 10–40+ km?","opts":[{"h":"Multi-mode fiber (MMF)","why":"MMF has a wider core and shorter range (up to ~550 m–2 km)"},{"h":"Single-mode fiber (SMF)","ok":1,"why":"SMF uses lasers for long-distance campus/WAN links"},{"h":"Cat6a UTP","why":"Cat6a is copper, 100 m max"},{"h":"RG-6 Coaxial","why":"coaxial is copper"}],"why":"Single-mode fiber (SMF) supports long-haul runs using laser light.","tag":"04"},{"q":"You need to link two campus buildings 350 meters apart outdoors. Which cable should you choose?","opts":[{"h":"Cat5e UTP","why":"350 m exceeds the 100 m UTP limit"},{"h":"Cat6 UTP","why":"still limited to 100 m"},{"h":"Fiber-optic","ok":1,"why":"handles 350 m easily and is safe from outdoor lightning/EMI"},{"h":"Crossover copper","why":"still limited to 100 m"}],"why":"Fiber-optic handles distances well beyond 100 m and avoids ground loops/EMI.","tag":"04"},{"q":"In Cisco Packet Tracer, what does a <strong>green triangle</strong> on a link mean?","opts":[{"h":"The port is shut down","why":"shut down ports show red"},{"h":"Spanning Tree is still checking","why":"Spanning Tree shows amber/orange"},{"h":"The physical and data-link connection is up and active","ok":1,"why":"ready for traffic"},{"h":"The IP address is wrong","why":"link triangles show Layer 1/2 status, not IP validity"}],"why":"Green triangles indicate an active Layer 1/2 link.","tag":"05"},{"q":"Why do newly connected <strong>switch ports</strong> stay <strong>amber (orange)</strong> for ~30 seconds in Packet Tracer?","opts":[{"h":"The cable is broken","why":"a broken link is red"},{"h":"Spanning Tree Protocol (STP) is checking for loops before forwarding","ok":1,"why":"click Fast Forward Time (<code>Alt+D</code>) to skip the wait"},{"h":"The PC has no IP address","why":"STP runs before IP"},{"h":"The switch needs a password","why":"switches work out of the box"}],"why":"Switch ports transition through STP listening/learning (amber) before turning green.","tag":"05"},{"q":"In Packet Tracer, what is the difference between <strong>Realtime</strong> and <strong>Simulation</strong> mode?","opts":[{"h":"Realtime requires internet access","why":"Packet Tracer works completely offline"},{"h":"Realtime runs traffic immediately; Simulation pauses time so you can inspect packets hop by hop","ok":1,"why":"Simulation is best for learning ARP/ICMP/routing"},{"h":"Simulation mode disables all IP addresses","why":"Simulation uses your configured IPs"},{"h":"Realtime mode cannot run <code>ping</code>","why":"<code>ping</code> works in both modes"}],"why":"Simulation mode lets you step through each PDU with Capture/Forward.","tag":"05"},{"q":"Which Packet Tracer cable icon represents <strong>Copper Cross-Over</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"Solid black line","why":"solid black is Copper Straight-Through"},{"h":"Dashed black line","ok":1,"why":"dashed black is Copper Cross-Over"},{"h":"Light-blue curved line","why":"light-blue is Console"},{"h":"Orange solid line","why":"orange is Fiber"}],"why":"Copper Cross-Over is drawn as a dashed black line in Packet Tracer.","tag":"05"},{"q":"Where in Packet Tracer do you configure a PC's static IP address?","opts":[{"h":"Physical tab → Power button","why":"Physical tab shows hardware modules"},{"h":"Desktop tab → IP Configuration","ok":1,"why":"set Static IP, Subnet Mask, and Default Gateway here"},{"h":"Simulation tab → Event List","why":"Event List shows captured PDUs"},{"h":"Switch CLI → <code>ipconfig</code>","why":"<code>ipconfig</code> is a Windows/PC Command Prompt tool"}],"why":"PC IP settings live under Desktop → IP Configuration.","tag":"05"},{"q":"Which button in Packet Tracer speeds up the 30-second switch port startup delay?","opts":[{"h":"Delete (X)","why":"deletes devices/cables"},{"h":"Fast Forward Time (<code>Alt+D</code>)","ok":1,"why":"advances simulation clock by 30 seconds per click"},{"h":"Add Simple PDU","why":"sends a test ping"},{"h":"Inspect (magnifying glass)","why":"views ARP/routing tables"}],"why":"Fast Forward Time advances the virtual clock so amber STP ports turn green immediately.","tag":"05"},{"q":"In a single-switch LAN with <code>PC0</code> (<code>192.168.1.10/24</code>) and <code>PC1</code> (<code>192.168.1.11/24</code>), is a <strong>Default Gateway</strong> required for them to ping each other?","opts":[{"h":"Yes, <code>ping</code> always fails without a gateway","why":"a gateway is only needed to leave the local subnet"},{"h":"No, both PCs are on the same subnet (<code>192.168.1.0/24</code>)","ok":1,"why":"they communicate directly through the switch"},{"h":"Yes, the switch needs a gateway IP","why":"switches forward frames at Layer 2"},{"h":"Only if using Simulation mode","why":"mode does not change routing rules"}],"why":"Same-subnet communication does not use a default gateway.","tag":"06"},{"q":"Which protocol does <code>PC0</code> run first when you type <code>ping 192.168.1.11</code> for the very first time?","opts":[{"h":"DNS","why":"you typed an IP, not a domain name"},{"h":"HTTP","why":"<code>ping</code> uses ICMP, not HTTP"},{"h":"ARP","ok":1,"why":"<code>PC0</code> must discover <code>PC1</code>'s MAC address before building the Ethernet frame"},{"h":"DHCP","why":"IPs were configured statically"}],"why":"ARP resolves an IPv4 address to a MAC address on the local LAN.","tag":"06"},{"q":"Which protocol's Echo Request and Echo Reply messages are sent by <code>ping</code>?","opts":[{"h":"TCP","why":"TCP is for reliable streams, not <code>ping</code>"},{"h":"UDP","why":"<code>ping</code> does not use UDP ports"},{"h":"ICMP","ok":1,"why":"Internet Control Message Protocol carries Echo Request/Reply"},{"h":"ARP","why":"ARP resolves IP to MAC"}],"why":"<code>ping</code> uses ICMP Echo Request and Echo Reply.","tag":"06"},{"q":"<code>PC0</code> is <code>192.168.1.10 / 255.255.255.0</code> and <code>PC1</code> is <code>192.168.2.11 / 255.255.255.0</code> on one switch. Why does <code>ping</code> fail?","opts":[{"h":"<code>255.255.255.0</code> is an invalid mask","why":"it is a valid <code>/24</code> mask"},{"h":"They are in different IP subnets (<code>192.168.1.x</code> vs. <code>192.168.2.x</code>) and have no router","ok":1,"why":"Layer 2 switches do not route between subnets"},{"h":"Switch0 only supports 192.168.0.x","why":"switches are IP-agnostic for forwarding"},{"h":"You need a crossover cable","why":"PC to switch uses straight-through"}],"why":"Different subnets require a Layer 3 router to communicate.","tag":"06"},{"q":"What happens if <code>PC0</code> and <code>PC1</code> are both given <code>192.168.1.10/24</code> on the same LAN?","opts":[{"h":"Packet Tracer balances traffic between them","why":"duplicate IPs break networking"},{"h":"An IP address conflict occurs and <code>ping</code> fails","ok":1,"why":"every host on a network must have a unique IP"},{"h":"The switch automatically renumbers <code>PC1</code>","why":"switches never change host IPs"},{"h":"Both PCs speed up","why":"duplicate IPs cause errors"}],"why":"Duplicate IPv4 addresses on the same subnet cause an address conflict.","tag":"06"},{"q":"In <code>PC0</code>'s Command Prompt, what output confirms a successful <code>ping</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>Request timed out</code>","why":"indicates no reply was received"},{"h":"<code>Destination host unreachable</code>","why":"indicates routing or ARP failure"},{"h":"<code>Reply from 192.168.1.11: bytes=32 time&lt;1ms TTL=128</code>","ok":1,"why":"confirms Echo Reply was received"},{"h":"<code>Unknown host</code>","why":"indicates DNS failed for a hostname"}],"why":"A <code>Reply from ...</code> line with <code>0% loss</code> confirms bidirectional Layer 3 connectivity.","tag":"06"},{"q":"Which OSI layer is Layer 3?","opts":[{"h":"Data Link","why":"Layer 2"},{"h":"Network","ok":1,"why":"Layer 3 (IP addressing and routing)"},{"h":"Transport","why":"Layer 4"},{"h":"Session","why":"Layer 5"}],"why":"OSI from 1 to 7: Physical, Data Link, Network, Transport, Session, Presentation, Application.","tag":"07"},{"q":"What is the PDU called at the <strong>Transport</strong> layer (Layer 4) for TCP?","opts":[{"h":"Bit","why":"Layer 1"},{"h":"Frame","why":"Layer 2"},{"h":"Packet","why":"Layer 3"},{"h":"Segment","ok":1,"why":"Layer 4 (TCP Segment)"}],"why":"Transport = Segment (TCP) / Datagram (UDP); Network = Packet; Data Link = Frame; Physical = Bit.","tag":"07"},{"q":"Which OSI layer is responsible for <strong>encryption (TLS)</strong>, <strong>compression</strong>, and <strong>character encoding (UTF-8)</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"Application (7)","why":"Application provides app protocols like HTTP and SMTP"},{"h":"Presentation (6)","ok":1,"why":"Presentation handles data format, compression, and encryption"},{"h":"Session (5)","why":"Session manages dialog/sessions"},{"h":"Network (3)","why":"Network handles IP routing"}],"why":"Layer 6 (Presentation) formats, compresses, and encrypts data.","tag":"07"},{"q":"As data moves <strong>down</strong> the OSI stack on the sending computer (Layer 7 → Layer 1), what is the process called?","opts":[{"h":"Encapsulation","ok":1,"why":"each layer wraps the data with its own header (and trailer at Layer 2)"},{"h":"Decapsulation","why":"decapsulation happens moving up (1 → 7) on the receiver"},{"h":"Fragmentation","why":"fragmentation splits oversized IP packets"},{"h":"Modulation","why":"modulation encodes bits onto a carrier signal"}],"why":"Encapsulation adds protocol headers down the stack; decapsulation removes them up the stack.","tag":"07"},{"q":"Which OSI layer adds <strong>source and destination MAC addresses</strong> and a <strong>Frame Check Sequence (FCS)</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"Physical (1)","why":"Physical sends raw bits"},{"h":"Data Link (2)","ok":1,"why":"Data Link builds Ethernet/Wi-Fi frames with MACs and FCS"},{"h":"Network (3)","why":"Network adds IP addresses"},{"h":"Transport (4)","why":"Transport adds port numbers"}],"why":"Layer 2 (Data Link) builds Frames with MAC addresses and FCS.","tag":"07"},{"q":"Which OSI layer manages <strong>port numbers</strong> (such as 80 or 443) and breaks large data into smaller chunks?","opts":[{"h":"Network (3)","why":"Network uses IP addresses, not ports"},{"h":"Transport (4)","ok":1,"why":"Transport uses TCP/UDP ports and segmentation"},{"h":"Session (5)","why":"Session manages sessions"},{"h":"Data Link (2)","why":"Data Link uses MAC addresses"}],"why":"Transport (Layer 4) handles segmentation and port numbers.","tag":"07"},{"q":"Which list shows the <strong>correct order</strong> of OSI layers from Layer 1 (bottom) to Layer 7 (top)?","opts":[{"h":"Application, Presentation, Session, Transport, Network, Data Link, Physical","why":"that is 7 → 1 (top to bottom)"},{"h":"Physical, Data Link, Network, Transport, Session, Presentation, Application","ok":1,"why":"correct bottom-up order (1 → 7)"},{"h":"Physical, Network, Data Link, Transport, Session, Presentation, Application","why":"Data Link (2) comes before Network (3)"},{"h":"Data Link, Physical, Network, Transport, Presentation, Session, Application","why":"Physical is 1 and Session is 5 before Presentation 6"}],"why":"From Layer 1 up: Physical → Data Link → Network → Transport → Session → Presentation → Application.","tag":"07"},{"q":"On the receiving host, what happens during <strong>decapsulation</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"More headers are added at every layer","why":"headers are added on the sender (encapsulation)"},{"h":"Each layer reads and strips off its own header as data moves from Layer 1 up to Layer 7","ok":1,"why":"leaving clean application data at Layer 7"},{"h":"All MAC and IP addresses are converted to binary","why":"they are already binary on the wire"},{"h":"The frame is sent back to the switch","why":"the host delivers the data up to the app"}],"why":"Decapsulation strips headers from Layer 1 upward to Layer 7.","tag":"07"},{"q":"Using <strong>even parity</strong>, what parity bit is added to <code>1010110</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>0</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>1010110</code> has four <code>1</code>s (already even), so parity bit = <code>0</code>"},{"h":"<code>1</code>","why":"adding <code>1</code> would make five <code>1</code>s (odd)"},{"h":"<code>11</code>","why":"parity is one bit"},{"h":"<code>00</code>","why":"parity is one bit"}],"why":"Even parity keeps the total count of <code>1</code>s even.","tag":"08"},{"q":"What is the main weakness of a single <strong>parity bit</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"It cannot detect any 1-bit error","why":"it always detects 1-bit errors"},{"h":"It fails to detect an even number of flipped bits (such as a 2-bit error)","ok":1,"why":"two flips keep the parity even/odd unchanged"},{"h":"It doubles the size of every frame","why":"it adds only 1 bit"},{"h":"It requires a router","why":"parity is a simple bit check"}],"why":"Parity catches 1, 3, 5… bit errors, but misses 2, 4, 6… bit errors.","tag":"08"},{"q":"What does an Ethernet NIC or switch do when a frame fails the <strong>CRC / FCS</strong> check?","opts":[{"h":"Automatically repairs the corrupted bit","why":"CRC detects errors; it is not an error-correcting code"},{"h":"Discards (drops) the corrupted frame","ok":1,"why":"higher layers like TCP handle retransmission"},{"h":"Broadcasts the frame to all ports","why":"flooding bad frames wastes bandwidth"},{"h":"Sends an email to the user","why":"Layer 2 drops bad frames silently"}],"why":"Ethernet drops frames that fail the 32-bit FCS check.","tag":"08"},{"q":"Which media access control method is used by <strong>Wi-Fi (IEEE 802.11)</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"CSMA/CD","why":"CSMA/CD is for legacy wired Ethernet hubs"},{"h":"CSMA/CA","ok":1,"why":"Collision Avoidance (listen, random backoff, optional RTS/CTS)"},{"h":"Token Passing","why":"used in legacy Token Ring"},{"h":"Static TDM","why":"used in telephony circuits"}],"why":"Wi-Fi uses CSMA/CA because wireless radios cannot easily detect collisions while transmitting.","tag":"08"},{"q":"Why do modern switched <strong>full-duplex</strong> Ethernet links not experience collisions?","opts":[{"h":"Switches run CSMA/CA","why":"CSMA/CA is for Wi-Fi"},{"h":"Send and receive use separate wire pairs and only one host is on each switch port","ok":1,"why":"no two devices share the same transmit wire"},{"h":"Ethernet frames have no MAC addresses","why":"Ethernet frames always have MACs"},{"h":"Full-duplex disables FCS","why":"FCS is still checked"}],"why":"Full-duplex switched Ethernet gives each port dedicated transmit and receive paths.","tag":"08"},{"q":"How many bits are in a standard Ethernet <strong>MAC address</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"16 bits","why":"port numbers are 16 bits"},{"h":"32 bits","why":"IPv4 addresses are 32 bits"},{"h":"48 bits","ok":1,"why":"written as 6 bytes (12 hex digits), e.g., <code>00:1A:2B:3C:4D:5E</code>"},{"h":"128 bits","why":"IPv6 addresses are 128 bits"}],"why":"Ethernet MAC addresses are 48 bits (6 octets) written in hexadecimal.","tag":"08"},{"q":"In <strong>CSMA/CD</strong> (legacy wired Ethernet), what does a station do right after detecting a collision?","opts":[{"h":"Powers off its NIC","why":"it only pauses briefly"},{"h":"Sends a jam signal, waits a random backoff time, and tries again","ok":1,"why":"random backoff prevents both stations from colliding again immediately"},{"h":"Switches to Wi-Fi","why":"wired NICs retry on the same wire"},{"h":"Doubles its IP address","why":"Layer 2 does not modify IP addresses"}],"why":"CSMA/CD stations send a jam signal and wait a random backoff before retransmitting.","tag":"08"},{"q":"Which part of an Ethernet frame holds the <strong>CRC</strong> value?","opts":[{"h":"Preamble","why":"Preamble synchronizes clocks at the start"},{"h":"Source MAC","why":"holds sender's 48-bit MAC"},{"h":"FCS (Frame Check Sequence) trailer","ok":1,"why":"placed at the end of the frame"},{"h":"Type/Length field","why":"identifies the Layer 3 protocol (e.g., IPv4)"}],"why":"The 4-byte FCS trailer at the end of an Ethernet frame carries the CRC check.","tag":"08"},{"q":"How many layers are in the <strong>TCP/IP model</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"3","why":"TCP/IP has 4 layers"},{"h":"4","ok":1,"why":"Application, Transport, Internet, Network Access (Link)"},{"h":"5","why":"sometimes taught as a hybrid, but standard TCP/IP is 4"},{"h":"7","why":"OSI has 7 layers"}],"why":"The TCP/IP model has 4 layers.","tag":"09"},{"q":"Which TCP/IP layer corresponds to OSI Layers 5, 6, and 7 combined?","opts":[{"h":"Application","ok":1,"why":"TCP/IP Application covers OSI Session, Presentation, and Application"},{"h":"Transport","why":"maps to OSI Layer 4"},{"h":"Internet","why":"maps to OSI Layer 3"},{"h":"Network Access","why":"maps to OSI Layers 1 and 2"}],"why":"TCP/IP's Application layer merges OSI Layers 5, 6, and 7.","tag":"09"},{"q":"What are the three packets in a <strong>TCP three-way handshake</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>HELLO</code>, <code>OK</code>, <code>BYE</code>","why":"not TCP flags"},{"h":"<code>SYN</code>, <code>SYN-ACK</code>, <code>ACK</code>","ok":1,"why":"client sends SYN, server replies SYN-ACK, client confirms ACK"},{"h":"<code>ARP</code>, <code>ICMP</code>, <code>DNS</code>","why":"those are separate protocols"},{"h":"<code>FIN</code>, <code>RST</code>, <code>PSH</code>","why":"FIN/RST close or reset connections"}],"why":"TCP opens a reliable connection with <code>SYN</code> → <code>SYN-ACK</code> → <code>ACK</code>.","tag":"09"},{"q":"Which transport protocol is <strong>connectionless</strong>, has low overhead (8-byte header), and is preferred for live voice/video and DNS?","opts":[{"h":"TCP","why":"TCP is connection-oriented with a 20-byte header and retransmissions"},{"h":"UDP","ok":1,"why":"User Datagram Protocol is fast and connectionless"},{"h":"ICMP","why":"ICMP is a Layer 3 diagnostic protocol"},{"h":"ARP","why":"ARP resolves IP to MAC at Layer 2/3"}],"why":"UDP provides fast, connectionless delivery without handshakes or retransmissions.","tag":"09"},{"q":"Which port number does <strong>SSH</strong> (encrypted remote login) use by default?","opts":[{"h":"21","why":"port 21 is FTP control"},{"h":"22","ok":1,"why":"port 22 is SSH"},{"h":"23","why":"port 23 is unencrypted Telnet"},{"h":"25","why":"port 25 is SMTP"}],"why":"SSH runs on TCP port 22; Telnet runs on TCP port 23.","tag":"09"},{"q":"Which protocol uses <strong>UDP port 53</strong> (and sometimes TCP 53) to translate domain names like <code>igu.edu.tr</code> into IP addresses?","opts":[{"h":"DHCP","why":"DHCP uses ports 67/68"},{"h":"HTTP","why":"HTTP uses port 80"},{"h":"DNS","ok":1,"why":"Domain Name System uses port 53"},{"h":"SMTP","why":"SMTP uses port 25"}],"why":"DNS translates hostnames to IP addresses on port 53.","tag":"09"},{"q":"Which port number does <strong>HTTP</strong> (unencrypted web) use?","opts":[{"h":"25","why":"SMTP"},{"h":"53","why":"DNS"},{"h":"80","ok":1,"why":"HTTP uses TCP port 80 (HTTPS uses 443)"},{"h":"443","why":"HTTPS uses 443"}],"why":"HTTP = port 80; HTTPS = port 443.","tag":"09"},{"q":"Which protocol uses ports <strong>67 and 68</strong> to automatically assign IP addresses, subnet masks, and gateways to clients?","opts":[{"h":"DNS","why":"DNS resolves names (port 53)"},{"h":"DHCP","ok":1,"why":"Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol uses UDP 67 (server) and 68 (client)"},{"h":"FTP","why":"FTP transfers files (20/21)"},{"h":"SSH","why":"SSH is remote shell (22)"}],"why":"DHCP automatically leases IP configuration using UDP ports 67/68.","tag":"09"},{"q":"Why is <strong>SSH</strong> strongly preferred over <strong>Telnet</strong> for managing routers and servers?","opts":[{"h":"Telnet only works on IPv6","why":"Telnet works on IPv4"},{"h":"Telnet sends passwords and commands in plain text, while SSH encrypts all traffic","ok":1,"why":"anyone sniffing the LAN can read Telnet passwords"},{"h":"SSH uses UDP so it is faster","why":"SSH uses TCP port 22"},{"h":"Telnet cannot connect to switches","why":"switches support Telnet, though it is insecure"}],"why":"Always use SSH (port 22) instead of unencrypted Telnet (port 23).","tag":"09"},{"q":"Convert the 8-bit binary number <code>10101010</code> to decimal.","opts":[{"h":"168","why":"<code>10101000</code> is 168"},{"h":"170","ok":1,"why":"<code>128 + 32 + 8 + 2 = 170</code>"},{"h":"172","why":"<code>10101100</code> is 172"},{"h":"192","why":"<code>11000000</code> is 192"}],"why":"Add the active bit weights: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"128 + 32 + 8 + 2 = 170\"></span>.","tag":"10"},{"q":"Convert the decimal number <code>224</code> to 8-bit binary.","opts":[{"h":"<code>11000000</code>","why":"<code>128 + 64 = 192</code>"},{"h":"<code>11100000</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>128 + 64 + 32 = 224</code>"},{"h":"<code>11110000</code>","why":"<code>128 + 64 + 32 + 16 = 240</code>"},{"h":"<code>11111000</code>","why":"<code>248</code>"}],"why":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"128 + 64 + 32 = 224\"></span>, which is <code>11100000</code>.","tag":"10"},{"q":"What is the hexadecimal equivalent of the 8-bit binary number <code>11111111</code> (decimal 255)?","opts":[{"h":"<code>AA</code>","why":"<code>AA</code> is <code>10101010</code> (170)"},{"h":"<code>EE</code>","why":"<code>EE</code> is <code>11101110</code> (238)"},{"h":"<code>FF</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>1111</code> = <code>F</code> (15), so <code>11111111</code> = <code>FF</code>"},{"h":"<code>10</code>","why":"<code>10</code> in hex is 16 in decimal"}],"why":"Each 4-bit nibble <code>1111</code> equals hex <code>F</code>, so <code>255</code> = <code>FF</code>.","tag":"10"},{"q":"Which of these is an <strong>invalid</strong> IPv4 address?","opts":[{"h":"<code>10.0.0.1</code>","why":"valid private Class A IP"},{"h":"<code>172.16.254.1</code>","why":"valid private Class B IP"},{"h":"<code>192.168.1.256</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>256</code> exceeds the maximum 8-bit octet value (<code>255</code>)"},{"h":"<code>127.0.0.1</code>","why":"valid loopback address"}],"why":"Every IPv4 octet must be between <code>0</code> and <code>255</code>.","tag":"10"},{"q":"How many binary bits make up <strong>one hexadecimal digit</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"2 bits","why":"2 bits only give 4 values"},{"h":"4 bits","ok":1,"why":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^4 = 16\"></span> values (<code>0</code>–<code>F</code>)"},{"h":"8 bits","why":"8 bits = 2 hex digits (1 byte)"},{"h":"16 bits","why":"16 bits = 4 hex digits"}],"why":"One hex digit represents 4 binary bits (one nibble).","tag":"10"},{"q":"Convert hexadecimal <code>C0</code> to decimal.","opts":[{"h":"168","why":"<code>A8</code> is 168"},{"h":"192","ok":1,"why":"<code>C</code> = 12, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"12 \\times 16 + 0 = 192\"></span>"},{"h":"200","why":"<code>C8</code> is 200"},{"h":"255","why":"<code>FF</code> is 255"}],"why":"<code>C0</code> in hex = <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"(12 \\times 16) + 0 = 192\"></span>.","tag":"10"},{"q":"How many octets (bytes) are in an IPv4 address?","opts":[{"h":"2","why":"16 bits"},{"h":"4","ok":1,"why":"4 octets × 8 bits = 32 bits"},{"h":"6","why":"MAC addresses have 6 octets (48 bits)"},{"h":"8","why":"IPv6 has 8 hextets (128 bits)"}],"why":"IPv4 has 4 octets totaling 32 bits.","tag":"10"},{"q":"What is the decimal value of <code>00011111</code>?","opts":[{"h":"15","why":"<code>00001111</code> is 15"},{"h":"31","ok":1,"why":"<code>16 + 8 + 4 + 2 + 1 = 31</code> (or <code>32 - 1</code>)"},{"h":"32","why":"<code>00100000</code> is 32"},{"h":"63","why":"<code>00111111</code> is 63"}],"why":"<code>00011111</code> = <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"16+8+4+2+1 = 31\"></span>.","tag":"10"},{"q":"Which IPv4 class has a first octet between <code>192</code> and <code>223</code> and a default mask of <code>/24</code>?","opts":[{"h":"Class A","why":"<code>1–126</code> (<code>/8</code>)"},{"h":"Class B","why":"<code>128–191</code> (<code>/16</code>)"},{"h":"Class C","ok":1,"why":"<code>192–223</code> (<code>/24</code>)"},{"h":"Class D","why":"<code>224–239</code> (multicast)"}],"why":"Class C addresses start with <code>192</code>–<code>223</code>.","tag":"11"},{"q":"Which IPv4 address range is <strong>Class B private</strong> (RFC 1918)?","opts":[{"h":"<code>10.0.0.0 – 10.255.255.255</code>","why":"Class A private"},{"h":"<code>172.16.0.0 – 172.31.255.255</code>","ok":1,"why":"Class B private (<code>172.16.0.0/12</code>)"},{"h":"<code>172.16.0.0 – 172.64.255.255</code>","why":"stops at <code>172.31.255.255</code>, not <code>64</code>"},{"h":"<code>192.168.0.0 – 192.168.255.255</code>","why":"Class C private"}],"why":"Class B private is <code>172.16.0.0</code> to <code>172.31.255.255</code>.","tag":"11"},{"q":"What is <code>127.0.0.1</code> used for?","opts":[{"h":"Default gateway on all Cisco routers","why":"gateways are usually <code>.1</code> of your LAN subnet"},{"h":"Loopback address to test the local host's own TCP/IP stack","ok":1,"why":"<code>ping 127.0.0.1</code> never leaves the PC"},{"h":"Public DNS server","why":"public DNS is e.g. <code>8.8.8.8</code> or <code>1.1.1.1</code>"},{"h":"Broadcast to the entire internet","why":"<code>255.255.255.255</code> is local broadcast"}],"why":"<code>127.0.0.0/8</code> (<code>127.0.0.1</code>) is the IPv4 loopback range.","tag":"11"},{"q":"A laptop shows IP <code>169.254.23.10</code> after plugging in an Ethernet cable. What does this indicate?","opts":[{"h":"The laptop has a valid public IP","why":"<code>169.254.x.x</code> is link-local APIPA"},{"h":"The laptop could not reach a DHCP server and self-assigned an APIPA address","ok":1,"why":"check the DHCP service or cable/VLAN"},{"h":"NAT is blocking DNS","why":"APIPA happens before gateway/DNS"},{"h":"IPv6 is enabled","why":"<code>169.254.x.x</code> is IPv4 APIPA"}],"why":"APIPA (<code>169.254.0.0/16</code>) means DHCP failed to respond.","tag":"11"},{"q":"What problem does <strong>NAT (Network Address Translation)</strong> solve?","opts":[{"h":"It speeds up Wi-Fi channels","why":"NAT is Layer 3/4 address translation"},{"h":"It lets an entire private LAN share one (or a few) public IPv4 addresses on the internet","ok":1,"why":"mitigates IPv4 address exhaustion"},{"h":"It replaces MAC addresses on switches","why":"switches still use MACs"},{"h":"It encrypts all web traffic","why":"TLS encrypts web traffic, not NAT"}],"why":"NAT translates private RFC 1918 addresses to public IPv4 addresses at the router.","tag":"11"},{"q":"How many bits long is an <strong>IPv6</strong> address?","opts":[{"h":"32 bits","why":"IPv4 is 32 bits"},{"h":"48 bits","why":"MAC is 48 bits"},{"h":"64 bits","why":"only the interface ID half of IPv6"},{"h":"128 bits","ok":1,"why":"written as 8 groups of 4 hex digits"}],"why":"IPv6 uses 128-bit addresses.","tag":"11"},{"q":"What is the shortened form of the IPv6 address <code>2001:0db8:0000:0000:0000:0000:0000:0001</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>2001:db8::0::1</code>","why":"<code>::</code> can appear only once"},{"h":"<code>2001:db8::1</code>","ok":1,"why":"drop leading zeros (<code>0db8</code>→<code>db8</code>, <code>0001</code>→<code>1</code>) and compress the single run of zero groups with <code>::</code>"},{"h":"<code>2001:db8:1</code>","why":"missing <code>::</code> leaves the length ambiguous"},{"h":"<code>2001::db8::1</code>","why":"<code>::</code> cannot be used twice"}],"why":"Drop leading zeros in each group and replace one contiguous block of all-zero groups with <code>::</code>.","tag":"11"},{"q":"Which IPv4 address is a <strong>public</strong> (internet-routable) address?","opts":[{"h":"<code>10.45.12.1</code>","why":"Class A private"},{"h":"<code>192.168.50.25</code>","why":"Class C private"},{"h":"<code>172.24.0.1</code>","why":"Class B private (<code>172.16–31</code>)"},{"h":"<code>172.32.0.1</code>","ok":1,"why":"outside <code>172.31.255.255</code>, so it is public"}],"why":"<code>172.32.x.x</code> is outside the <code>172.16.0.0–172.31.255.255</code> private range.","tag":"11"},{"q":"What is the dotted-decimal subnet mask for <code>/26</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>255.255.255.128</code>","why":"<code>/25</code>"},{"h":"<code>255.255.255.192</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>128 + 64 = 192</code> (2 borrowed bits in 4th octet)"},{"h":"<code>255.255.255.224</code>","why":"<code>/27</code>"},{"h":"<code>255.255.255.240</code>","why":"<code>/28</code>"}],"why":"<code>/26</code> borrows 2 bits in the 4th octet: <code>11000000</code> = <code>192</code>.","tag":"12"},{"q":"How many <strong>usable host</strong> addresses are in a <code>/27</code> subnet?","opts":[{"h":"14","why":"<code>/28</code>"},{"h":"30","ok":1,"why":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"32 - 27 = 5\"></span> host bits; <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^5 - 2 = 30\"></span> usable hosts"},{"h":"32","why":"32 is total addresses before subtracting network and broadcast"},{"h":"62","why":"<code>/26</code>"}],"why":"A <code>/27</code> has 5 host bits: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^5 - 2 = 30\"></span> usable hosts.","tag":"12"},{"q":"What is the <strong>broadcast address</strong> for the subnet <code>192.168.5.64/26</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>192.168.5.64</code>","why":"that is the network address"},{"h":"<code>192.168.5.126</code>","why":"that is the last usable host"},{"h":"<code>192.168.5.127</code>","ok":1,"why":"block size is 64; next subnet is <code>.128</code>, so broadcast is <code>.127</code>"},{"h":"<code>192.168.5.255</code>","why":"<code>.255</code> is the broadcast for <code>/24</code>, not <code>/26</code>"}],"why":"Subnet <code>.64/26</code> spans <code>.64</code> to <code>.127</code>; the last address <code>.127</code> is the broadcast address.","tag":"12"},{"q":"Which subnet contains the host <code>192.168.1.200/27</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>192.168.1.128/27</code>","why":"<code>.128–.159</code>"},{"h":"<code>192.168.1.160/27</code>","why":"<code>.160–.191</code>"},{"h":"<code>192.168.1.192/27</code>","ok":1,"why":"block size is 32 (<code>192..223</code>), which contains <code>200</code>"},{"h":"<code>192.168.1.224/27</code>","why":"<code>.224–.255</code>"}],"why":"<code>/27</code> steps by 32: <code>0, 32, 64, 96, 128, 160, 192, 224</code>. <code>200</code> is in <code>.192/27</code>.","tag":"12"},{"q":"Why do we subtract <strong>2</strong> in the formula <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^h - 2\"></span>?","opts":[{"h":"Routers steal 2 addresses automatically","why":"only 1 IP is needed for a gateway, and it counts as a usable host"},{"h":"The first address (Network ID) and last address (Broadcast) cannot be assigned to hosts","ok":1,"why":"both are reserved per subnet"},{"h":"Two addresses are reserved for IPv6","why":"IPv4 subnets don't reserve for IPv6"},{"h":"Windows reserves 2 addresses for DNS","why":"DNS servers use normal host IPs"}],"why":"Network ID (all host bits 0) and Broadcast (all host bits 1) are unusable for hosts.","tag":"12"},{"q":"What is the <strong>first usable host</strong> address in <code>10.10.10.16/28</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>10.10.10.16</code>","why":"<code>.16</code> is the network address"},{"h":"<code>10.10.10.17</code>","ok":1,"why":"one above the network address <code>.16</code>"},{"h":"<code>10.10.10.30</code>","why":"<code>.30</code> is the last usable host"},{"h":"<code>10.10.10.31</code>","why":"<code>.31</code> is the broadcast address"}],"why":"Network <code>.16</code>, first host <code>.17</code>, last host <code>.30</code>, broadcast <code>.31</code>.","tag":"12"},{"q":"Which CIDR prefix corresponds to subnet mask <code>255.255.255.248</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>/27</code>","why":"<code>.224</code>"},{"h":"<code>/28</code>","why":"<code>.240</code>"},{"h":"<code>/29</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>248</code> = <code>11111000</code> (5 bits in 4th octet, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"24 + 5 = 29\"></span>)"},{"h":"<code>/30</code>","why":"<code>.252</code>"}],"why":"<code>248</code> has five <code>1</code> bits, giving <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"24 + 5 = /29\"></span>.","tag":"12"},{"q":"A host has IP <code>192.168.4.127</code> with mask <code>255.255.255.128</code> (<code>/25</code>). Why does the OS reject this IP?","opts":[{"h":"It is a Class A address","why":"<code>192.168.4.x</code> is Class C"},{"h":"<code>.127</code> is the <strong>broadcast address</strong> of the <code>192.168.4.0/25</code> subnet","ok":1,"why":"<code>.0–.127</code> broadcast is <code>.127</code>"},{"h":"It is the loopback address","why":"loopback is <code>127.x.x.x</code> in the first octet"},{"h":"<code>.127</code> is too large for an octet","why":"octets go up to <code>255</code>"}],"why":"In <code>192.168.4.0/25</code>, usable hosts are <code>.1</code>–<code>.126</code>; <code>.127</code> is the broadcast address.","tag":"12"},{"q":"You need a subnet that supports at least <strong>40 hosts</strong> while wasting the fewest addresses. Which mask should you use?","opts":[{"h":"<code>/27</code> (<code>255.255.255.224</code>)","why":"only 30 usable hosts (too small)"},{"h":"<code>/26</code> (<code>255.255.255.192</code>)","ok":1,"why":"62 usable hosts (smallest fit for 40)"},{"h":"<code>/25</code> (<code>255.255.255.128</code>)","why":"126 usable hosts (wastes more)"},{"h":"<code>/24</code> (<code>255.255.255.0</code>)","why":"254 usable hosts"}],"why":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^5 - 2 = 30 &lt; 40\"></span>, while <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^6 - 2 = 62 \\ge 40\"></span>, so <code>/26</code> is the best fit.","tag":"13"},{"q":"When designing <strong>VLSM</strong> subnets for departments needing 100, 45, and 10 hosts, in what order should you allocate them?","opts":[{"h":"Smallest to largest (10, 45, 100)","why":"allocating small blocks first can fragment space and block the 100-host subnet"},{"h":"Largest to smallest (100, 45, 10)","ok":1,"why":"keeps subnets aligned cleanly on power-of-2 boundaries"},{"h":"Alphabetical by room name","why":"size determines alignment, not name"},{"h":"Random order","why":"risks overlapping subnets"}],"why":"Always allocate the largest host requirement first in VLSM.","tag":"13"},{"q":"In a two-LAN Packet Tracer lab (<code>192.168.1.0/24</code> on <code>Gig0/0</code> and <code>192.168.2.0/24</code> on <code>Gig0/1</code>), what should <code>PC0</code> (<code>192.168.1.10</code>) use as its <strong>Default Gateway</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>192.168.2.1</code>","why":"the gateway must be on <code>PC0</code>'s own local subnet"},{"h":"<code>192.168.1.1</code> (the IP of <code>Router0</code> <code>Gig0/0</code>)","ok":1,"why":"hosts send cross-subnet packets to their local router interface"},{"h":"<code>255.255.255.0</code>","why":"that is a subnet mask, not a gateway"},{"h":"<code>127.0.0.1</code>","why":"that is loopback"}],"why":"A host's default gateway must be an IP in the host's own local subnet (the router interface on that LAN).","tag":"13"},{"q":"You cabled <code>Switch0</code> to <code>Router0</code> (<code>Gig0/0</code>) and configured <code>192.168.1.1</code>, but the triangle stays <strong>red</strong>. What did you likely forget?","opts":[{"h":"Setting DNS on the router","why":"DNS doesn't affect Layer 1/2 link light"},{"h":"Enabling the interface (<code>Port Status: On</code> / <code>no shutdown</code>)","ok":1,"why":"Cisco router ports are shut down by default"},{"h":"Using a crossover cable","why":"Switch to Router uses straight-through"},{"h":"Waiting 30 seconds for STP","why":"STP turns amber, not red"}],"why":"Cisco router interfaces start administratively down until <code>no shutdown</code> / Port Status On is set.","tag":"13"},{"q":"Which subnet mask is ideal for a point-to-point link between two routers (needing only 2 usable IPs)?","opts":[{"h":"<code>/28</code>","why":"14 usable IPs"},{"h":"<code>/29</code>","why":"6 usable IPs"},{"h":"<code>/30</code> (<code>255.255.255.252</code>)","ok":1,"why":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^2 - 2 = 2\"></span> usable IPs"},{"h":"<code>/24</code>","why":"254 usable IPs"}],"why":"<code>/30</code> wastes zero usable host addresses on a 2-router link.","tag":"13"},{"q":"Starting at <code>192.168.10.0/24</code>, you assign <code>192.168.10.0/26</code> to Lab A. What is the <strong>network address</strong> of the next available <code>/27</code> subnet for Lab B?","opts":[{"h":"<code>192.168.10.63</code>","why":"<code>.63</code> is Lab A's broadcast address"},{"h":"<code>192.168.10.64</code>","ok":1,"why":"Lab A uses <code>.0</code>–<code>.63</code>, so Lab B starts at <code>.64/27</code>"},{"h":"<code>192.168.10.65</code>","why":"<code>.65</code> is the first usable host of <code>.64/27</code>"},{"h":"<code>192.168.10.128</code>","why":"leaves <code>.64–.127</code> unused"}],"why":"<code>192.168.10.0/26</code> ends at <code>.63</code>, so the next subnet begins at <code>192.168.10.64</code>.","tag":"13"},{"q":"In Packet Tracer, why does the <strong>first</strong> <code>ping</code> across a newly built routed network often say <code>Request timed out</code>, while replies 2, 3, and 4 succeed?","opts":[{"h":"The router reboots after packet 1","why":"routers do not reboot on ping"},{"h":"ARP takes a moment at each hop (PC→Router, Router→Target PC) before the 2-second ICMP timeout expires","ok":1,"why":"subsequent pings use the cached ARP entries"},{"h":"TCP handshake takes 3 packets","why":"<code>ping</code> uses ICMP, not TCP"},{"h":"The switch deletes MAC addresses after every packet","why":"switches cache MACs for minutes"}],"why":"Initial ARP resolution across two hops often causes the very first ICMP echo to time out.","tag":"13"},{"q":"Which command shows your Windows PC's IPv4 address, subnet mask, and default gateway?","opts":[{"h":"<code>nslookup</code>","why":"queries DNS"},{"h":"<code>ipconfig</code>","ok":1,"why":"displays local IP configuration (<code>ipconfig /all</code> adds MAC and DNS)"},{"h":"<code>tracert</code>","why":"traces router hops"},{"h":"<code>arp -a</code>","why":"shows cached IP-to-MAC pairs"}],"why":"<code>ipconfig</code> displays local TCP/IP adapter settings on Windows.","tag":"14"},{"q":"Which command lists every <strong>router hop</strong> between your PC and <code>8.8.8.8</code>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>ping 8.8.8.8</code>","why":"only tests end-to-end reachability"},{"h":"<code>netstat -an</code>","why":"shows local connections and listening ports"},{"h":"<code>tracert 8.8.8.8</code>","ok":1,"why":"prints each router hop along the path"},{"h":"<code>ipconfig /all</code>","why":"shows local adapter config only"}],"why":"<code>tracert</code> (Windows) / <code>traceroute</code> (Linux/macOS) maps each hop.","tag":"14"},{"q":"<code>ping 8.8.8.8</code> succeeds, but <code>ping google.com</code> says <em>\"Ping request could not find host google.com\"</em>. What is broken?","opts":[{"h":"Physical Ethernet cable","why":"<code>8.8.8.8</code> succeeded"},{"h":"Default gateway","why":"traffic reached <code>8.8.8.8</code> on the internet"},{"h":"DNS name resolution","ok":1,"why":"IP works, but domain-to-IP lookup failed"},{"h":"Switch MAC table","why":"Layer 2 is working"}],"why":"Reaching raw IPs while domain names fail indicates a DNS issue.","tag":"14"},{"q":"Which command shows active <strong>TCP/UDP connections</strong> and <strong>listening ports</strong> on your computer?","opts":[{"h":"<code>arp -a</code>","why":"shows IP-to-MAC cache"},{"h":"<code>nslookup</code>","why":"queries DNS"},{"h":"<code>netstat -an</code>","ok":1,"why":"displays open ports and active connections numerically"},{"h":"<code>tracert</code>","why":"traces network hops"}],"why":"<code>netstat -an</code> inspects local sockets, connections, and listening ports.","tag":"14"},{"q":"Which command displays the local <strong>IP-to-MAC</strong> address cache on a PC?","opts":[{"h":"<code>arp -a</code>","ok":1,"why":"shows learned IP-to-MAC mappings on the LAN"},{"h":"<code>ipconfig</code>","why":"shows your own IP/mask/gateway"},{"h":"<code>ping -a</code>","why":"resolves hostname via DNS"},{"h":"<code>netstat -r</code>","why":"shows the routing table"}],"why":"<code>arp -a</code> prints the Address Resolution Protocol cache.","tag":"14"},{"q":"Why might <code>ping</code> to a public website time out even though you can open the site in a browser?","opts":[{"h":"Browsers do not use IP","why":"browsers use IP and TCP"},{"h":"Many servers and firewalls block incoming ICMP Echo Requests for security","ok":1,"why":"HTTPS (port 443) is allowed while ICMP is dropped"},{"h":"<code>ping</code> only works on private LANs","why":"<code>ping</code> works over the internet when allowed"},{"h":"<code>ping</code> requires Telnet","why":"<code>ping</code> uses ICMP"}],"why":"Firewalls often drop ICMP (<code>ping</code>) while permitting HTTPS (<code>443</code>).","tag":"14"},{"q":"Which three channels in the <strong>2.4 GHz</strong> Wi-Fi band do <strong>not</strong> overlap?","opts":[{"h":"1, 2, 3","why":"adjacent channels overlap"},{"h":"3, 6, 9","why":"3 and 6 overlap (need 5 channels spacing)"},{"h":"1, 6, 11","ok":1,"why":"spaced 5 channels apart, zero overlap"},{"h":"1, 5, 10","why":"1 and 5 overlap"}],"why":"Always assign 2.4 GHz APs to channels 1, 6, and 11.","tag":"15"},{"q":"How does <strong>5 GHz</strong> Wi-Fi compare to <strong>2.4 GHz</strong> Wi-Fi?","opts":[{"h":"5 GHz has longer range through concrete walls","why":"2.4 GHz penetrates walls better"},{"h":"5 GHz provides higher throughput and less congestion, but shorter range","ok":1,"why":"higher frequency attenuates faster"},{"h":"5 GHz has fewer channels than 2.4 GHz","why":"5 GHz has many more non-overlapping channels"},{"h":"5 GHz is only for Bluetooth","why":"Bluetooth uses 2.4 GHz"}],"why":"2.4 GHz = better range; 5 GHz = faster speed and cleaner spectrum.","tag":"15"},{"q":"Which IEEE standard corresponds to <strong>Wi-Fi 6</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"802.11b","why":"early 11 Mbps 2.4 GHz"},{"h":"802.11n","why":"Wi-Fi 4"},{"h":"802.11ac","why":"Wi-Fi 5"},{"h":"802.11ax","ok":1,"why":"Wi-Fi 6 (high efficiency in dense environments)"}],"why":"802.11ax is Wi-Fi 6; 802.11ac is Wi-Fi 5; 802.11n is Wi-Fi 4.","tag":"15"},{"q":"Which wireless security protocol is the <strong>strongest modern standard</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"WEP","why":"completely broken"},{"h":"WPA (TKIP)","why":"deprecated"},{"h":"Open with hidden SSID","why":"unencrypted; sniffers still see traffic"},{"h":"WPA3","ok":1,"why":"modern standard protecting against offline dictionary attacks (WPA2-AES is also widely used)"}],"why":"Use WPA3 (or WPA2-AES) and never WEP.","tag":"15"},{"q":"Why is <strong>hiding the SSID</strong> not sufficient security for a Wi-Fi network?","opts":[{"h":"It turns off the Wi-Fi radio","why":"the AP still transmits beacons/probe responses"},{"h":"Anyone with a wireless sniffer can still capture the SSID and read unencrypted frames","ok":1,"why":"real security requires WPA2/WPA3 encryption"},{"h":"It breaks DHCP","why":"DHCP still works"},{"h":"It forces 2.4 GHz only","why":"SSID hiding works on both bands"}],"why":"Hiding an SSID is obscurity, not encryption.","tag":"15"},{"q":"What do the three letters of the <strong>CIA triad</strong> stand for?","opts":[{"h":"Control, Internet, Access","why":"incorrect"},{"h":"Confidentiality, Integrity, Availability","ok":1,"why":"the three core pillars of information security"},{"h":"Cipher, IP, Authentication","why":"incorrect"},{"h":"Cisco, IEEE, ANSI","why":"those are organizations/standards"}],"why":"CIA = Confidentiality, Integrity, Availability.","tag":"16"},{"q":"Ensuring that a message cannot be secretly modified during transit protects which CIA goal?","opts":[{"h":"Confidentiality","why":"Confidentiality prevents unauthorized reading"},{"h":"Integrity","ok":1,"why":"Integrity guarantees data has not been altered"},{"h":"Availability","why":"Availability ensures uptime for authorized users"},{"h":"Scalability","why":"not part of the CIA triad"}],"why":"Integrity ensures data accuracy and detects tampering (via hashes/HMAC).","tag":"16"},{"q":"In a <strong>DDoS (Distributed Denial of Service)</strong> attack, which CIA pillar is directly targeted?","opts":[{"h":"Confidentiality","why":"DDoS does not steal secrets directly"},{"h":"Integrity","why":"DDoS does not modify stored files"},{"h":"Availability","ok":1,"why":"flooding a server makes it unreachable for legitimate users"},{"h":"Encryption","why":"not a CIA pillar"}],"why":"DDoS attacks exhaust bandwidth or server resources to break Availability.","tag":"16"},{"q":"How does a <strong>stateful firewall</strong> improve on a simple stateless packet-filtering firewall?","opts":[{"h":"It replaces all switches","why":"firewalls filter traffic, switches forward LAN frames"},{"h":"It tracks active connection state so return traffic for allowed outgoing requests is permitted automatically","ok":1,"why":"reduces manual rule errors and blocks unsolicited inbound packets"},{"h":"It physically cuts cables","why":"firewalls filter in software/hardware"},{"h":"It assigns MAC addresses","why":"NICs have burned-in MACs"}],"why":"Stateful firewalls track connection tables rather than inspecting every packet in isolation.","tag":"16"},{"q":"In what order does a router evaluate rules in an <strong>ACL (Access Control List)</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"Bottom to top","why":"always top to bottom"},{"h":"Top to bottom, stopping at the first match","ok":1,"why":"place specific rules before broad deny/permit rules"},{"h":"Most permissive rule first","why":"order in the list determines priority"},{"h":"Randomly","why":"deterministic top-down order"}],"why":"ACLs stop on the first matching rule from top to bottom.","tag":"16"},{"q":"What does a <strong>VPN</strong> provide when connecting from a coffee shop Wi-Fi to your university's internal network?","opts":[{"h":"Free faster Wi-Fi bandwidth","why":"VPNs add slight encryption overhead"},{"h":"An encrypted, authenticated tunnel across the untrusted public network","ok":1,"why":"protects traffic from local Wi-Fi sniffers"},{"h":"A new physical MAC address","why":"VPNs operate at Layer 3/4/7"},{"h":"Automatic antivirus removal","why":"VPNs encrypt transit traffic, not disk files"}],"why":"A VPN wraps your traffic in an encrypted tunnel over public networks.","tag":"16"},{"q":"Which organization/project created HTTP, HTML, and URLs in 1989?","opts":[{"h":"ARPANET","why":"ARPANET (1969) built packet switching"},{"h":"CERN (Tim Berners-Lee)","ok":1,"why":"invented the World Wide Web in 1989"},{"h":"IEEE","why":"IEEE standardizes Ethernet and Wi-Fi"},{"h":"Cisco","why":"Cisco builds networking hardware"}],"why":"Tim Berners-Lee proposed the World Wide Web at CERN in 1989.","tag":"01"},{"q":"Which topology uses the <strong>most</strong> cable because every node has a direct link to every other node?","opts":[{"h":"Bus","why":"single shared backbone"},{"h":"Star","why":"one link per host to switch"},{"h":"Ring","why":"one link to each neighbor"},{"h":"Full mesh","ok":1,"why":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"n(n-1)/2\"></span> links"}],"why":"Full mesh maximizes redundancy at the cost of the most cabling.","tag":"02"},{"q":"Which device breaks up <strong>both</strong> collision domains AND broadcast domains?","opts":[{"h":"Hub","why":"one collision domain, one broadcast domain"},{"h":"Layer 2 Switch","why":"separates collision domains per port, but shares one broadcast domain"},{"h":"Router","ok":1,"why":"each router interface is a separate collision and broadcast domain"},{"h":"Repeater","why":"extends Layer 1 signals only"}],"why":"Routers separate broadcast domains (and collision domains).","tag":"03"},{"q":"Which Ethernet cable category supports 1 Gbps up to 100 m and is the minimum standard for modern Gigabit LANs?","opts":[{"h":"Cat3","why":"10 Mbps legacy phone/Ethernet"},{"h":"Cat5e","ok":1,"why":"supports 1000 Mbps (1 Gbps) up to 100 m"},{"h":"RG-59","why":"coaxial cable"},{"h":"RJ-11","why":"telephone connector"}],"why":"Cat5e (and Cat6) support Gigabit Ethernet up to 100 meters.","tag":"04"},{"q":"In Packet Tracer, which tab on a PC lets you open <strong>Command Prompt</strong> to run <code>ping</code> and <code>ipconfig</code>?","opts":[{"h":"Physical","why":"shows power button and NIC modules"},{"h":"Config","why":"shows basic GUI interface fields"},{"h":"Desktop","ok":1,"why":"contains IP Configuration, Command Prompt, Web Browser"},{"h":"Attributes","why":"shows object metadata"}],"why":"Command Prompt and IP Configuration are inside the Desktop tab.","tag":"05"},{"q":"In Guided Lab 1, why did <code>Switch0</code> learn <code>PC0</code>'s MAC address automatically?","opts":[{"h":"You typed it into Switch0's IP Configuration","why":"Layer 2 switches don't need manual MAC entry"},{"h":"Switch0 reads the <strong>Source MAC</strong> of incoming frames and records the port in its MAC table","ok":1,"why":"dynamic MAC learning"},{"h":"ARP asked the router","why":"there was no router in Lab 1"},{"h":"<code>ping</code> programmed the switch","why":"any Ethernet frame triggers source MAC learning"}],"why":"Switches dynamically learn source MAC addresses and their incoming port numbers.","tag":"06"},{"q":"Which OSI layer is responsible for starting, maintaining, and ending a communication dialog (session)?","opts":[{"h":"Presentation (6)","why":"formatting, compression, encryption"},{"h":"Session (5)","ok":1,"why":"dialog control and session management"},{"h":"Transport (4)","why":"ports and segmentation"},{"h":"Network (3)","why":"IP addressing and routing"}],"why":"Layer 5 (Session) manages sessions between applications.","tag":"07"},{"q":"What does <strong>CSMA</strong> stand for in CSMA/CD and CSMA/CA?","opts":[{"h":"Central Switch Media Access","why":"incorrect"},{"h":"Carrier Sense Multiple Access","ok":1,"why":"listen to the medium before transmitting"},{"h":"Cyclic Signal Mode Allocation","why":"incorrect"},{"h":"Cable Speed Modulation Algorithm","why":"incorrect"}],"why":"Carrier Sense Multiple Access means devices listen for an idle channel before sending.","tag":"08"},{"q":"Which port numbers does <strong>FTP</strong> use?","opts":[{"h":"22 and 23","why":"SSH and Telnet"},{"h":"20 and 21","ok":1,"why":"FTP data (20) and control (21)"},{"h":"67 and 68","why":"DHCP"},{"h":"80 and 443","why":"HTTP and HTTPS"}],"why":"FTP uses TCP ports 20 (data) and 21 (control).","tag":"09"},{"q":"Which protocol sends outgoing email between mail servers on TCP port <strong>25</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"DNS","why":"port 53"},{"h":"HTTP","why":"port 80"},{"h":"SMTP","ok":1,"why":"Simple Mail Transfer Protocol (port 25)"},{"h":"DHCP","why":"ports 67/68"}],"why":"SMTP sends outgoing email on port 25.","tag":"09"},{"q":"Convert decimal <code>128</code> to 8-bit binary.","opts":[{"h":"<code>10000000</code>","ok":1,"why":"only the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^7 = 128\"></span> bit is 1"},{"h":"<code>01000000</code>","why":"64"},{"h":"<code>11000000</code>","why":"192"},{"h":"<code>10000001</code>","why":"129"}],"why":"<code>10000000</code> = 128.","tag":"10"},{"q":"Convert hexadecimal <code>0A</code> to decimal.","opts":[{"h":"8","why":"<code>08</code>"},{"h":"10","ok":1,"why":"<code>A</code> = 10 in hexadecimal"},{"h":"11","why":"<code>B</code> = 11"},{"h":"16","why":"<code>10</code> in hex = 16"}],"why":"Hex <code>A</code> equals decimal <code>10</code>.","tag":"10"},{"q":"Which IPv4 class is reserved for <strong>multicast</strong> (<code>224–239</code>)?","opts":[{"h":"Class B","why":"<code>128–191</code>"},{"h":"Class C","why":"<code>192–223</code>"},{"h":"Class D","ok":1,"why":"<code>224–239</code> (multicast)"},{"h":"Class E","why":"<code>240–255</code> (experimental)"}],"why":"Class D (<code>224.0.0.0–239.255.255.255</code>) is used for multicast.","tag":"11"},{"q":"What is the IPv6 <strong>loopback</strong> address?","opts":[{"h":"<code>127.0.0.1</code>","why":"IPv4 loopback"},{"h":"<code>::1</code>","ok":1,"why":"127 zero bits followed by a 1 bit"},{"h":"<code>fe80::1</code>","why":"typical link-local gateway"},{"h":"<code>2001:db8::</code>","why":"documentation prefix"}],"why":"<code>::1</code> is the IPv6 loopback address.","tag":"11"},{"q":"How many <strong>usable hosts</strong> are in a <code>/24</code> (<code>255.255.255.0</code>) subnet?","opts":[{"h":"256","why":"total addresses before subtracting network and broadcast"},{"h":"254","ok":1,"why":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^8 - 2 = 254\"></span>"},{"h":"255","why":"broadcast is also reserved"},{"h":"126","why":"<code>/25</code>"}],"why":"A <code>/24</code> has 8 host bits: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^8 - 2 = 254\"></span> usable hosts.","tag":"12"},{"q":"What is the <strong>block size</strong> in the 4th octet for a <code>/28</code> (<code>255.255.255.240</code>) mask?","opts":[{"h":"8","why":"<code>/29</code>"},{"h":"16","ok":1,"why":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"256 - 240 = 16\"></span>"},{"h":"32","why":"<code>/27</code>"},{"h":"64","why":"<code>/26</code>"}],"why":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"256 - 240 = 16\"></span>, so subnets step by 16 (<code>0, 16, 32, 48...</code>).","tag":"12"},{"q":"You need <strong>100 usable hosts</strong> in one subnet. What is the smallest CIDR prefix that fits?","opts":[{"h":"<code>/26</code>","why":"62 usable hosts (too small)"},{"h":"<code>/25</code>","ok":1,"why":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^7 - 2 = 126\"></span> usable hosts"},{"h":"<code>/24</code>","why":"254 usable hosts (larger than needed)"},{"h":"<code>/27</code>","why":"30 usable hosts"}],"why":"<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^7 - 2 = 126 \\ge 100\"></span>, so <code>/25</code> (<code>255.255.255.128</code>) is the smallest fit.","tag":"13"},{"q":"In <code>192.168.10.32/27</code>, what is the <strong>last usable host</strong> address?","opts":[{"h":"<code>192.168.10.64</code>","why":"next subnet"},{"h":"<code>192.168.10.63</code>","why":"broadcast address of <code>.32/27</code>"},{"h":"<code>192.168.10.62</code>","ok":1,"why":"one below the broadcast <code>.63</code>"},{"h":"<code>192.168.10.33</code>","why":"first usable host"}],"why":"Block size 32 → range <code>.32–.63</code>; usable hosts are <code>.33–.62</code>.","tag":"13"},{"q":"Which command checks if your own PC's TCP/IP stack is functioning without touching any external cable or router?","opts":[{"h":"<code>ping 127.0.0.1</code>","ok":1,"why":"tests the local loopback interface"},{"h":"<code>ping 8.8.8.8</code>","why":"requires working gateway and internet"},{"h":"<code>tracert 1.1.1.1</code>","why":"sends packets across external routers"},{"h":"<code>nslookup google.com</code>","why":"queries an external DNS server"}],"why":"Pinging <code>127.0.0.1</code> tests the local TCP/IP stack internally.","tag":"14"},{"q":"What does <code>nslookup gelisim.edu.tr</code> return?","opts":[{"h":"The MAC address of the web server","why":"MACs are only local on your LAN"},{"h":"The IP address(es) returned by your DNS server for <code>gelisim.edu.tr</code>","ok":1,"why":"verifies DNS resolution"},{"h":"Every router hop to the website","why":"<code>tracert</code> shows hops"},{"h":"All open TCP ports on your PC","why":"<code>netstat</code> shows open ports"}],"why":"<code>nslookup</code> queries DNS to resolve a domain name to its IP address.","tag":"14"},{"q":"On Windows, which command shows your <strong>Default Gateway</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"<code>arp -a</code>","why":"shows IP-to-MAC cache"},{"h":"<code>netstat -an</code>","why":"shows active ports"},{"h":"<code>ipconfig</code>","ok":1,"why":"prints IPv4 Address, Subnet Mask, and Default Gateway"},{"h":"<code>nslookup</code>","why":"queries DNS"}],"why":"<code>ipconfig</code> displays your IP, mask, and default gateway.","tag":"14"},{"q":"In <code>tracert</code>, what does a line showing <code>*  *  *  Request timed out.</code> at one hop usually mean?","opts":[{"h":"Your PC is unplugged","why":"earlier hops already replied"},{"h":"That specific router is configured not to send ICMP Time Exceeded replies","ok":1,"why":"later hops may still reply normally"},{"h":"DNS is down","why":"<code>tracert</code> can still trace IPs"},{"h":"Your MAC address expired","why":"unrelated to <code>tracert</code>"}],"why":"Some routers drop/ignore TTL-expired probes without breaking forwarding.","tag":"14"},{"q":"Which Wi-Fi standard was the first to operate exclusively in <strong>5 GHz</strong> with multi-gigabit speeds (Wi-Fi 5)?","opts":[{"h":"802.11b","why":"2.4 GHz"},{"h":"802.11g","why":"2.4 GHz"},{"h":"802.11n","why":"2.4 &amp; 5 GHz (Wi-Fi 4)"},{"h":"802.11ac","ok":1,"why":"5 GHz only (Wi-Fi 5)"}],"why":"802.11ac (Wi-Fi 5) operates in the 5 GHz band.","tag":"15"},{"q":"What does <strong>SSID</strong> stand for?","opts":[{"h":"Secure Signal ID","why":"incorrect"},{"h":"Service Set Identifier","ok":1,"why":"the broadcast name of a Wi-Fi network"},{"h":"Static Subnet ID","why":"incorrect"},{"h":"Switch Serial ID","why":"incorrect"}],"why":"SSID = Service Set Identifier (your Wi-Fi network name).","tag":"15"},{"q":"Why does <strong>2.4 GHz</strong> Wi-Fi often experience more interference than <strong>5 GHz</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"2.4 GHz has 24 non-overlapping channels","why":"it only has 3 (1, 6, 11)"},{"h":"2.4 GHz has only 3 non-overlapping channels and is shared with Bluetooth, baby monitors, and microwave ovens","ok":1,"why":"crowded spectrum"},{"h":"2.4 GHz cannot use WPA2","why":"WPA2 works on both bands"},{"h":"2.4 GHz travels shorter distances","why":"2.4 GHz travels farther, picking up neighbors' APs too"}],"why":"Longer range + only 3 non-overlapping channels + shared devices cause 2.4 GHz congestion.","tag":"15"},{"q":"Which encryption cipher is required by <strong>WPA2</strong> and <strong>WPA3</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"DES","why":"broken 56-bit cipher"},{"h":"RC4 (WEP)","why":"broken stream cipher"},{"h":"AES","ok":1,"why":"strong block cipher used by WPA2/WPA3"},{"h":"ROT13","why":"trivial letter shift"}],"why":"WPA2 and WPA3 use AES encryption.","tag":"15"},{"q":"An attacker sends a fake email pretending to be your university IT desk to steal your login password. What is this attack called?","opts":[{"h":"DDoS","why":"floods availability"},{"h":"Phishing","ok":1,"why":"social engineering via deceptive email/site"},{"h":"MAC flooding","why":"overflows a switch MAC table"},{"h":"Port scanning","why":"probes open ports"}],"why":"Phishing tricks users into revealing credentials.","tag":"16"},{"q":"An attacker sits between a coffee-shop laptop and the router, silently reading and modifying traffic. What attack is this?","opts":[{"h":"Phishing","why":"deceptive email/web lure"},{"h":"Man-in-the-Middle (MITM)","ok":1,"why":"intercepting and potentially altering traffic between two parties"},{"h":"Brute force","why":"guessing passwords repeatedly"},{"h":"Ransomware","why":"encrypting files on disk"}],"why":"MITM intercepts communications between two endpoints.","tag":"16"},{"q":"Keeping redundant power supplies and daily tested backups mainly protects which part of the <strong>CIA triad</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"Confidentiality","why":"keeping secrets private"},{"h":"Integrity","why":"preventing unauthorized edits"},{"h":"Availability","ok":1,"why":"ensuring systems and data stay usable when needed"},{"h":"Authentication","why":"verifying identity"}],"why":"Backups and redundancy ensure Availability after hardware failure or attack.","tag":"16"},{"q":"What happens when a packet reaches the end of an ACL without matching any explicit rule?","opts":[{"h":"It is forwarded with high priority","why":"never"},{"h":"It hits the <strong>implicit <code>deny any</code></strong> rule and is dropped","ok":1,"why":"always add a <code>permit</code> rule for traffic you want to allow"},{"h":"It is sent to DNS","why":"unrelated"},{"h":"It is encrypted with VPN","why":"unrelated"}],"why":"Standard and extended ACLs end with an invisible implicit deny all.","tag":"16"},{"q":"Which protocol encrypts web browser traffic so local Wi-Fi sniffers cannot read passwords or cookies?","opts":[{"h":"HTTP","why":"plain text"},{"h":"Telnet","why":"plain text"},{"h":"FTP","why":"plain text"},{"h":"HTTPS (HTTP over TLS)","ok":1,"why":"encrypts HTTP traffic end-to-end"}],"why":"HTTPS uses TLS to encrypt web traffic.","tag":"16"},{"q":"What is the safest replacement for <strong>Telnet</strong> when managing a switch or Linux server remotely?","opts":[{"h":"HTTP","why":"web protocol, unencrypted"},{"h":"SSH (port 22)","ok":1,"why":"encrypted command-line session"},{"h":"TFTP","why":"unencrypted file transfer"},{"h":"ICMP","why":"diagnostic ping only"}],"why":"Always use SSH instead of Telnet.","tag":"16"},{"q":"Which Wi-Fi band is shared with Bluetooth and microwave ovens, often causing interference?","opts":[{"h":"2.4 GHz","ok":1,"why":"crowded unlicensed ISM band"},{"h":"5 GHz","why":"much less crowded"},{"h":"6 GHz","why":"Wi-Fi 6E band"},{"h":"60 GHz","why":"short-range directional"}],"why":"2.4 GHz is shared with many consumer devices.","tag":"15"},{"q":"Why should you disable <strong>Telnet</strong> (port 23) on routers and switches?","opts":[{"h":"Telnet uses too much CPU","why":"it is lightweight, just insecure"},{"h":"Telnet transmits usernames, passwords, and commands in unencrypted plain text","ok":1,"why":"use SSH (port 22) instead"},{"h":"Telnet only works over Wi-Fi","why":"it works over any IP link"},{"h":"Telnet blocks HTTPS","why":"unrelated"}],"why":"Telnet has no encryption; SSH replaces it securely.","tag":"16"}],"exercises":[],"challenges":[],"cheat":{"html":"<section class=\"sec sec-1-osi-7-vs-tcp-ip-4-pdus\"><h2 id=\"1-osi-7-vs-tcp-ip-4-pdus\">1. OSI (7) vs. TCP/IP (4) &amp; PDUs</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>OSI #</th><th>OSI Layer</th><th>TCP/IP Layer</th><th>PDU</th><th>Key protocols / devices</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>7</strong></td><td>Application</td><td>Application</td><td>Data</td><td>HTTP, HTTPS, DNS, DHCP, SSH, FTP</td></tr><tr><td><strong>6</strong></td><td>Presentation</td><td>Application</td><td>Data</td><td>TLS encryption, JPEG, UTF-8</td></tr><tr><td><strong>5</strong></td><td>Session</td><td>Application</td><td>Data</td><td>Session management</td></tr><tr><td><strong>4</strong></td><td>Transport</td><td>Transport</td><td><strong>Segment</strong></td><td>TCP (reliable), UDP (fast), ports</td></tr><tr><td><strong>3</strong></td><td>Network</td><td>Internet</td><td><strong>Packet</strong></td><td>IPv4, IPv6, ICMP, <strong>Router</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>2</strong></td><td>Data Link</td><td>Network Access</td><td><strong>Frame</strong></td><td>Ethernet, Wi-Fi, MAC, <strong>Switch</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>1</strong></td><td>Physical</td><td>Network Access</td><td><strong>Bit</strong></td><td>UTP (100 m), Fiber, <strong>Hub</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-2-well-known-ports\"><h2 id=\"2-well-known-ports\">2. Well-known ports</h2>\n<p><code>20/21</code> FTP · <code>22</code> SSH · <code>23</code> Telnet · <code>25</code> SMTP · <code>53</code> DNS · <code>67/68</code> DHCP · <code>80</code> HTTP · <code>443</code> HTTPS</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-3-ipv4-classes-private-ranges-rfc-1918\"><h2 id=\"3-ipv4-classes-private-ranges-rfc-1918\">3. IPv4 classes &amp; private ranges (RFC 1918)</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Class A</strong>: <code>1–126</code> (<code>/8</code>) · Private: <code>10.0.0.0/8</code></li><li><strong>Class B</strong>: <code>128–191</code> (<code>/16</code>) · Private: <code>172.16.0.0 – 172.31.255.255</code> (<code>/12</code>)</li><li><strong>Class C</strong>: <code>192–223</code> (<code>/24</code>) · Private: <code>192.168.0.0/16</code></li><li><strong>Loopback</strong>: <code>127.0.0.1</code> · <strong>APIPA (DHCP fail)</strong>: <code>169.254.0.0/16</code></li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-4-subnetting-quick-reference\"><h2 id=\"4-subnetting-quick-reference\">4. Subnetting quick reference</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Usable hosts</strong> = <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"2^h - 2\"></span> (subtract Network ID + Broadcast)</li><li><strong>Block size</strong> = <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"256 - \\text{interesting mask octet}\"></span></li></ul>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>CIDR</th><th>Mask</th><th>Block</th><th>Usable</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>/24</code></td><td><code>.0</code></td><td>256</td><td>254</td></tr><tr><td><code>/25</code></td><td><code>.128</code></td><td>128</td><td>126</td></tr><tr><td><code>/26</code></td><td><code>.192</code></td><td>64</td><td>62</td></tr><tr><td><code>/27</code></td><td><code>.224</code></td><td>32</td><td>30</td></tr><tr><td><code>/28</code></td><td><code>.240</code></td><td>16</td><td>14</td></tr><tr><td><code>/29</code></td><td><code>.248</code></td><td>8</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td><code>/30</code></td><td><code>.252</code></td><td>4</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-5-cabling-diagnostic-commands\"><h2 id=\"5-cabling-diagnostic-commands\">5. Cabling &amp; diagnostic commands</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Straight-through</strong>: unlike devices (<code>PC ↔ Switch</code>, <code>Switch ↔ Router</code>). <strong>Crossover</strong>: like devices (<code>PC ↔ PC</code>, <code>Switch ↔ Switch</code>).</li><li><code>ipconfig /all</code> (IP, mask, gateway, MAC, DNS) · <code>ping</code> (ICMP test) · <code>tracert</code> (router hops) · <code>nslookup</code> (DNS) · <code>arp -a</code> (IP→MAC) · <code>netstat -an</code> (ports)</li></ul>\n</section>","title":"Computer Networks — Cheat Sheet"},"mocks":{},"extra":{"games":{"osi":{"title":"Order the 7 OSI layers (top = 7, bottom = 1)","prompt":"Drag or move the OSI layers into the correct order from Layer 7 (top) down to Layer 1 (bottom).","items":["7. Application (HTTP, DNS, SMTP)","6. Presentation (Encryption, JPEG, UTF-8)","5. Session (Dialog control & sessions)","4. Transport (TCP/UDP, ports)","3. Network (IP addresses, routing)","2. Data Link (MAC addresses, frames)","1. Physical (Bits, cables, signals)"]},"tcpip":{"title":"Order the 4 TCP/IP layers (top to bottom)","prompt":"Arrange the 4 layers of the TCP/IP model from top (closest to the user app) to bottom (wire/radio).","items":["Application (HTTP, DNS, DHCP, SSH)","Transport (TCP, UDP)","Internet (IPv4, IPv6, ICMP)","Network Access / Link (Ethernet, Wi-Fi)"]},"encap":{"title":"Order data encapsulation on the sender (top to bottom)","prompt":"Put the steps of encapsulation in order as data travels down the sender's stack.","items":["1. Application creates user Data","2. Transport adds TCP/UDP port header → Segment","3. Network adds source & destination IP → Packet","4. Data Link adds MAC header + FCS trailer → Frame","5. Physical transmits raw Bits onto the medium"]},"devlayer":{"title":"Match each network device to its primary OSI layer","prompt":"Match each device on the left with the OSI layer where it operates.","pairs":[["Hub / Repeater","Layer 1 — Physical (repeats raw bits)"],["Switch (Layer 2)","Layer 2 — Data Link (MAC address table)"],["Wireless Access Point","Layer 2 — Data Link (bridges Wi-Fi to LAN)"],["Router","Layer 3 — Network (routes IP packets)"]]},"ports":{"title":"Match protocols to their well-known port numbers","prompt":"Match each application protocol to its default TCP/UDP port.","pairs":[["FTP (Control)","Port 21"],["SSH","Port 22"],["Telnet","Port 23"],["SMTP","Port 25"],["DNS","Port 53"],["DHCP","Ports 67 / 68"],["HTTP","Port 80"],["HTTPS","Port 443"]]},"pdu":{"title":"Match each OSI layer to its PDU","prompt":"Connect the OSI layer to the name of its Protocol Data Unit (PDU).","pairs":[["Application / Presentation / Session","Data"],["Transport (TCP)","Segment"],["Network (IP)","Packet"],["Data Link (Ethernet)","Frame"],["Physical (Cable / Radio)","Bit"]]}}},"essentials":{"html":"<p>A network is computers <strong>sending messages to each other</strong> through cables or radio. The exam is multiple choice, so you need clear ideas and a few facts. Six ideas cover most of it.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout-idea\"><div class=\"callout-h\">Network types and shapes</div><ul><li>By size: <strong>PAN</strong> (your phone + earbuds) &lt; <strong>LAN</strong> (a building) &lt; <strong>MAN</strong> (a city) &lt; <strong>WAN</strong> (countries; the Internet is the biggest WAN).</li><li>By shape (<strong>topology</strong>): <strong>star</strong> — everything connects to one central switch (the most common today); <strong>bus</strong> — one shared cable; <strong>ring</strong> — a circle; <strong>mesh</strong> — many links, very reliable.</li><li><strong>Client–server:</strong> one computer serves many (a website). <strong>Peer-to-peer:</strong> everyone is equal.</li></ul>\n<p><a href=\"#/lesson/net-02\">Go deeper: topologies and types</a></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-idea\"><div class=\"callout-h\">The main devices</div><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Job in one line</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Switch</strong></td><td>connects devices <strong>inside</strong> a LAN and sends each message only to the right one (uses MAC addresses)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Router</strong></td><td>connects <strong>different networks</strong> together, e.g. your home to the Internet (uses IP addresses)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Access point</strong></td><td>adds Wi-Fi to a network</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Firewall</strong></td><td>allows or blocks traffic using rules</td></tr><tr><td><strong>NIC</strong></td><td>the network card inside each computer</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Cables: <strong>UTP twisted pair</strong> with <strong>RJ-45</strong> plugs (most LANs), <strong>fibre</strong> for long or fast links (uses light).</p>\n<p><a href=\"#/lesson/net-03\">Go deeper: devices</a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/net-04\">cables</a></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-idea\"><div class=\"callout-h\">The layer models</div><p>Sending data is split into <strong>layers</strong>; each does one job. The <strong>OSI model</strong> has 7:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Layer</th><th>Think of it as</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>7</td><td>Application</td><td>the app you use</td><td>HTTP, DNS, email</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Presentation</td><td>translating &amp; encrypting</td><td>TLS, JPEG</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Session</td><td>keeping the conversation open</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Transport</td><td>reliable delivery, ports</td><td><strong>TCP, UDP</strong> — <em>segment</em></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Network</td><td>addresses and routes</td><td><strong>IP</strong>, router — <em>packet</em></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Data link</td><td>the local hop</td><td>MAC, switch — <em>frame</em></td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Physical</td><td>bits on the wire</td><td>cables, hub — <em>bits</em></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Memory aid (top → bottom): <strong>A</strong>ll <strong>P</strong>eople <strong>S</strong>eem <strong>T</strong>o <strong>N</strong>eed <strong>D</strong>ata <strong>P</strong>rocessing. <strong>TCP/IP</strong> is the real-world version with 4 layers: Application, Transport, Internet, Network Access.</p>\n<p><a href=\"#/lesson/net-07\">Go deeper: OSI</a> · <a href=\"#/lesson/net-09\">TCP/IP</a></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-idea\"><div class=\"callout-h\">TCP vs. UDP, and ports</div><ul><li><strong>TCP</strong> = careful: connection first, checks every piece arrived, resends lost ones (web, email, files).</li><li><strong>UDP</strong> = fast: no checks (video calls, games, DNS).</li></ul>\n<p>A <strong>port</strong> number says which program gets the data. Learn these: <strong>HTTP 80</strong>, <strong>HTTPS 443</strong>, <strong>DNS 53</strong>, <strong>SSH 22</strong>, <strong>FTP 20/21</strong>, <strong>SMTP 25</strong> (send mail), <strong>DHCP 67/68</strong>, <strong>RDP 3389</strong>.</p>\n<p><a href=\"#/lesson/net-09\">Go deeper: TCP, UDP and ports</a></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-idea\"><div class=\"callout-h\">IP addresses and subnetting</div><p>An <strong>IPv4 address</strong> is 4 numbers 0–255: <code>192.168.1.10</code> (32 bits). The <strong>subnet mask</strong> (or <strong>/prefix</strong>) says which part is the <strong>network</strong> and which part is the <strong>host</strong>.</p>\n<p><code>192.168.1.10/24</code> → mask <code>255.255.255.0</code> → network <code>192.168.1.0</code>, broadcast <code>192.168.1.255</code>, usable hosts <code>.1</code>–<code>.254</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Usable hosts = 2^(host bits) − 2</strong> (minus the network and broadcast addresses). /24 → 8 host bits → 254 hosts. /26 → 6 host bits → 62 hosts.</p>\n<p>Also know: private ranges <strong>10.x.x.x</strong>, <strong>172.16–31.x.x</strong>, <strong>192.168.x.x</strong>; <strong>127.0.0.1</strong> = this computer (loopback); <strong>DHCP</strong> gives addresses automatically; <strong>DNS</strong> turns names into IPs. <strong>IPv6</strong> is 128 bits, written in hexadecimal: <code>2001:db8::1</code>.</p>\n<p>Subnetting is the topic most likely to cost marks — do the drills until they are boring.</p>\n<p><a href=\"#/lesson/net-12\">Go deeper: subnetting</a> · <a href=\"#/tool/subnet-drill\">Unlimited subnetting drills</a></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-idea\"><div class=\"callout-h\">Commands to test a network</div><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Tells you</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>ping 8.8.8.8</code></td><td>can I reach that address?</td></tr><tr><td><code>ipconfig</code> (<code>/all</code>)</td><td>my IP, mask, gateway, DNS (Windows; <code>ip addr</code> on Linux)</td></tr><tr><td><code>tracert google.com</code></td><td>every router on the way</td></tr><tr><td><code>nslookup google.com</code></td><td>which IP a name has (DNS check)</td></tr><tr><td><code>netstat</code></td><td>open connections and ports</td></tr><tr><td><code>arp -a</code></td><td>IP ↔ MAC table</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><a href=\"#/lesson/net-14\">Go deeper: commands</a></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout-remember\"><div class=\"callout-h\">If you remember only this</div><ul><li>Switch = inside a LAN (MAC). Router = between networks (IP).</li><li>OSI 7 layers: All People Seem To Need Data Processing. PDUs: segment → packet → frame → bits.</li><li>TCP reliable, UDP fast. HTTP 80, HTTPS 443, DNS 53, SSH 22.</li><li>Hosts = 2^h − 2. /24 = 254, /25 = 126, /26 = 62, /27 = 30, /28 = 14, /29 = 6, /30 = 2.</li><li><code>ping</code> = reachable? <code>ipconfig</code> = my settings. <code>tracert</code> = the path.</li></ul></div>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"Which device connects two different networks, such as your home LAN and the Internet?","opts":[{"h":"switch","why":"works inside one LAN"},{"h":"router","ok":1,"why":"routes between networks using IP addresses"},{"h":"hub","why":"repeats signals in one LAN"},{"h":"NIC","why":"the card inside a computer"}],"why":"Router = between networks."},{"q":"At which OSI layer do IP addresses and routers work?","opts":[{"h":"Layer 2 (Data link)","why":"that's MAC and switches"},{"h":"Layer 3 (Network)","ok":1,"why":"IP, routing, packets"},{"h":"Layer 4 (Transport)","why":"that's TCP/UDP"},{"h":"Layer 7 (Application)","why":"that's HTTP, DNS"}],"why":"Network layer = IP."},{"q":"Which port does HTTPS use?","opts":[{"h":"80","why":"HTTP (not encrypted)"},{"h":"53","why":"DNS"},{"h":"443","ok":1,"why":"HTTPS"},{"h":"22","why":"SSH"}],"why":"HTTPS = 443."},{"q":"How many usable hosts does a /27 network have?","opts":[{"h":"32","why":"subtract network and broadcast"},{"h":"30","ok":1,"why":"2^5 − 2"},{"h":"62","why":"that's /26"},{"h":"14","why":"that's /28"}],"why":"/27 → 5 host bits → 32 − 2 = 30."},{"q":"Which command shows your computer's IP address and default gateway on Windows?","opts":[{"h":"ping","why":"tests reachability"},{"h":"tracert","why":"shows the path"},{"h":"ipconfig","ok":1,"why":"shows IP settings"},{"h":"nslookup","why":"DNS lookups"}],"why":"<code>ipconfig</code> (add <code>/all</code> for more)."}]],"title":"Computer networks — the essentials","minutes":25,"intro":"devices, layers, ports, IP addresses, subnetting, commands"}});
